c.BC+|4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1756.13, _plural_ ἔντες “Tab.Heracl.” 1.117; _dative_ _plural_ ἔντασσι [prev. work]104; _genitive_ _plural_ παρ-έντων 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 64}}: _imperfect_ ἦν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.77, etc., _Epic dialect_ ἔον}} (also _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 127, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in 1787 “fragment” 3 ii 21), in _Attic dialect_ ἦ}} (uncertain in _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 14.9), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 77, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61b, etc., but usually altered to ἦν in codices (and ἦν}} is required by metre in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 280), _contraction_ from _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἦα (8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.808, +others, “IG” 12(8).449.2 (from Thasos)}}, whence Hom.and later _Ionic dialect_ ἔᾱ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.321, +others, ἔας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187, ἔατε [prev. author] 4.119)}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦεν, always with ν in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; ἔην as _1st pers._ _singular_, only {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.762 (assuming variant, +others ἔον), frequently as _3rd.pers._ _sing._ (generally before a consonant, so that ἔεν is possible), sometimes also ἤην}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσθα, later ἦς (which is variant in {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.26), sometimes in LXX (LXX.Judg.11.35, LXX.Ruth.3.2, +others), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 365e, Erinna Lyrica 4.4, NT.Matt.25.21, +others, ἦσθας 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 156, _Epic dialect_ ἔησθα}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦν, _Epic dialect_ ἔην, ἤην, ἦεν (see. above), _Doric dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ ἦς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 30.1, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 102, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.90, “SIG” 241.145 (Delph.)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἤστην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.10, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 387, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 982, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 272a, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ ἦμες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 21}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ ἦτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 276c, ἦστε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 821, “Ec.” 1086}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἦσαν, _Ionic dialect_ and poetical ἔσαν (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321, 825, ἦν is not _plural_ for ἦσαν}}, but is rather a peculiarity of syntax, see below see, but is _3rd.pers._ _plural_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, +others, “SIG” 560.15 (Epidamnus, 3rd c.BC))}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔον {{NT+5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 644.12; later ἤμην “PSI” 4.362.21 (3rd c.BC), “SIG” 527.46 (Crete, 3rd c.BC), “IGRom.” 4.1740 (from Cyme), always in LXX as LXX.Bar.1.19, cf. NT.Matt.23.30, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.174a, etc., and sometimes in codices of earlier writers, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.34, “Tragica Adespota” 124 (cited from 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 931 by 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus and from [prev. author] “Tr.” 474 by Aps.), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.9, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 26}}, _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσο {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 379 (from Aezani)}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦστο {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 1.455.7 (from Phrygia)}}, _1st pers._ _plural_ ἤμεθα {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.11 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Bar.1.19, LXX.1Ki.25.16, NT.Eph.2.3}}; _subjunctive_ ὦμαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PBaden” 48.12 (2nd c.BC), ἦται “GDI” , ἦνται probably in “IG” 5(1).1390.83 (from Andania)}}; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ also ἔσκον, used by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 656 (Lyric poetry):}}_future_ ἔσομαι, ἔσται, _Epic dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ also ἔσσομαι, ἔσεται, ἔσσετα; _Aeolic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἔσσῃ probably in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 67, 87; _Doric dialect_ 2 and _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἐσσῇ, ἐσσεῖται, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.393, 13.317, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.5, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐσσοῦνται Foed. cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77 codices (but ἔσσονται “Tab.Heracl.” 1.113)}}, _infinitive_ ἐσσεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 57.}} —All forms of the _present_ _indicative_ are enclitic (except _2nd pers._ _singular_ εἶ and _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔασι); but _3rd.pers._ _singular_ is written ἔστι when it begins a sentence or verse, or when it immediately follows οὐκ, καί, εἰ, ὡς, ἀλλά, or τοῦτ᾽, Hdn.Gr.{{Refs|1.553 (also μή _accusative_ to “Etymologicum Magnum” 301.3)}}; later Grammars wrote ἔστι as _substantive_ Verb, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 880.22.}}
**A** as the _substantive_ Verb,
**A.I** of persons, **exist**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὐδ᾽ ἔσσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.437}}; ἔτ᾽ εἰσί they **are** still **in being**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.433, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 445, etc.; τεθνηῶτος.. μηδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐόντος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.289}}; οὐκέτ᾽ ἐστί he **is** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1162}}; οὐδὲ δὴν ἦν he **was** not long-**lived**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.131}}; ὁ οὐκ ὤν, οἱ οὐκ ὄντες, of **those who are** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.45, 44}}; οἱ ὄντες the **living**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.29.2}}; ὁ ὤν the {{LXX+8th c.BC+|“Eternal”, LXX.Exo.3.14, +others, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.289; θεοὶ αἰὲν ἐόντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.290}}; ἐσσόμενοι **posterity**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2.119; κἀγὼ γὰρ ἦ ποτ᾽, ἀλλὰ νῦν οὐκ εἴμ᾽ ἔτι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 284}}; ὡς ἂν εἶεν ἅνθρωποι **might continue in being**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190c; ζώντων καὶ ὄντων Ἀθηναίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.72, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 318b25}}; of things, εἰ ἔστι ἀληθέως [ἡ τράπεζα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.17, etc.}}; of cities, ὄλωλεν, οὐδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ Τροία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1292, compare “Heracl.” 491}}; δοκεῖ μοι Καρχηδόνα μὴ εἶναι **censeo Carthaginem esse delendam**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 27}}; ἂν ᾖ τὸ στράτευμα **be in existence**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.17}}; of money, **to be in hand**, τῶν ὄντων χρημάτων καὶ τῶν προσιόντων {{Refs|“IG” 12.91.25}}; τὰ ὄντα **property**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 511a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 24, etc.}}; τὸ ἐσόμενον ἐκ.. **future revenue** from.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BCH” 46.420 (Olymos, 1st c.BC)}}; of place, τὴν οὖσαν ἐκκλησίαν the **local** church, {{NT|NT.Act.13.1}}; of time, τοῦ ὄντος μηνός in the **current** month, {{Refs|“BGU” 146.4, etc.}}; **in office**, ἱερέων τῶν ὄντων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PPar.” 5.4 (2nd c.BC)}}; αἱ οὖσαι [ἐξουσίαι] the powers **that be, Ep. Rom.**{{Refs|13.1.}}
**A.II** of the **real** world, **be**, opposed to become, γίγνεται πάντα ἃ δή φαμεν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152d, etc.}}; τὸ ὄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Being”, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.35, 5th c.BC: Protagoras Philosophus 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 27d, etc.}}; opposed to τὸ μὴ ὄν, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 3 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, etc.; οὐδὲν γίνεται ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.5U.; ἐξ οὐκ ὄντων ἐποίησεν αὐτὰ ὁ θεός LXX.2Mac.7.28}}; τὰ ὄντα the **world of things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 7, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 129.5, etc.}}; ὄν_indeclinable_, τῶν ὂν εἰδῶν species **of Being**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.2.10.}}
**A.II.2** of circumstances, events, etc., **to happen**, τά τ᾽ ἐόντα, τά τ᾽ ἐσσόμενα, πρό τ᾽ ἐόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.70; ἡ ἐσβολὴ ἔμελλεν ἔσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, etc.}}; τῆς προδοσίας οὔσης since treachery **was there**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.103}}; ἕως ἂν ὁ πόλεμος ᾖ so long as it **last**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.58; αἱ σπονδαὶ ἐνιαυτὸν ἔσονται [prev. author] 4.118}}; τί ἐστι; what **is** it? what's the matter? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 193}}; τί οὖν ἦν τοῦτ; how **came** it **to pass**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58a: repeated with a _relative_ to avoid a positive assertion, ἔστι δ᾽ ὅπῃ νῦν ἔ.}} things **are** as they **are**, i.e. are ill, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 67.}}
**A.III** **be the fact** or **the case**, διπλασίαν ἂν τὴν δύναμιν εἰκάζεσθαι ἤ ἐστιν twice as large as it **really is**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10}}; αὐτὸ ὅ ἐστι καλόν beauty **in its essence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 211c, compare “Phd.” 74b}}; frequently in _participle_, τὸν ἐόντα λόγον λέγειν or φαίνειν the **true** story, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, 116}}; τῷ ἐόντι χρήσασθαι tell the **truth**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 30; τὰ ὄντα ἀπαγγέλλειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.8}}; σκῆψιν οὐκ οὖσαν, λόγον οὐκ ὄντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 584, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1052}}; τῷ ὄντι **in reality, in fact**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328d, etc.}}; to apply a quotation to a case in point, τῷ ὄντι κλαυσίγελως **real** 'smiles through tears' (with allusion to {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.484), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196d}}; κατὰ τὸ ἐόν according to the **fact, right**ly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97}}; πᾶν τὸ ἐόν the whole **truth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.11; τοῦ ἐόντος ἀποτεύξεται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 2.}}
**A.IV** followed by the _relative_, οὐκ ἔστιν ὅς or ὅστις **no one**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὃς.. ἀπαλάλκοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.348; οὐκ ἔ. οὐδεὶς ὅς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 903}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅτῳ, ={οὐδενί}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 293 (_anapaest meter_), compare 989: frequently in _plural_, εἰσὶν οἵ}}, = Latin _sunt qui_, used exactly like{ἔνιοι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 7.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 77d, “Grg.” 503a, etc. (εἰσί τινες οἵ.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24)}}; ἐστὶν ἃ χωρία, πολίσματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.12, 65; ἐστὶν ἃ εἰπεῖν [prev. author] 2.67; ἦσαν οἵ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.14}}; the _singular_ Verb is used even with _masculine_ and _feminine_ _plural_, ἐστὶν οἵ, αἵ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 1, “VC” 4, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.16}}; more frequently in oblique cases, ποταμῶν ἐστὶ ὧν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.187; ἐστὶν ἀφ᾽ ὧν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.65}}; ἐστὶ παρ᾽ οἷς, ἐστὶν ἐν οἷς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.23, 5.25: in questions ὅστις is used, ἔστιν ἥντινα δόξαν.. ἀπεκρίνατ; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 85b: with _relative_ Particles, ἐστὶν ἔνθα}}, = Latin _est ubi_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.15, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅπῃ, ἔσθ᾽ ὅπου, **some**how, **some**where, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 331d, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 517, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 448, etc.}}; in questions expecting a _negative_ answer, ἐ. ὁπόθεν, ὅπω; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 35a, “R.” 493e, etc.; οὐ γάρ ἐσθ᾽ ὅπως 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 61, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.102, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 620}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅπως οὐ **in any case, necessarily**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 188; οὐκ ἔ. ὡς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 76e, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅτε, ἔσθ᾽ ὅτε, **some**times, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 180.2, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 56, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21, etc.}}
**A.V** ἦν is sometimes used with _plural_ _masculine_ and _feminine_, usually at the beginning of a sentence, **there was**, τῆς δ᾽ ἦν τρεῖς κεφαλαί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321; (but in ἦν δ᾽ ἐρῳδιοί τε πολλοί 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, compare 59, +others, it may be taken as _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_)}}; ἦν δ᾽ ἀμφίπλεκτοι κλίμακες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 520 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἦν ἄρα κἀκεῖνοι ταλακάρδιοι epigram cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.184; less frequently ἔστι, ἔστι δὲ μεταξὺ.. ἑπτὰ στάδιοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, compare 7.34; ἔστι.. ἄρχοντές τε καὶ δῆμος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 463a}}; before dual Nouns, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 58, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 500d.}}
**A.VI** ἔστι _impersonal_, with _infinitive_, **it is possible**, ἔστι γὰρ ἀμφοτέροισιν ὀνείδεα μυθήσασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.246; ἔστι μὲν εὕδειν, ἔστι δὲ τερπομένοισιν ἀκούειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.392}}; εἴ τί πού ἐστι (i.e. πιθέσθαι){{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.193; τοιάδε.. ἐστὶν ἀκοῦσαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1055 (_anapaest meter_); ἔστι τεκμήρια ὁρᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1163, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.105, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.272, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 53.6, etc.}}; so in _imperative_, _optative_, and _subjunctive_, ἔστω ἀποφέρεσθαι τῷ βουλομένῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 12.10.7; μυρία ἂν εἴη λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 271e; ὅπως ἂν ᾖ δρᾶν “IG” 2.1054.91: more frequently in _negative_ clauses, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.267, etc.}}; followed by ὥστε with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 656}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ἁδόντα δ᾽ εἴη με τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς ὁμιλεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.96; ἔστιν ἐκπεσεῖν ἀρχῆς Δία 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 757}}: sometimes not _impersonal_ in this sense, θάλασσα δ᾽ οὐκέτ᾽ ἦν ἰδεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pers.” 419.}}
**A.VI.b** ἔστω in argument, **let it be granted**, ἔστω τοῦτο ἀληθὲς εἶναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 25; ἔστω σοι τοῦθ᾽ οὕτως 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.987b; ἔστω εἶναί τινα τοιοῦτον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator Chr.74.24.}}
**B** most frequently, **to be**, the Copula connecting the predicate with the Subject, both being in the same case: hence, **signify, import**, τὸ γὰρ εἴρειν λέγειν ἐστίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 398d}}; especially in the phrase τοῦτ᾽ ἔστι, **hoc est**; Σκαιόλαν, ὅπερ ἐστὶ Λαϊόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 17: with numerals, τὰ δὶς πέντε δέκα ἐστίν}} twice five **are** ten, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.7}}; εἶναί τις or τι, **to be** somebody, something, **be** of some consequence, see at {τι; οὐδὲν εἶναι} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 562d, etc.}}
**B.2** _periphrastic_ with the Participle to represent the finite Verb: with _perfect_ _participle_ once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, τετληότες εἰμέν, for τετλήκαμεν, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.873}}; so in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, ἦν τεθνηκώς, for ἐτεθνήκει, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 869}}; ἔσται δεδορκώς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1179; εἰμὶ γεγώς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1299; πεφυκός ἐστι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1473; δεδρακότες εἰσίν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68; κατακεκονότες ἔσεσθε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.36}}: with _aorist_ _participle_, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, βλήμενος ἦν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.211}}; so προδείσας εἰμί, οὐ σιωπήσας ἔσε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 90, 1146, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 460}}: with _present_ _participle_, ἦν προκείμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 371; φεύγων Ὀρέστης ἐστίν [prev. author] “Ch.” 136; εἴην οὐκ ἂν εὖ φρονῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1330}}; τί δ᾽ ἐστί.. φέρο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 991, compare 274, 708; λέγων ἐστίν τις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1179; ἦν τίς σ᾽ ὑβρίζων [prev. author] “HF” 313; πόρρω ἤδη εἶ πορευόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 204b; βαδίζων εἰμί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 36; frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, ἦσαν ἱέντες 1.57, +others; even εἰσὶ διάφοροι ἐόντες 3.49 (assuming variant):}} — if the _Article_ is joined with the Part., the noun is made emphatic, Κᾶρές εἰσι οἱ καταδέξαντες the persons who showed her **were** Carians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.171; αὐτὸς ἦν ὁ μαρτυρῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 798; δόλος ἦν ὁ φράσας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 197 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**C** εἶναι is frequently modified in sense by the addition of _adverbs_, or the cases of Nouns without or with Preps.:
**C.I** εἶναι with _adverbs_, where the _adverb_ often merely represents a Noun and stands as the predicate, ἅλις δέ οἱ ἦσαν ἄρουραι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.122, etc.}}; ἀκέων, ἀκήν εἶναι, **to be** silent,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.22, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.82; σῖγα πᾶς ἔστω λεώς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 532; διαγνῶναι χαλεπῶς ἦν ἄνδρα ἕκαστον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.424}}; ἀσφαλέως ἡ κομιδὴ ἔσται **will go on** safely, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134}}; ἐγγύς, πόρρω εἶναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e: frequently _impersonal_ with words implying good or ill fortune, Κουρήτεσσι κακῶς ἦν}} **it fared** ill with them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.551; εὖ γὰρ ἔσται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 89, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1188, etc.; ἡδέως ἂν αὐτοῖς εἴη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.30.}}
**C.II** with _genitive_, to express descent or extraction, πατρὸς δ᾽ εἴμ᾽ ἀγαθοῖο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.109; αἵματός εἰς ἀγαθοῖο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.611, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.71, etc.; πόλεως μεγίστης εἶ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.19.}}
**C.II.b** to express the material of which a thing is made, ἡ κρηπίς ἐστι λίθων μεγάλων **consists of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93; τῆς πόλιος ἐούσης δύο φαρσέων [prev. work] 186}}; τοιούτων ἔργων ἐστὶ ἡ τυραννίς **is made up of**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.92}}.ή, etc.
**C.II.c** to express the class to which a person or thing belongs, εἶ γὰρ τῶν φίλων **you are one of** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 345; ἐτύγχανε βουλῆς ὤν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70; ὅσοι ἦσαν τῶν προτέρων στρατιωτῶν [prev. author] 7.44; Κριτίας τῶν τριάκοντα ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.31}}; ἔστι τῶν αἰσχρῶν **it is in the class of** disgraceful things, i. e. **it is** disgraceful, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.2.}}
**C.II.d** to express that a thing belongs to another, Τροίαν Ἀχαιῶν οὖσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 269; τὸ πεδίον ἦν μέν κοτε Χορασμίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117, etc.}}: hence, **to be of the party of**, ἦσαν.. τινὲς μὲν φιλίππου, τινὲς δὲ τοῦ βελτίστου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.56, compare 37.53}}; **to be de pendent upon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 737, etc.}}; **to be at the mercy of**, ἔστι τοῦ λέγοντος, ἢν φόβους λέγῃ {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 917.}}
**C.II.e** to express one's duty, business, custom, nature, and the like, οὔτοι γυναικός ἐστι **'tis** not a woman's **part**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 940; τὸ ἐπιτιμᾶν παντὸς εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.16}}; τὸ δὲ ναυτικὸν τέχνης ἐστίν **is matter of** art, **requires** art, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, compare 83.}}
**C.II.f** in LXX, **to be occupied about**, ἦσαν τοῦ θύειν {{LXX|LXX.2Ch.30.17}}; ἔσεσθαι, with _genitive_, **to be about to**, ἐσόμεθα τοῦ σῶσαί σε {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.10.11.}}
**C.III** with the _dative_, ἐστί μοι I **have**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}
**C.III.2** with two datives., σφίσι τε καὶ Ἀθηναίοισι εἶναι οὐδὲν πρῆγμα that they and the Athenians **have** nothing **to do** one with another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.84; μηδὲν εἶναι σοὶ καὶ φιλίππῳ πρᾶγμα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.283}}; more shortly, σοί τε καὶ τούτοισι πρήγμασι τί ἐστ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33}}; τί τῷ νόμῳ καὶ τῇ βασάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.36}}; τί ἐμοὶ καὶ σο; Latin _quid tecum est mihi?_ {{NT|NT.Mark.5.7, etc.}}; also ἐμοὶ οὐδὲν πρὸς τοὺς τοιούτους (i.e. ἐστίν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.12; ἐν οἷς πρὸς τοὺς ἐναντίους ἐστὶ τῷ δήμῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.278}}; ἔσται αὐτῳ πρὸς τὸν θεόν, in tomb inscriptions, {{Refs|“JHS” 18.113, etc.}}
**C.III.3** with ἄσμενος, βουλόμενος, etc., added, ἐμοὶ δέ κεν ἀσμένῳ εἴη **'twould be** to my delight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.108; οὐκ ἂν σφίσι βουλομένοις εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.35; προσδεχομένῳ [prev. author] 6.46; θέλοντι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1356 (Lyric poetry); ἡδομένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 187c.}}
**C.IV** with Preps., εἶναι ἀπό τινος, ={εἶναί τινος}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(above 11.a), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.9; εἰσὶν ἀπ᾽ ἐναντίων αὗται πραγμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 12d}}; but εἶναι ἀπ᾽ οἴκου **to be** away from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99.}}
**C.IV.2** εἶναι ἔκ τινος **to be sprung** from, εἴμ᾽ ἐκ Παιονίης, Μυρμιδόνων ἔξ εἰμι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.154, 24.397, etc.}}; ἔστιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης **it is** of necessity, i. e. necessary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 256d.}}
**C.IV.3** εἶναι ἐν.. **to be** in a certain state, ἐν εὐπαθείῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22}}; ἐν ἀθυμία, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.46, etc.; ἐν ταραχαῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.218}}; εἶναι ἐν ἀξιώματι **to be** in esteem, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130}}; οἱ ἐν τέλεϊ ἐόντες those in office, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.18, etc.}}; but εἶναι ἐν τέχνῃ, ἐν φιλοσοφία **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 562, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59a.}}
**C.IV.3.b** ἐν σοί ἐστι **it depends** on thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 963; ἐν σοὶ γάρ ἐσμεν [prev. author] “OT” 314; so also ἐπί τινι [prev. author] “Ph.” 1003, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.2, etc.}}
**C.IV.4** εἶναι διά.., much like{εἶναι ἐν}.., εἶναι διὰ φόβου, ={φοβεῖσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34}}; εἶναι δι᾽ ὄχλου, ={ὀχληρὸν εἶναι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.73; εἶναι διὰ μόχθων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.25}}; εἶναι δι᾽ αἰτίας, ={αἰτιᾶσθαι}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.70}}; Geometry texts, **pass** through, διὰ τᾶς ἑτέρας διαμέτρου ἐόντος τοῦ ἐπιπέδου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 20.}}
**C.IV.5** εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτῆς **to be** by oneself, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.23}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ ὀνόματος **to bear** a name, {{Refs|[prev. author] 39.21}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τοῖς πράγμασιν **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.12}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινα **to be** against him, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.33}}; εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑξήκοντα στάδια **to reach** sixty stadia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.11}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τὰς ἁφάς **pass** through the points of contact, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Perg. “Con.” 4.1}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινι, see above 3 b.
**C.IV.6** εἶναι πρός τινος **to be** in one's favour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.10, 29, etc.}}; **to suit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.11, etc.}}; εἶναι πρός τινι **engaged** in, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84c, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.31}}; πρὸς τοῖς ἰδίοις **mind** one's own affairs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309a6, “Ath.” 16.3; εἶναι πρὸς τὸ κωλύειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.26.3}}; πρὸς τὸ πονεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Teles p.46 H.; εἶναι περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.7, etc.}}
**C.IV.7** εἶναι παρά τινι or τινα, = παρειναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 6.2.15, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140.ά}} (assuming variant).
**C.IV.8** εἶναι ὑπό τινα or τινι **to be** subject to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.17 (assuming variant), 6.2.4.}}
**C.IV.9** περὶ τούτων ἐστίν that **is** the question, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 30.}}
**C.IV.10** εἶναι ἀπό.., in Geometry texts, **to be constructed** upon, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.9, “Con.Sph.” 7.}}
**D** ἐστί is very frequently omitted, mostly in the _present_ _indicative_ before certain predicates, as ἀνάγκη, ἄξιον, δυνατόν, εἰκός, ἕτοιμον, οἷόν τε, ῥᾴδιον, χρεών, etc., and after the _neuter_ of Verbals in -τέος, and such forms as θαυμαστὸν ὅσον: less frequently with other persons and moods, εἰμί omitted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 92, “Aj.” 813}}; εἶ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.206}}; ἐσμέν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 634}}; ἐστέ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.463}}; εἰσί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 499 (Lyric poetry), “IG” 2.778 B}}; _subjunctive_ ᾖ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.376, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 659, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.32}}; _optative_ εἴη, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1183.12}}; _imperfect_ ἦν, {{Refs|[prev. work]2.778 B}}; _future_ ἔσονται, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.394.}}
**E** the Inf. frequently seems redundant,
**E.1** in phrases implying power or will to do a thing, ἑκὼν εἶναι (see. ἑκών) κατὰ δύναμιν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.32; εἰς δύναμιν εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 300c}}; τὸ ἐπ᾽ ἐκείνοις εἶναι, **quantum in illis esset**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.9, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.58; τὸ ἐπὶ σφᾶς εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28; τὸ κατὰ τοῦτον εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.9; κατὰ τοῦτο εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317a}}; τὸ τήμερον, τὸ νῦν εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cra.” 396e, “La.” 201c, Theopomp. Comedy texts 98, Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 31.2, etc.}}
**E.2** after Verbs of naming or choosing, σοφιστὴν ὀνομάζουσι τὸν ἄνδρα εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311e; σύμμαχόν μιν εἵλοντο εἶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.134}}; of giving, δῶκε ξεινήϊον εἶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.20.}}
**F** _imperfect_ ἦνissts. used where other languages take the _present_,
**F.1** after ἄρα, to express a fact which **is and has always been** the same, δέρμα δὲ ἀνθρώπου.. ἦν ἄρα σχεδὸν δερμάτων πάντων λαμπρότατον human skin then **it appears is**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64; Κύπρις οὐκ ἄρ᾽ ἦν θεός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 359}}; ὡς ἄρ᾽ ἦσθ᾽ ἐμὸς πατὴρ ὀρθῶς{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 1169; ἦ πολύμοχθον ἄρ᾽ ἦν γένος.. ἁμερίων [prev. author] “IA” 1330; ἦ στωμύλος ἦσθα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.79}}; so also when there is reference to a past thought, τουτὶ τί ἦ; what **is** this? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 157, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 387c: so in the Aristotelian formula τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι (“APo.” 82b38, +others)}}, used to express **the essential nature** of a thing, where τί ἦν (for ἐστί) takes the place of the _dative_ in such phrases as τὸ ἀγαθῷ εἶναι, τὸ μεγέθει εἶναι, {{Refs|“APr.” 67b12, “de An.” 429b10.}}
**G** ἐγώ εἰμι, in {{LXX|LXX, pleonastic for ἐγώ, ἐγώ εἰμι οὐχ ἥμαρτον LXX.Judg.11.27, compare 6.18; also ἔσται πᾶς ἀποκτενεῖ με LXX.Gen.4.14.}}

3803:
** πᾰγίς**, ίδος, ἡ,
(πήγνυμι) ={πάγη}, **trap**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 117, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 458, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.109 (Antip.); παγίδας ἱστάναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 527 (_anapaest meter_), compare 194.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **trap, snare**, of women, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 23, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 689}}; δουρατέα π., of the Trojan horse, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.152 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}; τοῖς ἄρτοις.. ἱστᾶσι παγίδας they try to 'raise the wind', {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 66}}; of women's ornaments, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 666.}}
**II** ἄγκυρα νεῶν π. the anchor **which holds** ships **fast,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 6.5 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus).}}

3804:
** πᾰθ-ημα**, ατος, τό,
_dative_ _plural_ παθημάτοις {{Refs|“Comica Adespota” 283 (Aetol. _accusative_ to 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 279.42, 1761.36)}}:—**that which befalls one, suffering, misfortune**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 142, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.48, etc.}}; τὸ π. τοῦ Χριστοῦ the **passion** of Christ, {{NT|NT.2Cor.1.5}}; of good fortune, χαῖρε παθὼν τὸ π. (deification) {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 32f}}: mostly in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.136, etc.; παθήμαθ᾽ ἅπαθον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 361}}; ἀκούσια π., opposed to ἑκούσια καὶ ἐκ προνοίας ἀδικήματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.27}}; τὰ δέ μοι π. μαθήματα γέγονε my **sufferings** have been my lessons {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (πάθος 1.2), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.207, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 199, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 222b.}}
**II** **emotion** or **condition, affection**, π. τῆς ψυχῆς εἶναι τὴν σωφροσύνην, οὐ μάθημα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.17, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 79d}}; opposed to ποίημα, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Sph.” 248b; τὸ τῆς ἑτέρας χειρὸς π. 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.9.2}}; but in early writers mostly in _plural_, **affections, feelings**, opposed to ποιήματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 437b; τὰ περὶ τὸ σῶμα π. [prev. author] “Phlb.” 33d; ὅσα διὰ τοῦ σώματος π. ἐπὶ τὴν ψυχὴν τείνει [prev. author] “Tht.” 186c; π. ἐν τῇ ψυχῇ γιγνόμενα [prev. author] “R.” 511d}}; παθήμασιν ὑπηρετεῖν obey **the feelings**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1254b24}}; opposed to ἤθη, ἕξεις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1396b33, compare “Po.” 1449b28.}}
**II.2** in Medicine texts, _plural_, **troubles, symptoms**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 2, “Epid.” 2.2.24; π. καὶ νοσήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 439d, compare 389c.}}
**III** in _plural_, **incidents, happenings**, τὰ ἐν.. Ὀδυσσείᾳ π.{{Refs|[prev. work] 393b; πάντα εἴδη καὶ π. πολιτειῶν [prev. author] “Lg.” 681d.}}
**III.2** **incidents** or **changes** of material bodies, τὰ οὐράνια π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ion” 531c, compare “Phd.” 98a; τὰ τῆς σελήνης π. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 982b16, compare “Mete.” 363a24, 365a12.}}
**III.3** in Logic, **incidents, properties**, or **accidents**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 271b, “Prm.” 141d, 157b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 76b13, “Cael.” 310a20}}; τὰ π. τὰ αἰσθητά, of colour, etc., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sens.” 445b4.}}

3805:
** πᾰθ-ητός**, ή, όν,
**one who has suffered**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 457.}}
**II** **subject to suffering,** {{NT|NT.Acts.26.23}}; τὸ θνητὸν καὶ π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 16, compare “Num.” 8.}}
**II.2** **liable to external influence** or **change**, opposed to ἀπαθής, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 392a33; π. καὶ μεριστόν 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.4.8}}; **passive**, opposed to δραστήριος, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.2; νοῦς ἐστι π. καὶ μεριστός 6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedonem commentaria” p.101 N.}}; but ὁ νοῦς ἀπαθής, ἡ δὲ γένεσις π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 414, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.176.}}
**II.3** in Medicine texts, **diseased, affected**, στεφάνη {{Refs|“PMed.Strassb.” p.8.}}

3806:
** πάθος** [ᾰ], εος, τό,
(πάσχω) **that which happens** to a person or thing, τὰ ἐν τοῖς κατόπτροις τῆς ὄψεως π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 193c}}; τὰ ἐν τῷ ἀνθρωπίνῳ βίῳ [τῆς ψυχῆς] π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 612a}}; **incident, accident**, τὰ ἀνθρωπήϊα π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.4}}; τὸ συντυχὸν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 313}}; οὗ τόδ᾽ ἦν π. where this **incident** took place, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 732}}; ἔξωθεν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 381a}}; **unfortunate accident**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.10.}}
**2** **what one has experienced**, good or bad, **experience**, τὸν πάθει μάθος θέντα κυρίως ἔχειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 177 (Lyric poetry)}}; τά γ᾽ ἐμὰ π. my **experiences**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 96a; τὸ δρᾶμα τοῦ πάθους πλέον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 533}}; opposed to ἔργα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 245c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 298a28}}; opposed to πρᾶξις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 876d; ἤθη καὶ π. καὶ πράξεις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1447a28.}}
**2.b** in bad sense, **misfortune, calamity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 703, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.91, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.10, etc.; οὐλίῳ σὺν πάθει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 932 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὰ τῆς Νιόβης π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 380a, etc.}}; ἀνήκεστον π. ἔρδειν to do **an act which is** an irreparable **mischief** to one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.137}}; μετὰ τῆς θυγατρὸς τὸ π., i.e. her **death**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.133}}; π. μέγα πεπονθέναι, of a great **defeat**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.147, compare 5.87, +others}}
**2.c** ={πάθημα}{{Refs 4th c.BC+|11.2, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 738a16, 750a30, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.91.}}
**II** of the soul, **emotion, passion** (λέγω δὲ πάθη.. ὅλως οἷς ἕπεται ἡδονὴ ἢ λύπη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1105b21), σοφίη ψυχὴν παθῶν ἀφαιρεῖται 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 31; διὰ πάθους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.84}}; ἐρωτικὸν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 265b}}; π. ποιεῖν to excite **passion**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1418a12; ἐν π. εἶναι [prev. author] “Pol.” 1287b3}}; ἐκτὸς τοῦ π. εἶναι to be exempt from **passion**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Teles Philosophus p.56 H.; ἔξω τῶν π. γίγνεσθαι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 60.3}}; περὶ παθῶν, title of work by Zeno the Stoic, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.4; in 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus}}, **sensation** (including pleasure and pain), ἀκουστικὸν π. {{Refs|“Ep.” 1p.13U., compare p.19 U. (_plural_); ὡς κανόνι τῷ π. πᾶν ἀγαθὸν κρίνοντες [prev. work] 3p.63U.}}
**III** **state, condition**, τὸ τῆς παντοδαπῆς ἀγνοίας π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 228e, compare 243c, “Plt.” 277d, “Ap.” 22c}}; opposed to ἐνέργεια, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 12.17}}; opposed to ποίημα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 248d.}}
**III.2** **incidents** of things, **changes** or **happenings** occurring in them, τὰ οὐράνια π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 285c}}; τὰ περὶ τὸν οὐρανὸν π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phd.” 96c; τὰ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ π. καὶ μέρη 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 986a5; π. τοῦτο, ὃ καλεῖν εἰώθαμεν σεισμόν [prev. author] “Mu.” 395b36.}}
**III.3** **properties, qualities** of things, opposed to οὐσία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 11a}}; π. λέγεται.. ποιότης καθ᾽ ἣν ἀλλοιοῦσθαι ἐνδέχεται, οἷον τὸ λευκὸν καὶ τὸ μέλαν, καὶ γλυκὺ καὶ πικρόν, καὶ βαρύτης καὶ κουφότης, κτλ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1022b15; τῶν ἀριθμῶν π. [prev. work] 985b29}}; ἀριθμοῖς καὶ γραμμαῖς καὶ τοῖς τούτων π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 23; γεωμετρία περὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότα πάθη τοῖς μεγέθεσι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1355b31, compare “APo.” 75b1}}; τῶν φυτῶν τὰ μέρη καὶ τὰ π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.1.1; αἱ δυνάμεις καὶ τὰ π. [prev. work] 8.4.2.}}
**IV** Grammars, **modification in form** of words (especially dialectal), πάθη τῆς λέξεως {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1460b12, compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 38.24, +others}}
**IV.2** in Syntax, **modified construction**, of omission or redundancy, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Synt.” 6.15, 267.8.}}
**IV.2.b** **passivity**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 637.30, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 12.17, +others}}
**IV.2.c** in writing, signs other than accents and breathings (ἀπόστροφος, ὑφέν, ὑποδιαστολή), {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus “Supp.” 1p.107U.}}
**V** Rhet., **emotional style** or **treatment**, τὸ σφοδρὸν καὶ ἐνθουσιαστικὸν π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 8.1; πάθος ποιεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rh.” 1418a12; πράγματα π. ἔχοντα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.711e, etc.: _plural_, πάθη διεστῶτα ὕψους 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 8.2.}}

3808:
** παιδᾰρ-ιον**, τό,
_diminutive_ of παῖς, **little boy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 494, “Pl.” 536, etc.}}; ἐκ παιδαρίου from **a child**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 207d; ἐκ μικροῦ π. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.19}}; π. εἶ you're **a mere boy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 821}}; also, **little girl**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 1203, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 164, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 428 (in this sense only _Attic dialect_ _accusative_ to Moer. p.321 P.)}}: in _plural_, **young children**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 568; π. καὶ γύναια 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.130, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.305.}}
**II** **young slave**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 823, 843, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.21, “PPetr.” 2p.128 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}

3809:
** παιδεία**, ἡ,
**rearing of a child**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 18.}}
**2** **training and teaching, education**, opposed to τροφή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 961, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.39 (_plural_); π. καὶ τροφή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107d, “Phlb.” 55d.}}
**3** its result, **mental culture, learning, education**, ἡ π. εὐτυχοῦσι κόσμος, ἀτυχοῦσι καταφύγιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 180, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 327d, “Grg.” 470e, “R.” 376e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1338a30, etc.; τῆς Λακεδαιμονίων π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 343a}}: in _plural_, **parts** or **systems of education**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 653c, 804d.}}
**4** **culture** of trees, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.7.4.}}
**5** πλεκτὰν Αἰγύπτου παιδείαν ἐξηρτήσασθε the twisted **handiwork** of Egypt, i.e. (_accusative_ to Scholia) **ropes of papyrus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 129 (Lyric poetry).}}
**6** **anything taught** or **learned, art, science**, π. ἱερή, of medicine, {{Refs|“IG” 14.2104.}}
**7** **chastisement**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Pro.22.15, NT.Heb.12.5.}}
**II** **youth, childhood**, παιδείης πολυήρατον ἄνθος {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1305, compare 1348; ἐκ παιδείας φίλος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.11; so (probably) στερρὰν παιδείαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 206 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** in _collective_ sense, **body of youths**, παιδείας λιπαρὴς ὄχλος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 6.}}

3810:
** παιδ-ευτής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**teacher, instructor**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 493c, +others, “IG” 22.1011.35.}}
**2** **minister of education**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 811d, +others}}
**II** **corrector, chastiser,** {{NT|NT.Heb.12.9.}}

3811:
** παιδ-εύω**,
_future_ -σω: _aorist_ ἐπαίδευσα: _perfect_ πεπαίδευκα:—_middle_, _future_ παιδεύσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1068}}: _aorist_ ἐπαιδευσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 546b:}} —_passive_, _future_ παιδευθήσομαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 376c}}; παιδεύσομαι (in _passive_ sense) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cri.” 54a}}: _aorist_ ἐπαιδεύθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 562, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 236a, etc.}}: _perfect_ πεπαίδευμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 920a, etc.}}: (παῖς):—**bring up** or **rear a child**, λευκὸν αὐτὴν.. ἐπαίδευσεν γάλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 648}}:—_passive_, ἐπαιδεύθην ξένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 562; Ἅιδου δ᾽ ἐν δόμοις παιδεύεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 953: but mostly}}
**II** opposed to τρέφω or ἐκτρέφω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 54a, +others)}}, **train and teach, educate**, παῖδας, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 451, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 917; τοὺς νέους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 24e, etc.; κάκιστον ἡ εὐπετείη παιδεῦσαι τὴν νεότητα 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 178}}; οἱ πεπαιδευμένοι **educated, cultured** persons, opposed to ἀυαθεῖς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 185; τὴν Ἑλλάδα πεπαίδευκεν.. ὁ ποιητής 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 606e}}; also, of animals, **train**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 10.6 (_passive_), see below:}} —Constr.: π. τινά τινι **educate in** or **by**.., παιδείᾳ πεπαιδευμένους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 741a; μουσικῇ καὶ γυμναστικῇ π. τινάς [prev. author] “R.” 430a}}; ἔθεσι τοὺς φύλακας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 522a; π. τινὰ ἐν τοῖς ἔργοις 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.3, etc.}}; ἐν ἤθεσι, ἐν ἀρετῇ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.82, 12.138; ἐν μουσικῇ καὶ γυμναστικῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 50e}}; π. τινὰ εἰς ἀρετήν, εἰς τέχνην τινά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Grg.” 519e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.17 (_passive_)}}; πεπαιδευμένον πρὸς ἀρετήν, πρὸς τὸ μετρίων δεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 492e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.1 (_passive_)}}; πρὸς τὴν πολιτείαν βλέποντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1260b15; ἐπ᾽ ἀρετήν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 13.3 (_passive_)}}; περὶ βύρσας {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ap.” 29, etc.: with double _accusative_, π. τινά τι}} **teach** one a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 414d; ἀείμνηστον παιδείαν αὐτοὺς ἐπαίδευσε 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.148}}: with _accusative_ _of things_ only, **teach** a thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1337b23}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, π. τινὰ κιθαρίζειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.155}}: with predicative _adjective_ or _substantive_, π. τινὰ κακόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 919; γυναῖκας σώφρονας π. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 601}}:—in _passive_, with _accusative_ _of things_, **to be taught** a thing, παιδεύεσθαι τέχνην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 695a, +others; ἀκούσματα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Kith.fragment” 5: with _accusative_ _cognate_ (attracted), ἀπὸ παιδεύσιος τῆς ἐπεπαίδευτο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.78}}: with _infinitive_, π. ἄρχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.3; ὄρνιθες ἐπεπαίδευντό σοι.. ὥστε ὑπηρετεῖν [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.6.39 (in later Gr., of things, ἡ ὕλη παιδεύεται φέρεσθαι.. Palladius Medicus “in Hippocratem” 2.106 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator)}}; ἐν τοῖς ἀναγκαιοτάτοις π. **to be educated** only in what is indispensable, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.84: especially in}}_perfect_ _participle_ _passive_ πεπαιδευμένος, **educated, trained, expert**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.17}}; opposed to ἀπαίδευτος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 654d; ἱκανῶς π. [prev. work]b; φαυλοτέρως π. δικασταί [prev. work] 876d}}; opposed to δημιουργός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Amat.” 135d; ἰατρὸς ὅ τε δημιουργὸς καὶ ὁ ἀρχιτεκτονικός, καὶ τρίτος ὁ π. περὶ τὴν τέχνην 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1282a4}}; π. also, **well-bred**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1128a21}}:—_middle_, **to have** any one **taught, cause** him **to be educated**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1068}}; οὓς ἡγεμόνας πόλεως ἐπαιδεύσασθε **educated** as leaders, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 546b: with _accusative_ _cognate_, πολλὰ ἃ ἐκεῖνος αὐτὸν ἐπαιδεύσατο [prev. author] “Men.” 93d:}} —also in _active_ in this sense, ἐν Ἀρίφρονος ἐπαίδευε **had** him **educated** in the house of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Ariphron Lyricus, [prev. author] “Prt.” 320a, compare “Cri.” 50e: with _accusative_ _cognate_, [prev. author] “Men.” 93e}}; of animals, **cause to be trained**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Nausicrates Comicus 2.8 (whereas}}_middle_ is sometimes used like _active_, τροφαὶ αἱ παιδευόμεναι **educating** nurture, i.e. education, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 561 (Lyric poetry)).}}
**II.2** _absolutely_, **give instruction, teach**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.226.}}
**III** **correct, discipline**, τοὐμὸν ἦθος π. νοεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 595; διαίτῃ τὴν ψυχὴν ἐπαίδευσε καὶ τὸ σῶμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.5}}; ὕβρις πεπαιδευμένη **chastened** (i.e. **well-bred**) insolence, Aristotle's definition of εὐτραπελία, {{Refs|“Rh.” 1389b11.}}
**III.2** **chastise, punish**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Hos.7.12, NT.Luke.23.16, +others}}

3813:
** παιδί-ον**
(_paroxytone_), τό, _diminutive_ of παῖς (never in Trag.), **little** or **young child** (up to 7 years, _accusative_ to {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus cited in 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.26), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.110, 2.119, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 50; τὰ νεωστὶ γεγονότα π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 212e}}; ἐκ παιδίου from **a child**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 412, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.20: proverbial, τοῦ πατρὸς τὸ π.}} 'chip of the old block', {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Comica Adespota” 672, title of satire by Varro; so τῆς μητρὸς τὸ π. 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.3.15 (with play on Μήτηρ).}}
**II** **young slave**, male or female, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.329.27, 22.1554.67, 1556.22, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 37, “Nu.” 132, “Av.” 1150 (assuming variant).}}
**III** τὸ παιδίον, a disease of children, probably **convulsions**, uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.”}}
**III.3** **παίδι-ος**, ὁ, barbarism for {παιδιόθεν}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 27.}}

3814:
** παιδίσκ-η**, ἡ,
_diminutive_ of παῖς (ἡ), **young girl, maiden**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.11, 4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 22.26, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 102, etc.}}; π. νέα, of a wife, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 41.}}
**II** **young female slave, bondmaid**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.12, 13.67, “PCair.Zen.” 142 (3rd c.BC), NT.Gal.4.22}}: generally, **maidservant, **{{NT|NT.Mark.14.66}}; τοὺς παῖδας καὶ τὰς π. {{NT|NT.Luke.12.45.}}
**II.2** **prostitute**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.19, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 24, “Cat. Ma.” 24, etc.; αἱ δημόσιαι π. 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.437e.}}

3815:
** παίζω**,
_Doric dialect_ **παίσδω** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.42}}: _Laconian dialect_ _present_ _participle_ _genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ **παιδδωἇν** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1313 (Lyric poetry):}}_future_ παιξοῦμαι Syrac. in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 9.2}}, παίξομαι {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.2Ki.6.21, “Anthologia Graeca” 12.46 (3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus), παίξω [prev. work] 211 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus), “Anacreontea” 41.8}}: _aorist 1_ ἔπαισα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1055, etc.}}: _perfect_ πέπαικα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 923.3}}: _perfect_ _passive_ πέπαισμαι [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.77 (variant{πέπλασται}), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1227; _imperative_ πεπαίσθω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 278d}}]: also _aorist_ ἔπαιξα {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 23, 5th-6th c.BC: Ctesias Historicus “fragment” 29.59, LXX.Judg.16.25, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 6.4, etc.}}: _perfect_ πέπαιχα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 9}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐπαίχθην {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. author] 2.123f, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 8.6}}: _perfect_ πέπαιγμαι {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 979.3 (from Philae)}}; _infinitive_ πεπαῖχθαι Timarch. cited in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.501f; _imperative_ πεπαίχθω 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.106 K., Fronto “Epig.Gr.” 5.86: 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} uses only _present_ and _imperfect_, and (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.251)}}_aorist_ _imperative_ παίσατ; Trag. only _present_: (παῖς):—properly, **play like a child, sport**, τῇ δέ θ᾽ ἅμα Νύμφαι.. ἀγρονόμοι παίζουσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.106, compare 7.291 (never in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.114, etc.: _metaphorically_, αἰὼν παῖς ἐστὶ παίζων 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 52.}}
**2** especially **dance**, παίσατε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.251; δῶμα περιστεναχίζετο ποσσὶν ἀνδρῶν παιζόντων 23.147, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 277; π. τε καὶ χορεύειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 409, compare 390; ἐνόπλια χαλκωθεὶς ἔπαιζεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.86:}} —_passive_, ἀλλὰ πέπαισται μετρίως ἡμῖν, of the chorus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1227.}}
**3** **play** [a game], σφαίρῃ π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.100; κλεψύδρῃ 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 100.9}}; κύβοις ἐπὶ συνθήκαις π. Ctes.[same place]; ἀντ᾽ ἀστραγάλων κονδύλοισι π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 43, compare 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 92}}; π. διὰ γραμμῆς (see. γραμμή{{Refs 5th c.BC+|III. 2); π. πρὸς κότταβον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 46.1}}; μετά τινων with others, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.114: with _accusative_ _cognate_, κότταβον ἀγκύλῃ π. 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 53 (uncertain); σφαῖραν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 73; π. παιδιὰν πρός τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1055, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.110b}}; κύνα καὶ πόλιν π., of a game similar to our draughts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 56: with _adverbs_, φαινίνδα π. 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 283, compare 5th c.BC: Crates Comicus [same place], etc.}}
**4** **play** on a musical instrument, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 206: with _accusative_, Πὰν ὁ καλαμόφθογγα παίζων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 230}}; **dance and sing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.16.}}
**5** **play amorously**, πρὸς ἀλλήλους {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 9.2; μετά τινος LXX.Gen.26.8}}; of mares, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 572a30.}}
**6** **hunt, pursue game**, π. κατ᾽ ἄλσος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 567.}}
**II** **jest, sport**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.28, 5.4, 9.11}}; opposed to σπουδάζω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.1.1}}; opposed to σπουδῇ λέγω, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.3.47}}; παίζετε ταῦτα λέγοντες (opposed to σπουδάζετε) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 283b; π. καὶ χλευάζειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 376; π. καὶ γελᾶν 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 218.4; πῖνε, παῖζε 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 8}}; π. πρός τινα **make fun** with a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 952, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 79a, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 198}}; π. εἴς τι **play** with a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89b}}: with _adjective_ _neuter_, τοιαῦτα ἔπαιζον σπουδῇ πρὸς ἀλλήλους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.6:}}_participle_ παίζων is frequently _absolutely_, **jestingly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 145b, +others}}; opposed to σπουδάζων, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 636c, +others:}} —_passive_, ὁ λόγος πέπαισται **has been made up as a jest** (variant for{πέπλασται}), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.77}}; ταῦτα πεπαίσθω ὑμῖν enough of **jest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 278d, compare “Phdr.” 278b, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus [same place]}}; πεπαῖχθαι τὴν λέξιν Timarch. [same place]; τοῦτο τὸ παιζόμενον 'as the joke is', {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1090f; τὸ Μενεδήμῳ πεπαιγμένον [prev. work]81e; but οἷα πέπαιγμαι, in _active_ sense, “epigram Gr.” 979.3 (from Philae).}}
**II.2** with _accusative_, **play with, make sport of**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 20, “Anthologia Graeca” 10.64 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.3** Grammars, of words **played upon** or **coined for the joke's sake**, οἱ κωμῳδοὶ παίζειν εἰώθασι τὰ τοιαῦτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 42, compare 68, etc.}}

3816:
** παῖς**,
also **παῦς** (which see), παιδός, ὁ, ἡ, _genitive_ _plural_ παίδων, _Doric dialect_ παιδῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Greg.Cor. p.317 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; _dative_ _plural_ παισί, _Epic dialect_ παίδεσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.381, etc.}}; in early _Epic dialect_ frequently _disyllable_ in _nominative_ **πάϊς**, e. g. when forming part of two different feet, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.609, 5.704, etc.}}; probably also in the fifth foot, {{Refs|9.57, 11.389}}; and before bucolic diaeresis, {{Refs|2.205, +others}}; also in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 38, 85}}; and in _Boeotian dialect_, {{Refs|“IG” 7.690, +others (from Tanagra)}}, compare πῆ; πάϊ [ᾰῑ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.192}} (παιδ- is never _disyllable_ in oblique cases in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus); _accusative_ πάϊν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus R.4.697, “Anthologia Graeca” 3.8 (Inscription Cyzic.), 9.125}}; _genitive_ παϊδός epigram cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Symp.” 41}}; _dative_ παϊδί probably in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 17:}}
**I** in relation to Descent, **child**, whether **son**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.205, 609, +others (with special reference to the father, opposed to τέκνον, which see): _plural_, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.4, etc.}}; or **daughter**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.20, 443, 3.175; παῖδες ἄρρενες καὶ θήλειαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 788a}}; παῖς, opposed to κόρα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.7 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC)}}; of an **adopted son**, ἀλλά σε παῖδα ποιεύμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.494; παίδων παῖδες, τοί κεν μετόπισθε γένωνται 20.308, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.100, “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.11 H., etc.; Ἀγήνορος παῖδες ἐκ παίδων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 281}}; frequently in orators of legal **issue**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.9, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.31, etc.}}; of animals, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 50 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**I.2** _metaphorically_, ἀμπέλου π., of wine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.52; χορῶν ἐραστὴς κισσὸς ἐνιαυτοῦ δὲ παῖς 4th c.BC: Chaeremon Tragicus 5}}; ὀρείας πέτρας π., of Echo, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1110}}; ὅρκου π. ἀνώνυμος, of the **penalty** of perjury, Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.86}}.; ἄναυδοι π. τᾶς ἀμιάντου, of fishes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 578 (Lyric poetry).}}
**I.3** _periphrastic_, οἱ Λυδῶν παῖδες **sons** of the Lydians, i. e. the Lydians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.27, compare 5.49; π. Ἑλλήνων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 402}}; οἱ [Ἀσκληπιοῦ] π., i. e. physicians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407e}}; οἱ ζωγράφων π. painters, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 769b}}; παῖδες ῥητόρων orators, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 19}}; π. ἰατρῶν, π. πλαστῶν καὶ γραφέων, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Dips.” 5, “Im.” 9; compare υἱός 2.}}
**II** in relation to Age, **child, boy** or **girl**, νέος π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.665; παῖδες νεαροί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.289; σμίκρα π. 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 34: with another}} _substantive_, π. συφορβός **boy**-swineherd, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.282; παῖδα κόρην γαμεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 595; ἐν παισὶ νέοισι π. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.72; π. ἔτ᾽ ὤν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 755, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.710; ἔτι π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e; παιδὸς μηδὲν βελτίων [prev. work] 342e: distinguished from παιδίον, μειράκιον, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἑβδομάδων - Die Hippokratische Schrift von der Siebenzahl” 5, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.17, “Cyr.” 8.7.6, 1.2.4}}; ἐκ παιδός **from a child**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 374c; ἐκ παιδὸς εἰς γῆρας 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.180; ἐκ τῶν παίδων εὐθύς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 694d, compare “R.” 386a; ἀκούων τῶν παίδων εὐθύς [prev. author] “Lg.” 642b; εὐθὺς ἐκ παίδων ἐξελθών 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.154}}; ἡλικίαν ἔχειν τὴν ἄρτι ἐκ π. to be just out of one's **childhood**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.25; ἐκ μικρῶν π. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1336a14; [Ἡρακλῆς] ἐν παισὶν ὄφεις ἀπέκτεινεν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 56.36}}; ἐν παισὶ (variant{παιδὶ}) ποιμαίνων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 6.8.1}}; χορηγεῖν παισί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (χορηγέω 11): proverbial, τοῦτο κἂν π. γνοίη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 279d; δῆλον τοῦτό γε ἤδη καὶ παιδί [prev. author] “Smp.” 204b; παῖδας [τοὺς πρὸ αὐτοῦ] ἀπέφηνε 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Peregr.” 11, compare “Alex.” 4}}; ἔνι τις καὶ ἐν ἡμῖν π., of the superstitious fears of a child, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 77e, compare 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.41.}}
**III** in relation to Condition, **slave, servant, man** or **maid** (of all ages), παῖ, παῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 653, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 395, 4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 5.2, etc.; παῖ, παιδίον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 132}}: _plural_, of the crew of a ship, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.8. (From Παϝις, compare παῦρος}}, Latin _puer._)

3817:
** παίω**,
_Boeotian dialect_ **πήω** Hdn.Gr.2.949; _Attic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ _imperative_ παῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.18}} codices: _future_ παίσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 688, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.19, παιήσω 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1125, “Lys.” 459}}: _aorist_ ἔπαισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 2.509.4, +others (Crete, 5th c.BC), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 397, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.10}}: _perfect_ πέπαικα {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Num.22.28, (ὑπερ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1118, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.34}}:—_middle_, _imperfect_ ἐπαιόμην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 24}}: _aorist_ ἐπαισάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.3.6}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐπαίσθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 957, “Ch.” 184, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 10:}}_perfect_ πέπαισμαι (ἐμ-) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.543f}}; but the _passive_ tenses were mainly supplied by πλήσσω (παίσαντές τε καὶ πληγέντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 171)}}; and ἐπάταξα (from πατάσσω) was generally used as _aorist_:—poet. Verb (not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, rare in _Attic dialect_ Prose)}}, **strike, smite**, whether with the hand, or with a rod or other weapon, σκυτάλοισί τινας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.137, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1384, etc.: frequently with _accusative_ omitted, παισθεὶς ἔπαισας [prev. author] “Th.” 957}}; παῖε πᾶς **strike home!**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 685}}; παισάτω πᾶς (παῖς codices), παῖ δή, παῖ δή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyn.” [same place]; π. τινὰ ἐς τὴν γῆν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.107; π. τινὰ μάστιγι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 242 (Lyric poetry), etc.; π. ὑφ᾽ ἧπαρ αὑτήν [prev. author] “Ant.” 1315; παίσας πρὸς ἧπαρ φασγάνῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1063; π. τινὰ ἐς τὴν γαστέρα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 549; εἰς τὰ στέρνα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.4; τινὰ ἐς πλευρὰν ξίφει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 794; κατὰ τὸ στέρνον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.26; κάρα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 308, compare “OT” 1270; τὸν νῶτόν τινος 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.43: with double _accusative_, π. ῥοπάλῳ τινὰ τὸ νῶτον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 497: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ὀλίγας π. (i.e. πληγάς) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.12}}; τί μ᾽ οὐκ ἀνταίαν ἔπαισέν τις (i.e. πληγήν) **;** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1309 (Lyric poetry)}}; π. ἅλμην, of rowers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 397, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1391:}} —_middle_, ἐπαίσατο τὸν μηρόν **he smote his** thigh, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.3.6, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 24}}:—_passive_, παιομένους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.47, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 416, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.4, etc.}}; πὺξ παιόμενος, opposed to ἐγχειριδίῳ πληγείς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.6.}}
**b** rarely of missiles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.4.18}}:—_passive_, τὰ παιόμενα τοῖς κεραυνοῖς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.665d}}; of atoms, παίονται καὶ παίουσι τὸν ἅπαντα χρόνον {{Refs|[prev. work]1111e.}}
**2** with _accusative_ instrumenti, **drive, dash** one thing against another, ναῦς ἐν νηῒ στόλον ἔπαισε **struck** its beak against.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 409; π. λαιμῶν εἴσω ξίφος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1472 (Lyric poetry); [ναῦς] θάλασσα π. πρὸς χωρία δύσορμα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 15: _metaphorically_, ἐν δ᾽ ἐμῷ κάρᾳ θεὸς.. μέγα βάρος ἔπαισεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1274 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** **drive away**, τοὺς σφῆκας ἀπὸ τῆς οἰκίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 456.}}
**4** of sexual intercourse, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pax” 874.}}
**5** **hit hard in speaking**, π. στρογγύλοις τοῖς ῥήμασιν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 686, compare 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 63.34.}}
**6** _metaphorically_, **quench** one's **thirst**, διψῶντα.. ὄξει παίειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 138 (_anapaest meter_); compare πατάσσω 11.2.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **strike, dash against** or **upon**, λόγοι παίουσ᾽ εἰκῇ πρὸς κύμασιν ἄτης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 885 (_anapaest meter_); πρὸς τὰς πέτρας π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.3: with _accusative_, ἔπαισεν ἄφαντον ἕρμα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1007 (Lyric poetry)}}; λανθάνει στήλην ἄκραν παίσας, of a charioteer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 745. (From Παϝιω}}, cf. Latin _pavio_, **pavimentum.**)
**III** **devour**, παίειν ἐφ᾽ ἁλὶ τὴν μάδδαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 835, compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; perhaps to be read in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 35.12}}: it has been doubted whether this is the same word as παίω {{Refs|1, but compare ἐρείδω 11.2, κόπτω 1.10, σποδέω 111, φλάω 1.2.}}

3819:
** πάλαι** [ᾰ],
_adverb_ **long ago**, π. οὔ τι νέον γε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.527; π. κοὐ νεωστί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1049; π. πολλὰ ἤδη ἔτη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18b}}; π. ποτέ **once upon a time**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1002, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 110a: frequently with}}_present_ of an act lasting to the _present_, ὁρῶ.. π. I have **long** seen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 5}}; ἰχνεύω π.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 20, compare “Ph.” 589, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 91a, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 257, etc.}}; but, π. ποτ᾽ ὄντες ye who were **long ago**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 1060}}: also with _perfect_, τέθνηχ᾽ ὑμῖν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1030, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 998, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 556: with}}_imperfect_ of an act lasting to a past time, ἔχεν π. had **long** been holding it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.871, compare 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 11; ἔχεις π. ὧν ἐπεθύμεις 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.17: with _Article_, τὸ π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.5, 7.74, 95, 142, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.5, etc.; ἀπὸ π. “BGU” 1036.24 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** like an _adjective_ with _Article_ and Noun, οἱ π. φῶτες men **of old**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.1; Κάδμου τοῦ π. νέα τροφή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1; τοῦ πρόσθε Κάδμου τοῦ π. τ᾽ Ἀγήνορος [prev. work] 268}}; τὰ καινὰ τοῖς π. τεκμαίρεται{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 916, compare “Tr.” 1165, “El.” 1490, etc.; τὰ π. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.276 (nisi to be read παλαί᾽)}}; οἱ π. the **ancients**, opposed to οἱ νῦν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1069a29.}}
**II** **before**, opposed to the present, sometimes of time **just past**, ἠμὲν π. ἠδ᾽ ἔτι καὶ νῦν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.105, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 181}}: hence, **not long ago, just now**, like{ἄρτι}, οἱ π. λόγοι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 845}}; ὁ π. λόγος the reason **just** given, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1282a15, compare 1282b7; π. σοὶ ἔλεγον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.17, compare 18.10}}; but opposed to ἄρτι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 142a.}}

3820:
** πᾰλαιός**, ά, όν,
_Aeolic dialect_ **πάλαος** {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 28.33, “epigram Gr.” 992 (from Balbilla)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **παληός** {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 32.6}}; _Laconian dialect_ **παλεός** (see. below): regular _comparative_ and _superlative_ παλαιότερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.53, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1 codices, παλαιότατος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 83a, etc.: more frequently παλαίτερος, παλαίτατος (from πάλαι), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.58, “N.” 7.44, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.4, etc.}} [The _penultimate_ is sometimes short in Poets, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 956 (assuming variant), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 497, 1st c.AD: Damocrates Poeta Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.1049; παλεός γα ναὶ τὸν Κάστορα ἄνθρωπος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 988}}; in these places παλεός (a form mentioned by Hdn.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Gr. 2.909, compare 9th c.AD: Theognostus Grammaticus “Can.” 50.3, Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lys.” [same place], 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus, and corroborated by the Papyrus (4th c.BC) of Timotheus (see. παλεομίσημα, παλεονυμφάγονος)) may be retained or restored).]:}}
**I** **old in years**,
**I.1** mostly of persons, **aged**, ἢ νέος ἠὲ παλαιός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.108; νέοι ἠδὲ παλαιοί 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.395, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.59U.; παλαιῷ φωτὶ ἐοικώς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.136}}; π. γέρων, π. γρηῦς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.432, 19.346, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 676; χρόνῳ π. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 112; ἐν παλαιτέροισι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.73; ἔνθα δὴ παλαίτατοι θάσσουσι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 68}}: in bad sense, **a dotard** (μωρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, but σκώπτης 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 988.}}
**I.2** of things, οἶνος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.340}}; νῆες.. νέαι ἠδὲ π.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 293; τρὺξ π. καὶ σαπρά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1086; [τριήρεις] π. ἀντὶ καινῶν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.4; ὑποδήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 91d; σπέρματα 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.1.6.}}
**II** **of old date, ancient**,
**II.1** of persons, ξεῖνος π. an **old** guest-friend, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.215, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 263, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 212; Ἴλου παλαιοῦ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.166; κέρδεα.. οἷ᾽ οὔ πώ τιν᾽ ἀκούομεν οὐδὲ παλαιῶν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.118; Μίνως παλαίτατος ὧν ἀκοῇ ἴς μεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.4; οἱ πάνυ π. ἄνθρωποι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 411b}}; οἱ π. the **ancients**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3; π. ἡμερῶν LXX.Dan.7.9.}}
**II.2** of things, λέκτρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.296; παλαιά τε πολλά τε εἰδώς 7.157; καινὰ καὶ π. ἔργα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.26; νόμοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 778 (Lyric poetry); κατὰ τὸ νόμιμον τὸ π. καὶ ἀρχαῖον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.51; κατὰ τὸν π. λόγον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 499c; ἡ π. παροιμία [prev. author] “R.” 329a; παλαί᾽ ἂν [εἴη], ἐξ ὅτου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 493; παλαιᾷ σύντροφος ἁμερᾷ [prev. author] “Aj.” 622 (Lyric poetry)}}; of places, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 17 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 4, etc.}}; καιροὶ π. **ancient** times, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.15 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὸ π. as _adverb_, **anciently, formerly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 102 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.171, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 401c, etc.}}; ἐκ παλαιοῦ from **of old**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.157; ἐκ π. ἐχθρὸς ὢν αὐτοῦ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.5; ἐκ τῶν παλαιῶν 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.102}}; ἐκ παλαιτέρου from **older time**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60; ἐκ παλαιτάτου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.18; also εὐθὺς ἀπὸ παλαιοῦ [prev. work] 2}}; ἀρχαῖα καὶ παλαιά joined, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.14, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator (see. above); παλαιὸν δῶρον ἀρχαίου ποτὲ θηρός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 555.}}
**II.3** of things, also,
**II.3.a** in good sense, **venerable, held in esteem**, like Latin _antiquus_, ἅπερ μέγιστα καὶ παλαιότατα τοῖς ἀνθρώποις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.4.}}
**II.3.b** in bad sense, **antiquated, obsolete**, κωφὰ καὶ π. ἔπη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 290.}}
**II.3.c** π. δρᾶμα a drama **which has been previously acted**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 1078 lxxxvii (Athens, 4th c.BC).}}
**III** _adverb_ παλαιῶς **in an old way**, τὰ καινὰ π. διδάσκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Socratis et Socraticorum Epistulae “Ep.” 30.9: _comparative_ παλαίτερον}} **at an earlier time**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.57, +others}}

3821:
** πᾰλαιότης**, ητος, ἡ,
**age**, π. καὶ πλῆθος ἐτῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.42}}; of seeds, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.1.6.}}
**2** more frequently **antiquity, obsoleteness**, π. γὰρ τῷ λόγῳ γ᾽ ἔνεστί τις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1056; ὑπὸ παλαιότητος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 421d; εἴτε π. εἴτε σαπρότης [prev. author] “R.” 609e}}; π. γράμματος, opposed to καινότης πνεύματος, {{NT+1st c.BC+|NT.Rom.7.6; in Lit. Crit., 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 10.19.}}

3822:
** πᾰλαιόω**,
_aorist_ ἐπαλαίωσα {{LXX|LXX.Lam.3.4}}: _perfect_ πεπαλαίωκα {{NT|NT.Heb.8.13}}: (παλαιός):—**make old**, {{LXX|LXX.Job.9.5, +others}}:—mostly in _passive_, **decay through lapse of time**, οἱ παλαιούμενοι νεκροί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 390a22; κηρὸς παλαιούμενος [prev. author] “HA” 557b6, +others; τὸ.. παλαιούμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 208b, compare “Ti.” 59c; τὸ ἱερὸν πεπαλαιῶσθαι “Sammelb.” 5827.11 (1st c.BC)}}; πεπαλαιωμένον ἔκπτωμα βραχίονος one **which is of long standing**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 7; γένος παλαιωθὲν ὑπὸ χρόνου 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.10; πεπαλαιωμένε ἡμερῶν κακῶν LXX.Sus.52}}; of wine, **become old**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.7.5, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.14, +others, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.33a.}}
**II** **abrogate, cancel**, [διαθήκην] {{NT|NT.Heb.[same place]}}

3823:
** πάλη** [ᾰ], ἡ,
**A** **wrestling**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.635; ἢ πὺξ ἠὲ πάλῃ ἢ καὶ ποσίν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.206: κρατέων πάλα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.20; νικᾶν πυγμὴν καὶ π. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1031. compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.(Sp.”) 62, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 795b, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.638d, 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 6.28.3; τίνα π. ἐμάνθανε; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1238}}; παίδων, ἐφήβων νεωτέρων, μέσων, πρεσβυτέρων, ἀνδρῶν π., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 959 (from Chios), compare “IG” 5(2).549.16,30 (Lycosura, 4th c.BC), etc.}}
**A.2** generally, **fight, battle**, ἅπτειν πάλην τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 866 (_anapaest meter_); π. δορός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 159.}}

3824:
** πᾰλιγ-γενεσία**, ἡ,
**rebirth, regeneration**, of the world, παλιγγενεσίας ἡγεμόνες, of Noah and his sons, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author = 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus] 2.144; ἡ ἀνάκτησις καὶ π. τῆς πατρίδος 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 11.3.9}}; **renewal** of a race, {{Refs|“Corpus Hermeticum” 3.3}}; of persons, **beginning of a new life**, εἰς π. ὁρμᾶν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.159, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Musc.Enc.” 7}}: hence of **restoration** after exile, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 6.6.4}}; **transmigration, reincarnation** of souls, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.998c}}; compare μετεμψύχωσις near the end
**2** in Stoic Philos., **rebirth** of the κόσμος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.191: _plural_, [prev. work] 187, 2nd c.BC: Boethus Sidonius Stoicus 3.265; so later, ἡ περιοδικὴ π. τῶν ὅλων 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 11.1, compare 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Timaeum commentarii” 3.241 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**3** in Medicine texts, **relapse**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.83}}; **regrowth** of a tumour, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 45.2.7.}}
**II** in Roman Law, = **restitutio natalium**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 18.11.}}
**III** in “NT.”,
**III.1** **resurrection,** {{NT|NT.Matt.19.28.}}
**III.2** **regeneration** by baptism, διὰ λουτροῦ παλιγγενεσίας {{NT|NT.Tit.3.5.}}

3825:
** πάλῐν** [ᾰ],
_poetry_ also **πάλι** (q.see), _adverb_
**1** of Place, **back, backwards** (the usual sense in early _Epic dialect_), mostly joined with Verbs of going, coming, etc.; π. χώρει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.72}}; π. ἐλεύσεται, κατελθεῖν, ἐπανέλθωμεν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 854, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 601, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 438a, etc.; κέλευθον ἥνπερ ἦλθες ἐγκόνει π. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 962; δίκα καὶ πάντα π. στρέφεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 412 (Lyric poetry)}}; δεῦρο σωθήσῃ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 725, compare 1400}}; δόμεναι π. give **back, restore**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.116, etc.; π. ἀποδοῦναι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.23}}; π. ἀγκαλέσαι to call **back**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1021 (Lyric poetry): less frequently with _genitive_, π. τράπεθ᾽ υἷος ἑοῖο}} she turned **back from** her son, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.138; δόρυ Ἀχιλλῆος π. ἔτραπεν 20.439, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.143}}: coupled with other _adverbs_, π. αὖτις ἔβαινον νηὸς ἐπὶ γλαφυρῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|14.356, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.65}}; αὖ π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.125; ἂψ π. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.280; π. εἶσιν ὀπίσσω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.149; π. φέρεσθαι ἐξοπίσω 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 181}}; ἄψορρον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 53}}; π. οἴκαδε, π. οἴκαδ᾽ αὖ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 792, “Ra.” 1486; π. αὖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 318e, etc.}}: with the _Article_, ἡ π. ὁδός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 125.}}
**2** to express **contradiction**, π. ἐρέει **gain**say, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.56}}; π. ὅ γε λάζετο μῦθον took **back** his word, **unsaid** it, {{Refs|4.357}}; opposed to ἀληθέα εἰπεῖν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.254}}; μηδέ τῳ δόξῃ π. let no one think **contrariwise**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1045}}: in Prose, **contrariwise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 482d; π. αὖ [prev. author] “R.” 507b}}; αὖ.. π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ap.” 27d: in this sense sometimes with _genitive_, τὸ π. νεότατος}} youth's **opposite**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).87}}; χρόνου τὸ π. **the change** of time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 777 (Lyric poetry)}}; compare ἔμπαλιν.
**II** of Time, **again, once more**, rare in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.276, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1166, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.11, etc.: frequently coupled with αὖ, αὖθις (which see)}}; π. ἐξ ἀρχῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 997, etc.}}; π. καὶ π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.565d, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 1.4}}; ἔγχει καὶ π. εἰπέ, π. π. Ἡλιοδώρας” {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.135 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus): both senses (I and II) are appropriate in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.456, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 322b, etc.}}
**III** **in turn**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 371, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 342, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 87, etc.; π. ὁ Κῦρος ἠρώτα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.7; π. ἀπαιτῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 612d}}; **again**, πρῶτον μέν.. ἔπειτα π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1289b29, etc. (In _composition_ πάλιν}} sometimes means **doubly**, as in παλιμμήκης, παλίνσκιος.)

3827:
** πάμ-πολυς**, πόλλη
(see. below), πολυ, **very great, large**, or **numerous**, κατάγελως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 320; γῆς πλῆθος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 677e; στράτευμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.26; τύχη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 640d (but πάμπολλος as _feminine_, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cyn.” 1, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 4.8, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 89.12, “Synt.” 36.27)}}: in _plural_, **very many**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 694, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 373c, etc.}}
**II** _neuter_ πάμπολυ, as _adverb_, **very much**, [{{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 255d, etc.; compare {πάμπολις}}}]

3831:
** πᾰνήγῠρ-ις**,
_Doric dialect_ **πανᾱγ-**, εως, ἡ, (πᾶς, ἄγυρις) **general** or **national assembly**, ἐν πανηγύρει βουλευσόμεσθα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 845}}; especially **a festal assembly** in honour of a national god, Δήμητρος ἁγνῆς καὶ Κόρης {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 120; Ζηνὸς ἀμφὶ πανάγυριν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.96}}; πανηγύριας πανηγυρίζειν, ποιήσασθαι, to hold **such festivals**, keep **holy-days**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.59, 58; συναγαγεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.1; διαλύειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.10; ἐς π. θεωρεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 342}}; θεωρίαι ἐς τὰς ἐν τῇ Ἑλλάδι π. Decrees cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.91; Ὀλυμπίαζε εἰς τὴν τῶν Ἑλλήνων π. ἐπανιών 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 363c; in Caria, “PCair.Zen.” 341 (a) 11 (3rd c.BC)}}; in Egypt, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 27.76 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; ἐν ταῖς π. καὶ δείξεσι τῶν σοφιστῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.256S.; ἁμίλλαις ἱππικαῖς καὶ πανηγύρει προσκαθήμενος 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.39c; π. ἐμπορικόν τι πρᾶγμα 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.5.4, compare “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4474.35 (from Baetocaece), Prisc.p.277 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**2** **any assembly**, θεῶν ἅδε πανάγυρις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 220 (Lyric poetry)}}; νεοσσῶν, φίλων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 239, “HF” 1283.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, π. ὀφθαλμῶν **feast** for the eyes, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 3.1, compare 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 59.145}}; τὸν.. Ἔχετον χρυσὸν ἀποδείξων καὶ π. destined to exhibit {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} as gold and a **feast** (by comparison with himself), {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.236D.}}
**3.b** in bad sense, παίγνιον ὁ βίος καὶ πλάνη καὶ π. {{Refs|Vett. Val. 246.2.}}
**II** **people assembled**, τῇ π. δέος ἐγένετο μὴ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.50}}: generally, **crowd, audience**, ὅταν ᾖ π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 6.7.}}

3834:
** πᾰνουργ-ία**, ἡ,
**knavery**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 603, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 927, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 747c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1144a27}}: in _plural_, **villainies**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 300, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 684, etc.}}
**2** of animals, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 588a23 (_plural_), 614a30.}}
**3** **adulteration** of drugs or honey, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.27.}}

3835:
** πᾰνουργ-ος**
(_properispomenon_), ον, **ready to do anything, wicked, knavish**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 384, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 766, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 250, 450, +others: _superlative_, opposed to εὐηθέστερος, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.44}}: as _substantive_, **knave, rogue**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1400, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 249, +others; ὦ πανοῦργε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1257, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 311}}; τὰ π. **the knavish sort**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 448}}; τὸ π., ={πανουργία}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 1507}}: _comparative_ **-ότερος**{{LXX|LXX.Pro.21.11}}; **-έστερος** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.395c}}: _superlative_ **-ότατος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 45, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator [prev. cited]}}
**2** _adverb_ **-γως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 317, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 239c}}: _superlative_ **-ότατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 56.}}
**2.b** πανούργως κατασκευάζεσθαι to be **adulterated**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.54.}}
**3** of animals, as the fox, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 488b20, compare 613b23.}}
**II** in a less positively bad sense, **cunning, clever, smart**, π. καὶ δεινός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 177a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1144a28; π. τε καὶ σοφός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 409c; κομψὸς καὶ π. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.28a}}: _superlative_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.75.2}}. _adverb_ -γως, π. καὶ ὑποκριτικῶς λέγειν τὰ ἔπη {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.407a.}}

3836:
** παντᾰχόθεν**,
_adverb_ **from all quarters, from every side**, ἐκ τῆς Ἀσίης π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.25, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1007, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190e, +others}}; περιέχεσθαι π. **on all sides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.80.}}
**II** **from every side**, i.e. **in every way**, π. ἡ Ἑλλὰς κατείχετο μή.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.17}}; π. καλῶς ὑπάρχον πολεμεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 124, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.25; π. βάσκανος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.242.}}

3837:
** παντᾰχοῦ**,
_adverb_ **everywhere**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117 (nisi to be read πενταχοῦ), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.108, etc.; οἱ φρονοῦντες εὖ κρατοῦσι π. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1252; οὐδαμοῦ καὶ π. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 568; ἄλλοθι π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 160a: with _genitive_, π. τῆς γῆς (variant{πολλαχοῦ}) [prev. author] “Phd.” 111a}}]: later with Verbs of Motion, ἐξῆλθε ἡ ἀκοὴ αὐτοῦ π. {{NT|NT.Mark.1.28: in early writers πανταχοῖ}} should be _restored_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 68, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1230.}}
**II** **altogether, absolutely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 503a}}; οὐ π. ᾔσθησαι not **at all**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prm.” 128b.}}

3838:
** παντελής**, ές,
**all-complete, absolute**, παντελῆ σάγην ἔχων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 560; μοναρχία 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1163}}; πανοπλία, ἐλευθερία, μανία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 796c, 698a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 38.17}}; π. δάμαρ **mistress** of the house, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 930}}; δήμου π. ψηφίσματα **with full authority**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 601; π. κήρυγμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 13}}; π. ἐσχάραι **complete tale** of sacrificial hearths, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 1016.}}
**2** π. δύναμις ἁ τᾶς δεκάδος **perfect**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1 “Prooem.” 3, compare 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 195.}}
**II** _active_, **all-accomplishing**, Ζεύς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 118 (Lyric poetry, assuming variant); χρόνος [prev. author] “Ch.” 965 (Lyric poetry); π. εὐεργέτης 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 79.}}
**III** _adverb_ παντελῶς, _Ionic dialect_ -έως, **altogether, utterly**, with Verbs, διῶρυξ π. πεποιημένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.37; λίθινα π. ἐξειργασμένα “IG” 12.372.93}}; παντελέως εἶχε τὸ οἴκημα it was **quite finished**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.95; π. διώρισε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 440; π. κρανθήσεται [prev. work] 911}}; π. θανεῖν to die **outright**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 669; ἐκμεμάθηκα ταῦτα π. 4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 4, etc.: with _adjectives_, π. βαθεῖα φάλαγξ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.34; π. ἄφρων 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 694; ἄχρηστα π. 4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 12; π. Βοιώτιοι 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 237.1}}; οὐ π. not **at all**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 5}}; **from first to last**, π. ἕως ἂν διεξέλθῃ διὰ πάντων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1298a16.}}
**III.2** in answers, **most certainly**, παντελῶς γε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 379c, 485d; π. μὲν οὖν [prev. author] “Prm.” 155c, 160b, “R.” 401a.}}
**III.3** later εἰς τὸ παντελές, ={παντελῶς}, {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.567, NT.Luke.13.11, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 17.27, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.30, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.61c}}; = **for ever,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Rev.Bibl. 39.544,546 (from Palmyra), “PLond.” 3.1164f11 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}; κατὰ τὸ π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.90, +others}}

3839:
** πάντῃ**
(σὺν τῷ ῑ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Legal icon Messanense” p.409, and so written in “PEleph.” 1.14 (4th c.BC))}}, _Doric dialect_ **παντᾷ** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.117, 9.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 169, 180, “Tab.Heracl.” 1.141}}:—also **παντεῖ**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 366 A 12 (Tolophon, 3rd c.BC)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **πάντᾳ** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in 2076ii 18}}: _adverb_:—**every way, on every side**, frequently followed by a _preposition_, π. ἀνὰ στρατόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.384; π. περὶ τεῖχος 12.177, etc.; π. ἀμφὶ νέκυν 23.34; π. φοιτῶντες ἐπ᾽ αἶαν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 125; ὅσον τε ἐπὶ ιή σταδίους.. π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126; also π. παπταίνοντι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.233; διασκοπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 246}}; ἱρὸν δύο σταδίων π. **on every side**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.181, compare 2.168; κύκλῳ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.2.}}
**II** **in every way, altogether**, π. συγγίνεσθαι ἀήθεα {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 22.8; οὐδὲν ἦν π. καλόν “Tragica Adespota” 26; π. πάντως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 29c, “Prm.” 160b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1100b20; πάντως καὶ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 60c; π. καὶ πανταχοῦ NT.Act.24.3}}; οὐ π. not **quite**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.8, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 2.}}

3843:
** πάντως**,
_adverb_, (πᾶς) **in all ways**, ἄλλως τε π. καὶ.., i.e. **especially (since**).., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 689, “Eu.” 726, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 35d; σκιδνάμενον πάντῃ π. 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 2.3; περιφέρεσθαι καὶ π. φέρεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 411b, compare “Grg.” 527a: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus always followed by οὐ}}, **in no wise, by no means**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.450, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.91, +others; πάντως γὰρ οὐ πείσεις νιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 335, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.34, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 497b, etc.: without οὐ first in 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus [same place], 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus (see. above)}}; ἔδεε πάντως it was **absolutely** necessary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31; εἰ δὴ δεῖ γε π. [prev. author] 7.10}}. ; π. θελῆσαι to wish **at all hazards**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.42}}; εἰ π. ἐλεύσονται if they **positively will** go, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.9}}; π. κου πυνθάνεαι **no doubt**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.157}}: with an _adjective_, π. ἀναρίστητος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 233: frequently with πᾶς or its derivations, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.87, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 107d, +others; compare πάντῃ 11.}}
**II** in strong affirmations, **at all events, at any rate**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 16, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.111, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 33d, NT.1Cor.9.22, etc.; so νηστεύωμεν δὲ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 984 (Lyric poetry); π. κρέ᾽ ἡμῖν ἐστίν 4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 15.11; π. γε μήν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 232; π. δήπου [prev. author] “Th.” 805}}; **assuredly**, opposed to ἴσως, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.222a}}; παρήγγειλά σοι ὅτι μὴ ἀπέλθῃς.., καὶ ἀπῆλθες π. and you did (_emphatic_), {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 7249.5 (3rd-4th c.AD)}}; τάχ᾽ οὖν.. μᾶλλον δὲ π. nay rather **I am sure**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.89}}; π. ὅτι.. **evidently** because.., {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 96}} (but, **it follows that**.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 86).}}
**II.2** with _imperative_, in command or entreaty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.156, etc.}}; ἀλλ᾽ ἐμοὶ πείθεσθε π. do **but** obey me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 357}}; π. παρατίθετε **just** put on the table, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175b}}; καὶ τὸ ἱερεῖον δὲ π. ἡμῖν ἀπόστειλον **be sure to** send.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 191.14 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.3** in answers, **by all means, no doubt**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 574b}}; πάντως γάρ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 273; π. δήπου 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.102, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 75e, etc.}}

3844:
** πᾰρά** [ρᾰ],
_Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry also **παραί**: shortened **πάρ**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, Lyric poetry (but rarely in Trag., in Lyric poetry passages, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 553, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 636)}}, and in all dial ects except _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“GDI” 5434.8 (from Paros), “IG” 5(2).3.14 (Tegea, 4th c.BC), “Inscription Magn.” 26.28 (Thess.)}}, etc.:—_preposition_ with _genitive_, _dative_, and _accusative_, properly **beside**: hence,
**A** WITH GEN. properly denoting motion **from the side of, from beside, from**:
**A.I** of Place, πὰρ νηῶν ἔλθωμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.744; παρὰ ναῦφιν ἐλευσόμεθ᾽ 12.225, etc.; παρ᾽ Ὠκεανοῖο ῥοάων.. ἐπερχομένη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.197; πὰρ νηῶν ἀπώσεται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.533, etc.; δῶρα π. νηὸς ἐνεικέμεν 19.194; φάσγανον ὀξὺ ἐρυσσάμενος π. μηροῦ 1.190, compare 21.173; σπασσάμενος.. ἄορ παχέος π. μηροῦ 16.473}}; πλευρὰ παρ᾽ ἀσπίδος ἐξεφαάνθη was exposed **beside** the shield,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4.468, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 624.}}
**A.II** commonly of Persons,
**A.II.1** with Verbs of going or coming, bringing, etc., ἦλθε.. πὰρ Διός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.787; παρ᾽ Αἰήταο πλέουσα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.70, etc.; ἀγγελίη ἥκει π. βασιλέος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140. ; αὐτομολήσαντες π. βασιλέως 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.13; ἐξεληλυθὼς παρ᾽ Ἀριστάρχου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.117}}; ὁ π. τινὸς ἥκων his messenger, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.53; so οἱ π. τινός 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.10, NT.Mark.3.21, etc.; ὅστις ἀφικνεῖτο τῶν π. βασιλέως πρὸς αὐτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.5, etc.}}; τεύχεα καλὰ φέρουσα παρ᾽ Ἡφαίστοιο **from** his workshop, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.137, compare 617, etc.; ἀπαγγέλλειν τι π. τινός 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.20; σὺ δὲ οἰμώζειν αὐτοῖς παρ᾽ ἐμοῦ λέγε 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 1.2.}}
**A.II.2** **issuing from** a person, γίγνεσθαι π. τινός to be born **from**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 179b}}; λόγος (i.e. ἐστί) π. Ἀθηναίων with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.55: frequently following a Noun, δόξα ἡ π. τῶν ἀνθρώπων}} glory **from (given by**) men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 232a}}; ἡ π. τινὸς εὔνοια the favour **from**, i. e. **of**, any one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.12}}; τὸ παρ᾽ ἐμοῦ ἀδίκημα **done by** me, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 5.5.13}}; τὰ π. τινός **all that issues from** any one, as commands, commissions, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 2.3.4, etc.; or promises, gifts, presents, [prev. author] “Mem.” 3.11.13}}; τὰ παρ᾽ ἐμοῦ my opinions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 219a}}; παρ᾽ ἑωυτοῦ διδούς giving **from** oneself, i. e. **from** one's own **means**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.129, 8.5; παρ᾽ ἑαυτοῦ προσετίθει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.3}}; νόμον θὲς παρ᾽ ἐμοῦ **by** my **advice**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 322d}}; αὐτοὶ παρ᾽ αὑτῶν **of** themselves, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 150d, compare “Phdr.” 235c.}}
**A.II.3** with Verbs of receiving, obtaining, and the like, τυχεῖν τινος π. τινός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.290, 15.158; πὰρ δ᾽ ἄρα μιν Ταφίων πρίατο 14.452; ἀρέομαι πὰρ μὲν Σαλαμῖνος Ἀθαναίων χάριν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.76; εὑρέσθαι τι π. τῶν θεῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.14, compare “IG” 12.40.10}}; δέχεσθαι, λαμβάνειν, ἁρπάζειν π. τινός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 9.11, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 914}}; ἀντιάσαι, αἰτήσασθαι π. τινός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 870 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.4; ἀξιοῖ π. τοῦ ἰατροῦ φάρμακον πιὼν ἐξεμέσαι τὸ νόσημα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 406d; κόσμος τοῖς πράξασι γίγνεται π. τῶν ἀκουσάντων [prev. author] “Mx.” 236e: without Verb, ὁ καρπὸς ὁ π. τῶν δημάρχων “IG” 12.76.27: with Verbs of learning, etc., μεμαθηκέναι π. τινῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.104, etc.}}
**A.II.4** with _passive_ Verbs, πὰρ Διὸς.. μῆνις ἐτύχθη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.122; π. θεῶν ἡ τοιαύτη μανία δίδοται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 245c, etc.}}; τὰ π. τῶν θεῶν σημαινόμενα, συμβουλευόμενα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.2; τὰ π. τινὸς λεγόμενα [prev. work] 6.1.42}}; τὰ π. τῆς τύχης δωρηθέντα **the presents of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.26; με π. σοῦ σοφίας πληρωθήσεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175e.}}
**A.III** rarely for παρά with _dative_, **by, near**, πὰρ ποδός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.62, 3.60}}; παρὰ δὲ κυανέων πελαγέων uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 966 (Lyric poetry); τὸν Ῥειτὸν τὸν παρὰ τοῦ ἄστεως “IG” 12.81.5}}; πολλοὶ παρ᾽ ἀμφοτέρων ἔπιπτον, ={ἀμφοτέρωθεν}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.42.}}
**A.IV** π. τῆς συγχωρήσεώς τινος **without** his consent, {{Refs|“BCH” 46.337 (from Teos).}}
**B** WITH DAT. denoting rest **by the side of** any person or thing, answering the question **where**?
**B.I** of Places, κατ᾽ ἂρ ἕζετ᾽.. πὰρ πυρί, ἔκειτο π. σηκῷ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.154, 9.319; νέμονται π. πέτρῃ 13.408; ἑσταότες παρ ὄχεσφιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.565}}; πὰρ ποσὶ μαρναμένων ἐκυλίνδετο **at** their feet, {{Refs|14.411}}, etc.; π. θύρῃσι **at** the door,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|7.346; π. ῥηγμῖνι θαλάσσης 2.773; δεῖπνον.. εἵλοντο παρ᾽ ὄχθῃσιν ποταμοῖο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.97, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.475, 20.53, etc.; κεῖσθαι παρ᾽ Ἅιδῃ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 972}}; παρ᾽ οἴνῳ **over** wine, {{Refs|[prev. work] 780, etc.}}
**B.II** of persons, **beside**, πὰρ δὲ οἷ αὐτῷ εἷσε Θεοκλύμενον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.285; κεῖτο παρὰ μνηστῇ ἀλόχῳ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.556, compare 6.246, etc.; παρ᾽ ἀνδράσιν εὐνάζεσθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.119; δαίνυσθαι π. τινί 8.243}}; πὰρ δέ οἱ ἑστήκει stood **by** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.367.}}
**B.II.2** **at** one's **house** or **place, with** one, μένειν π. τισί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|9.427; θητευέμεν ἄλλῳ, ἀνδρὶ παρ᾽ ἀκλήρῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.490; φιλέεσθαι π. τινί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.627}}; παρ᾽ ἑωυτοῖσι **at** their own **house**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.105, compare 86; παιδευθῆναι π. τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.15; καταλύειν π. τινί 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.82 (but παρά τινα καταλῦσαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.136), etc.: hence οἱ παρ᾽ ἐμοί}} those **of my household**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.4, etc.}}; τὰ παρ᾽ ἐμοί life **with me**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 1.7.4}}; οἱ παρ᾽ ἡμῖν ἄνθρωποι our people, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 64b}}; ἡ παρ᾽ ἡμῖν πολιτεία, ὁ παρ᾽ ὑμῖν δῆμος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.19}}; ὁ παρ᾽ αὑτῷ βίοτος one's **own** life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 612; τὸ παρ᾽ ἡμῖν πῦρ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 29f; ὅσος παρ᾽ ὑμῖν ὁ φθόνος φυλάσσεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 382; τὸ παρ᾽ ἡμῖν σῶμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 29f}}; also, **in** one's **hands**, τὰ π. τοῖς Ἑλληνοταμίαις ὄντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.91.6; ἔχειν παρ᾽ ἑωυτῷ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.130, etc.}}; οὔπω παρ᾽ ἐμοὶ τότ᾽ ἦν λέγειν I had no **right** to speak then, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 98.}}
**B.II.3** **before, in the presence of**, ἤειδε π. μνηστῆρσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.154}}; **before** a judge, δίκας γίγνεσθαι π. τῷ πολεμάρχῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.16.9; π. Δαρείῳ κριτῇ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.160; π. τῷ βασιλέϊ [prev. author] 4.65; παρὰ δικασταῖς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.73; εἰς κρίσιν καθιστάναι τινὰ π. τισί 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.13: hence παρ᾽ ἐμοί}} **in** my **judgement**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 589, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 881, NT.1Cor.3.19}}; π. τούτῳ μέγα δυνήσεται **with** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 510e.}}
**B.II.4** in quoting authors, παρ᾽ Ἐφόρῳ, παρ᾽ Αἰσχίνῃ, π. Θουκυδίδῃ, **in** Ephorus, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.2.4, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 9, 18.}}
**B.III** [{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, ={π}}}]. with _genitive_, **from**, καθὰ εἶχον τὰς ἰντολὰς π. τᾷ ἰδίᾳ πόλι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 559.9 (Megalopolis, 3rd c.BC), compare 558.10 (Ithaca, 3rd c.BC).}}
**C** WITH ACCU{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus in three main senses}}
**C.I** **beside, near, by**,
**C.II** **along**,
**C.III** **past, beyond.**
**C.I** **beside, near, by**:
**C.I.1** with Verbs of coming, going, etc., **to the side of, to**, ἴτην π. νῆας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.347, compare 8.220, etc.; βῆ.. π. θῖνα 1.34, compare 327, etc.}}; τρέψας πὰρ ποταμόν **to the side of**.., {{Refs|21.603, compare 3.187: more frequently of persons, εἶμι παρ᾽ Ἥφαιστον}}**to the chamber of** H.,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|18.143, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.285, etc.; ἐσιόντες π. τοὺς φίλους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51, etc.; φοιτᾶν π. τὸν Σωκράτη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59d}}; πέμπειν ἀγγέλους, πρέσβεις π. τινά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.141, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.58, etc.; ἄγειν π. τινά 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86; καταφυγὴ π. φίλων τινάς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.17.}}
**C.I.2** with Verbs of rest, **beside, near, by**, sometimes with _reference_ to past motion (expressed in such phrases as ἧσο παρ᾽ αὐτὸν ἰοῦσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.406, compare 11.577), ἔς ῥα θρόνους ἕζοντο παρ᾽ Ἀτρεΐδην Μενέλαον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.51, compare 13.372}}; κεῖται ποταμοῖο παρ᾽ ὄχθας lies **stretched beside**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.487, compare 12.381}}; παρ᾽ ἔμ᾽ ἵστασο come and stand **by** me, {{Refs|11.314, compare 592, 20.49}}, etc.; π. πυθμέν᾽ ἐλαίης θῆκαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.122; καταθέτω π. τὰ ἴκρια “IG” 12.94.28}}; κοιμήσαντο π. πρυμνήσια they lay down **by**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.32, compare 3.460; ὁ παρ᾽ ἐμὲ καθήμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 271b, compare “Phd.” 89b}}; ἐκάθητο π. τὴν πύλην, π. τὴν ὁδόν, [{{LXX+NT+8th c.BC+|LXX.Gen.19.1, NT.Mark.10.46; παρ᾽ αὐτὸν τὸν καλέσαντα κατακείμενος δειπνῆσαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 21.2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175c; ἐκαθέζετο π. τὸν Λύσιν [prev. author] “Ly.” 211a, compare “R.” 328c; στὰς παρ᾽ αὐτόν [prev. author] “Phd.” 116c; τέμενος νεμόμεσθα.. παρ᾽ ὄχθας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.313, compare 6.34, “IG” 12.943.45; τοῦ Εὐρίπου, παρ᾽ ὃν ᾤκει 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.90; κατελείφθη π. τὸν νηόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.87; τὴν παρ᾽ ἐμὲ ἐοῦσαν δύναμιν [prev. author] 8.140.ά (variant{ἐμοί})}}]; εἶπεν αὐτῷ μένειν παρ᾽ ἑαυτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.18, compare “An.” 1.9.31, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 451, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.16, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 248, Demetr.Comedy texts Nov. 1.5, “IG” 22.654.23 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.26.1, 11.14.3, 28.14.3; ἡ π. θάλασσαν Μακεδονία 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.99, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 184 (Lyric poetry), “Tr.” 636 (Lyric poetry); Καρβασυανδῆς π. Καῦνον “IG” 12.204.52; τὸ κουρεῖον τὸ π. τοὺς Ἑρμᾶς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 23.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.62, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.20, 8.35, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.182, 3.88, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 112; τᾶς παστάδος τᾶς παρ᾽ Ἀπόλλωνα “IG” 42 (1).109 iii 146 (Epid.)}}; παρ᾽ ὄμμα **before** one's eyes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 484}}; π. πόδας **on** the spot, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.78 W., “Rh.” 2.2 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; **immediately thereafter**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.7.5, 1.8.2, +others}}
**C.I.2.b** _Doric dialect_, _Boeotian dialect_, and Thess., = above {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.2}}, **at the house of.., with** a person, {{Refs|“IG” 7.3171.7 (Orchom. _Boeotian dialect_), “GDI” 1717 (Delph.)}}; παρ᾽ ἁμὲ πολυτίματος [ὁ σῖτος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 759 (Megarian dialect); τοῖς κατοικέντεσσι πὰρ ἀμμέ “IG” 9(2).517.18 (Larissa, 3rd c.BC); τοῖ πὰρ ἀμμὲ πολιτεύματος [prev. work]13; πεπολιτευκὼρ πὰρ ἁμέ “Schwyzer” 425.5 (Elis, 3rd-2nd c.BC): so in _Attic dialect_, θέμενος π. γυναῖκας}} depositing **with**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 465c.}}
**C.I.3** with Verbs of striking, wounding, etc., βάλε στῆθος π. μαζόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.480, etc.; τὸν δ᾽ ἕτερον.. κληῗδα παρ᾽ ὦμον πλῆξε 5.146; τύψε κατὰ κληῗδα παρ᾽ αὐχένα 21.117, compare 4.525, 8.325, etc.}}; αἰχμὴ δ᾽ ἐξελύθη παρὰ νείατον ἀνθερεῶνα {{Refs|5.293, compare 17.310; δησάμενος τελαμῶνι π. σφυρόν [prev. work] 290.}}
**C.I.4** with Verbs of placing, examining, etc., **side by side with**.., ὁ ἔλεγχος π. τὸν ἔλεγχον παραβαλλόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 475e, compare “Hp.Mi.” 369c, “Smp.” 214c, “R.” 348a; ἐξέτασον παρ᾽ ἄλληλα τὰ σοὶ κἀμοὶ βεβιωμένα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.265; ἄλλα παρ᾽ ἄλλατιθέμενα.. τῶν χρωμάτων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 375a24.}}
**C.I.4.b** Geometry texts, παραβάλλειν π. apply an area **to** (i. e. **along**) a finite straight line, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1.44, 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἰσορροπιῶν” 2.1; π. τὴν δοθεῖσαν αὐτοῦ γραμμὴν παρατείναντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 87a}}; ἡ [εὐθεῖα] παρ᾽ ἣν δύνανται αἱ καταγόμεναι τεταγμένως the line **to** which are applied the squares of the or dinates, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Perg. “Con.” 1.11: hence}}
**C.I.4.c** Arith., παραβάλλειν τι π. τι divide **by**.. (see. παραβάλλω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus VII. 2); μερίζω τι π. τι 3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus 4.33}}; ἐπὶ γ π. ί multiply by {{Refs 6th c.AD+|3 and divide by 10, “PLond.” 5.1718.2 (6th c.AD).}}
**C.I.5** Geometry texts, **parallel to**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 155, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 158b31, 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “Sph. Cyl.” 1.12, +others}}
**C.I.6** _metaphorically_ in Grammars, **like, as a parody of**.., π. τὸ Σοφόκλειον, π. τὰ ἐν Τεύκρῳ Σοφοκλέους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1240, “Nu.” 584.}}
**C.I.6.b** Grammars, of words which differ **as compared with** other words, π. τὸ τοῦ ἔρωτος ὄνομα σμικρὸν παρηγμένον ἐστίν.. [τὸ ἥρως] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 398d, compare 399a, “Lg.” 654a}}: hence, **derived from**.., π. τὸ ἔδαφος, δάπεδον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 31.16}}; π. τὸ δρῶ δρᾶμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.624; σύγκειται [τὸ αὐθέντης] π. τὸ εἷναι.. καὶ π. τὸ αὐτός 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.24 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**C.I.7** generally, of Comparison, **alongside of, compared with**, usually implying superiority, δοκέοντες π. ταῦτα οὐδ᾽ ἂν τοὺς σοφωτάτους ἀνθρώπων Αἰγυπτίους οὐδὲν ἐπεξευρεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.160, compare 7.20, 103; ἡλίου ἐκλείψεις αἳ πυκνότεραι π. τὰ ἐκ τοῦ πρὶν χρόνου μνημονευόμενα ξυνέβησαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23, compare 4.6; τῶν ἁπάντων ἀπερίοπτοί εἰσι π. τὸ νικᾶν [prev. author] 1.41; π. τὰ ἄλλα ζῷα ὥσπερ θεοὶ ἄνθρωποι βιοτεύουσι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.14; φαίνεται π. τὸ ἀλγεινὸν ἡδὺ καὶ π. τὸ ἡδὺ ἀλγεινὸν ἡ ἡσυχία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 584a, compare “Phdr.” 236d, “La.” 183c, +others; εὐδαίμων μᾶλλον π. πάντας “BCH” 26.332 (from Halae)}}; προετέρει π. πάντας {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.422.34 (3rd c.BC): sometimes implying inferiority or defect, ἠλάττωσας αὐτὸν βραχύ τι παρ᾽ ἀγγέλους}} a little **lower than** the angels, {{LXX|LXX.Psa.8.6}}; μιᾷ ἡμέρᾳ ὑστεροῦσι π. τὸν ἥλιον lag one day **behind** the sun, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 8.19}}; so perhaps παρ᾽ αὐτόν, ὑπὲρ αὐτόν (has passed the ball?) **short of** him, beyond him, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 234}}; μέγα τοι ἡμέρα παρ᾽ ἡμέραν γιγνομένη γνώμην ἐξ ὀργῆς μεταστῆσαι one day **compared with** another is important.., a day's delay makes a difference, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.72}}; τί γὰρ παρ᾽ ἦμαρ ἡμέρα τέρπειν ἔχει προσθεῖσα κἀναθεῖσα τοῦ γε κατθανεῖ; what joy has one day **compared with** another to offer, since it only brings us nearer to, or farther from, death (which is neither good nor evil)? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 475}}; ὃς μὲν κρίνει (prefers) ἡμέραν παρ᾽ ἡμέραν, ὃς δὲ κρίνει (approves) πᾶσαν ἡμέραν {{NT|NT.Rom.14.5.}}
**C.I.8** with Verbs of estimating, to set **at** so and so much, hence π. = **equivalent to**.., ταρβῶ μὴ.. θῆται παρ᾽ οὐδὲν τὰς ἐμὰς ἐπιστολάς set **at** nought, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 732, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 229 (Lyric poetry); παρ᾽ οὐδὲν ἄγειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 35}}; π. μικρὸν ἡγεῖσθαι or ποιεῖσθαί τι hold **of** small **account**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.79, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.51; παρ᾽ ὀλίγον ποιεῖσθαί τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.11}}; so with εἶναι, etc., παρ᾽ οὐδέν ἐστι are **as** nothing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 983, compare “Ant.” 466; παρ᾽ οὐδὲν αὐταῖς ἦν ἂν ὀλλύναι πόσεις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 569; οὐ π. μέγα ἔσεσθαι τὸ πταῖσμα 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.18.6}}; so perhaps π. σμικρὰ κεχώρηκε have turned out **of** little **account**, have **amounted to** little, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120.}}
**C.I.8.b** in Accountancy, without a verb, π. τὴν καταλλαγήν **on account of** κ., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.100.4 (3rd c.BC).}}
**C.I.9** of correspondence, ὀφείλειν στατῆρα π. στατῆρα stater **for** stater (one to each of two creditors), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“BCH” 50.214 (Thasos, 5th c.BC)}}; πληγὴν π. πληγὴν ἑκάτερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 643}}; συνεῖναι ἑκατέρῳ ἡμέραν παρ᾽ ἡμέραν stayed day **for** day with each, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.46}}; hence of alternation, ποιεῖσθαι ἁγνείας καὶ θυσίας δύο π. δύο, of four priests acting two and two alternately, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1198.12 (1st c.BC)}}; τοῦ καθημερινοῦ ἢ μίαν π. μίαν (i.e. ἡμέραν) [πυρετοῦ] quotidian or **tertian** fever, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. work] 956.3 (3rd c.AD): sometimes without doubling of the Noun, παρ᾽ ἡμέρην, opposed to καθ᾽ ἡμέρην}}**tertian**, opposed to quotidian, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 1.12}}; καθ᾽ ἡμέραν, παρ᾽ ἡμέραν, π. δύο, π. τρεῖς every day, **every second** day, every third (fourth) day, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.18.13}}; π. μίαν every second day, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.110.4}}; παρ᾽ ἐνιαυτόν every second year, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cleomenes” 15}}; παρ᾽ ἔτος year and year about, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 757a7}}; every second year, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.15.2}}; π. μέρος by turns (see. μέρος{{Refs 1st c.AD+|II. 2); ὁ ἀνὰ μέρος παρ᾽ ἓξ μῆνας ὑπὲρ γῆν τε καὶ ὑπὸ γῆν γινόμενος Ἄδωνις 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 28}}; π. μῆνα τρίτον every third month, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 582b4, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.942e}}; but π. τρία [ἔτεα] probably every fourth year, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 5(2).422 (from Phigalea), compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epict.”}}[same place]; ἕνα παρ᾽ ἕνα παραλειπτέον every second one, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 1.18}}; ἕνα π. δύο (τρεῖς) every third (fourth) one, {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; παρὰ δ᾽ ἄλλαν ἄλλα μοῖρα διώκει now one now another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 611.}}
**C.I.10** **precisely at the moment of**, παρ᾽ αὐτὰ τἀδικήματα **flagrante delicto**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.13, 21.26; ἀποδώσω π. τὸν εὔθυνον τὸ καθῆκον “IG” 12.188.31}}; π. τοιοῦτον καιρόν, π. τὰς χρείας, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.41,46}}; π. τὰ δεινά **in the midst of** danger, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 63; π. τὴν πρώτην γένεσιν 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.10b}}; π. τὴν πρώτην (i.e. ἐπίθεσιν) **at** the first attack, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 9.2; π. γε τὴν πρώτην ὁρμήν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 14.10.}}
**C.I.10.b** distributively, whether of Time, π. τὰ ἑβδομήκοντα ἔτεα **in each** complete period of seventy years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; ἐν ταῖς ὁδοιπορίαις π. στάδια διακόσια.. τοῖς ἑκατὸν σταδίοις διήνεγκαν ἀλλήλων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.18}}; πὰρ Ϝέτος **each** year, **every** year, {{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.101; π. τὸν ἐνιαυτὸν ἕκαστον “IG” 12(7).5.14 (from Amorgos)}}; παρ᾽ ἆμάρ τε καὶ νύκτα day and night, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 7}}; or more generally, πὰρ τὰν ἐλαίαν **in respect of each** olive plant, {{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.122}}; παρ᾽ ἡμέραν αἱ ἀμίαι πολὺ ἐπιδήλως αὐξάνονται **from** day **to** day, **per** day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 571a21; τὸ παρ᾽ ἑκάστην βάσιν γινόμενον μικρὸν πολὺ γίνεται π. πολλάς [prev. author] “Pr.” 881b26; ἡ παρ᾽ ἡμέραν χάρις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.70; τὸ παρ᾽ ἑκάστην ἡμέραν ἡδύ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 705a.}}
**C.I.10.c** παρ᾽ ἆμαρ **on** (this) day, **to-day**, τὸ μὲν πὰρ ἆμαρ, τὸ δέ.. **to**-day and to-morrow, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.63}}; but παρ᾽ ἦμαρ **to**-morrow, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1455 (Lyric poetry).}}
**C.I.10.d** **throughout** a period of time, π. τὴν ζόην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.46; π. τὸν βίον ἅπαντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 733a; π. πάντα τὸν χρόνον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.10}}; also more loosely, **during**, π. τὴν πόσιν **while** they were drinking, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. ; π. τὸν πότον 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.156; π. τὴν κύλικα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 24}}; π. δεῖπνον or π. τὸ δεῖπνον, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.737a,674f.}}
**C.II** **along**, ὄνος παρ᾽ ἄρουραν ἰών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.558; βῆ δὲ θέειν π. τεῖχος 12.352; π. ῥόον Ὠκεανοῖο ᾔομεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.21; ἔπλεον π. τὴν ἤπειρον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.193; π. πᾶσαν τὴν ὁδόν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.148}}; ὀρθὴν παρ᾽ οἶμον.. τύμβον κατόψει straight **along** the road, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 835; παρ᾽ ὅλην τὴν φάραγγα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.30.3; παρ᾽ αὐτὴν τὴν χαράδραν παραπορευομένων [prev. work] 9; for παραβάλλειν π., see above with 1.4b.}}
**C.II.2** **strictly according to, without deviating from**, εἶμι π. στάθμην ὀρθὴν ὁδόν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 945, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 474.5}}; ὠμοί τε δούλοις πάντα καὶ π. στάθμην, i.e. too strict, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1045}}; π. τὸν λόγον ὃν ἀποφέρουσιν.. ἐπιδείξω I will prove to you **strictly according to** the accounts which they themselves submit, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.34.}}
**C.III** **past, beyond**, παρὰ σκοπιὴν καὶ ἐρινεὸν ἠνεμόεντα.. ἐσσεύοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.145, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.172, 24.12; βῆ δὲ π. Κρουνούς “hymnus ad Apollinem” 425}}; π. τὴν Βαβυλῶνα παριέναι pass **by** Babylon, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.29}}; παρ᾽ αὐτὴν τὴν χύτραν ἄκραν ὁρῶντες looking **over the edge of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 390.}}
**C.III.2** _metaphorically_, **over and above, in addition to**, οὐκ ἔστι π. ταῦτ᾽ ἄλλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 698; π. ταῦτα πάντα ἕτερόν τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 74a, compare “R.” 337d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.139, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.5}}; ἑκὼν ἐπόνει π. τοὺς ἄλλους **more than** the others, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ages.” 5.3, compare “Mem.” 4.4.1, “Oec.” 20.16; ἃ τῷ ῥαψῳδῷ προσήκει καὶ σκοπεῖσθαι καὶ διακρίνειν π. τοὺς ἄλλους ἀνθρώπους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 539e.}}
**C.III.3** _metaphorically_, **in excess over**, πὰρ δύναμιν **beyond** one's strength, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.787, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Lycophrone” 16, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1423b29; π. τὴν δ. [prev. author] “Po.” 1451b38.}}
**C.III.4** _metaphorically_, **in transgression** or **violation of**, π. μοῖραν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.509; π. μοῖραν Δίος 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 14.10}}; παρ᾽ αἶσαν, παρὰ δίκαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.13, “O.” 2.16, etc.; π. τὸ δίκαιον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.90, etc.}}; π. τὰς σπονδάς, τὸν νόμον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.67, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.14; π. φύσιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.17, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 747b}}; π. τὴν στήλην probably in {{Refs|“IG” 12.45.20}}; π. καιρόν **out of** season, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.24, etc.}}; π. γνώμαν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 12.10, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 454}}; π. δόξαν, π. τὸ δοκοῦν ἡμῖν, π. λόγον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.93, 1.84, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.38.5}}; παρ᾽ ἐλπίδα or ἐλπίδας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 899, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 392, etc.}}; πὰρ μέλος **out of** tune, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.69; π. τὴν ἀξίαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.77, etc.}}; π. τὸ εἰωθός, τὸ καθεστηκός, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.17, 1.98.}}
**C.III.5** π. τοσοῦτον ἦλθε κινδύνου, ={παρῆλθε τοσοῦτον κινδύνου}, **passed over** so much ground within the sphere of danger, i.e. incurred such imminent peril, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.49, compare 7.2}}; in such phrases the tmesis was forgotten, and the _accusative_ came to be governed by παρά, which thus came to mean '**by** such and such a margin', '**with** so much **to spare**', ἐνίκησαν π. πολύ, ἡσσηθέντες π. πολύ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.29, 2.89, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36a}}; παρὰ δ᾽ ὀλίγον ἀπέφυγες **only just**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 870 (Lyric poetry); παρ᾽ ὀλίγον ἢ διέφευγον ἢ ἀπώλλυντο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.71}}; δεινότατον π. πολύ **by** far, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 445}}; παρ᾽ ὅσον **quatenus**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nec.” 17, etc.}}; π. δύο ψήφους ἀπέφυγε **by** two votes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 28, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.205; π. τέτταρας ψήφους μετέσχε τῆς πόλεως 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.37}}; π. τοσοῦτον ἐγένετο αὐτῷ μὴ περιπεσεῖν **by** so much (= little) he missed falling in with.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.33; π. πέντε ναῦς πλέον ἀνδρὶ ἑκάστῳ ἢ τρεῖς ὀβολοὶ ὡμολογήθησαν [prev. work] 29}}; οὐ π. μικρὸν ἐποίησαν they made no little **difference**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.59.}}
**C.III.5.b** in phrases like{π. τοσοῦτον ἦλθε κινδύνου, τοσοῦτον} was sometimes understood of the interval **from** danger, etc., and παρά came to mean '**by** so much **short of**' (τὸ π. μικρὸν ὥσπερ οὐδὲν ἀπέχειν δοκεῖ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 197a29)}}, **within** such and such **a distance of**, so **near to**, τὴν Ἠϊόνα π. νύκτα ἐγένετο (i.e. αὐτῷ) λαβεῖν he was **within** one night of taking {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.106}}; π. μικρὸν ἦλθον ἀποθανεῖν I came **within** a little **of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.22, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.43.7, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 39}}; παρ᾽ ἐλάχιστον ἦλθε.. ἀφελέσθαι was **within** an ace **of** taking away, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.76}}; παρ᾽ οὐδὲν μὲν ἦλθον ἀποκτεῖναι (were **within** a mere nothing, **within** an ace **of** killing him), ἐξεκήρυξαν δ᾽ ἐκ πόλεως {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.258, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 14, “Alex.” 62}}; π. τοσοῦτον ἦλθε διαφυγεῖν so **near** he came to escaping, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 4; παρὰ ἓν πάλαισμα ἔδραμε νικᾶν Ὀλυμπιάδα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.33; παρ᾽ οὐδὲν ἐλθόντες τοῦ ἀποβαλεῖν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.45.14, compare 2.55.4, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.42: hence without ἐγένετο or ἐλθεῖν, π. μίαν μονάδα (less)}} **by** one, i.e. **less** one, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 1.8}}; τεσσαράκοντα π. μίαν, ={{NT|39, NT.2Cor.11.24}}; παρ᾽ ἕνα τοσοῦτοι the same number **less** one, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 9}}; σύ μοι παρ᾽ ἕνα ἥκεις ἄγων you have brought me one **too few**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 4; δύναται π. δύο συλλαβὰς εἶναι τὸ καταληκτόν 7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 4.2}}; τὰ ὁλοκόττινα ηὑρέθησαν π. ἑπτὰ κεράτια seven carats **short,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PMasp. 70.2 (6th c.AD)}}; πάντες παρ᾽ ἕνα, πάντες παρ᾽ ὀλίγους, all **save** one (a few), {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 20, “Ant.” 5; ἔτη δύο π. ἡμέρας δύο “IG” 5(1).801 (from Laconia)}}; of one Μάρκος, θηρίον εἶ π. γράμμα you are a bear (ἄρκος) **all but** a letter, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.231 (2nd c.AD: Ammianus Epigrammaticus)}}; ὡς π. τι καὶ τὰς ὄψεις ἀφανίσαι so that he **all but** (literal **less** something) lost his sight, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 228.6}}; π. τι βυθίζεσθαι variant in {{NT|NT.Luke.5.7}}; τὸ π. τοῦτο the figure **less** that, i.e. the remainder or **difference,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 99.10 (2nd c.BC),compare “POxy.” 264.4 (1st c.AD), “PAmh.” 2.148.5 (5th c.AD)}}; hence of any **difference** whether of excess or defect, οὐδὲν π. τοῦτο ποιούμενοι τοὺς.. Λευκανούς τε καὶ τοὺς.. Σαυνίτας making no **difference** between.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.1.3, compare 14.5.11, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.24c.}}
**C.III.6** hence of the margin **by** which anything increases or decreases, and so of the cause **according to** which anything comes into existence or varies, τὸ εὖ π. μικρὸν διὰ πολλῶν ἀριθμῶν γίνεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Polyclitus Philosophus 2 (compare μικρός III. 5 c)}}; διαφέρει π. τὰς τῶν παθημάτων ἐναντιώσεις **according to**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 486b5; μεταπίπτει π. τὰ κλίματα 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 5.29, compare 11.5, +others}}; π. τὰ πράγματα conjecture in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Apollodorus Carystius Comicus 11.}}
**C.III.7** more generally of the margin **by** which an event occurs, i.e. of the necessary and sufficient cause or motive (τὸ μὴ π. τοῦτο γίνεσθαι τότε λέγομεν, ὅταν ἀναιρεθέντος τούτου μηδὲν ἧττον περαίνηται ὁ συλλογισμός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 65b6, compare 48a24, +others), κεινὰν π. δίαιταν}} **just for the sake of** unsatisfying food, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.65}}; ἕκαστος οὐ π. τὴν ἑαυτοῦ ἀμέλειαν οἴεται βλάψειν each thinks that his own negligence will not **suffice to cause** injury, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.48}}; π. τὴν αὑτοῦ ἁμαρτίαν **all through** his own fault, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.5, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.52, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.11, 18.232}}; πολλὰ.. ἐστιν αἴτια τούτων, καὶ οὐ παρ᾽ ἓν οὐδὲ δύ᾽ εἰς τοῦτο τὰ πράγματ᾽ ἀφῖκται not **from** one or two causes **only**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.2}}; οὐ π. τοῦτο οὐκ ἔστι **it does** not **follow that** it is not.., {{NT|NT.1Cor.12.15}}; π. τὸ τὴν ἀρίθμησιν ποιήσασθαι ἐξ ἑτοίμου τοὺς ἐργώνας οὐκ ὀλίγα χρήματα περιεποίησε τῇ πόλει **by the simple fact of** prompt payment, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IPE” 12.32 B 35 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC)}}; οὐδὲν ἂν παρ᾽ ἕνα ἄνθρωπον ἐγένετο τούτων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 63, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.103.2, 18.28.6, +others}}; οὐδεὶς παρ᾽ ἑαυτόν ἐστι βασιλεύς **thanks to** himself **alone**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Aristeas 224; παρ᾽ αὑτὸν ἀτυχεῖ 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.24.2, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.16 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; παρ᾽ ἡμᾶς ἡ τῶν ἀγαθῶν ἀπόστασις 5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 25p.477M.}}; εἶναι π. τοῦτο σωτηρίαν τε πόλει καὶ τοὐναντίον, i.e. **on** this **depends**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 715d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 1.5, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 36.5; π. μίαν ἡμέραν καὶ ἓν πρᾶγμα καὶ ἀπόλλυται προκοπὴ καὶ σῴζεται 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 51.2}}; π. τὸ Ἕλληνά με εἶναι **just because** I am a Greek, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 7.13 (2nd c.BC)}}; π. τὸ ἀγαπᾶν αὐτὸν αὐτήν {{LXX|LXX.Gen.29.20, cf. LXX.Exo.14.11}}; later more loosely, **because of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.158 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 6.24, etc.}}; οὐδὲν π. σὲ γέγονε it is no fault of yours, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 243.6 (2nd c.AD),compare “POxy.” 1420.7 (2nd c.AD).}}
**C.III.8** of a limit of possibility, εἴπερ ἐνεδέχετο π. τοὺς παρόντας καιρούς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.239}}; πεῖσαι τό γε παρ᾽ αὑτόν to persuade (the judges) **so far as in** you **lies**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.2.20}}; οἴμωζε παρ᾽ ἐμέ **as far as** I **am concerned, for all** I **care**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 846.}}
**D** POSITION: παρά may follow its _substantive_ in all three cases, but then becomes by anastrophe πάρα: when the _ultima _ is elided, the practice varies, τῇσι παρ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.400; but Ἡφαίστοιο πάρ᾽ [prev. work] 191.}}
**E** παρά _absolutely_, as _adverb_., **near, together**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.611, +others, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 201 (Lyric poetry).}}
**F** πάρα (with anastrophe) stands for πάρεστι and πάρεισι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.174, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 454, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 167, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.42, +others, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 285, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 862, etc.}}
**G** IN COMPOSITION (joined with other words),
**G.I** **alongside of, beside**, of rest, παράκειμαι, παράλληλοι, παρέζομαι, πάρειμι (εἰμί), παρίστημ; of motion, παραπλέω, πάρειμι (εἶμι).
**G.II** **to the side of, to**, παραδίδωμι, παρέχω.
**G.III** **to one side of, by, past**, παρέρχομαι, παροίχομαι, παραπέμπω, παρακμάζω, παρατρέχω.
**G.IV** _metaphorically_,
**G.IV.1** **aside** or **beyond**, i.e. **amiss, wrong**, παραβαίνω, παράγω, παροράω, παρορκέω, παρακούω, παραγιγνώσκω.
**G.IV.2** of comparison, as in παραβάλλω, παρατίθημι.
**G.IV.3** of alteration or change, as in παραλλάσσω, παραπείθω, παραπλάσσω, παρατεκταίνω, παραυδάω, παράφημι.
**G.IV.4** of a side-issue, παραπόλλυμι. (Cogn. with Gothic _faúr_ 'along', Latin _por_-.)

3845:
** παραβαίνω**,
_Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ παρέβασκε (see. below): _accusative_ _plural_ _present_ _participle_ παρβεῶντας {{Refs|“Abh.Berl.Akad.” 1925(5).21 (from Cyrene) is probably from a byform Παραβάω:}}_future_ -βήσομαι: _perfect_ -βέβηκ; _participle_ -βεβώς, _Epic dialect_ -βεβᾰώς: _perfect_ _passive_ -βέβασμαι (see. below 11.1): _aorist 2_ παρέβην: _aorist_ _passive_ παρεβάθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.23}}:—**go by the side of**, and in _perfect_, **stand beside**, twice in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, with _dative_, of one **standing beside** the warrior in the chariot (compare παραβάτης), Ἕκτορι παρβεβαώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.522}}; of two warriors, παρβεβαῶτε.. ἀλλήλοιιν {{Refs|13.708}}; also _imperfect_ παρέβασκε, of the combatant in the chariot,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.104; but παρεβεβήκεέ οἱ ἡνίοχος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.40.}}
**II** **pass beside** or **beyond**, mostly _metaphorically_ (literal π. τὸν ὅρον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHal.” 1.87 (3rd c.BC)), in _transitive_ sense:}}
**II.1** **overstep, transgress**, τὰ νόμιμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.65; δίκην 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 789 (_anapaest meter_); δίκην τὴν δεδικασμένην 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.87; εἴ τι τούτων παραβαίνοιμι “IG” 12.15.42, compare 76.57; θεοῦ νόμον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 230 (Lyric poetry); οὐ τοὺς νόμους μόνον, ἀλλὰ καὶ τὸν καιρὸν τῆς ἀναρρήσεως καὶ τὸν τόπον 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.204}}; θεσμούς, ὅρκους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 331, 332 (both Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.78, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.15; τὰς σπονδάς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 461: with _accusative_ _person_, π. τινὰ δαιμόνων}} **sin against** a god, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.12, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.23}}; οὓς παραβαίνειν αἰσχρόν **disappoint**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Chor. p.80 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus (compare see): _absolutely_, παραβάντες}} **transgressors**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 59 (_anapaest meter_); ὁ παραβαίνων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1325b5}}:—_passive_, **to be transgressed** or **offended against**, σπονδὰς.., ἅς γε ὁ θεὸς.. νομίζει παραβεβάσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.123; νόμῳ παραβαθέντι [prev. author] 3.67; ἐὰν καὶ ὁτιοῦν παραβαθῇ [prev. author] 4.23; παραβεβασμένοις ὅρκοις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 17.12}}; παραβαινομένων _absolutely_, **as offences were committed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45.}}
**II.1.b** with _preposition_, π. παρὰ τὴν συγγραφήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“AJA” 16.13 (Sardes, 4th-3rd c.BC).}}
**II.1.c** with _genitive_, **go aside from**, τῆς ἀληθείας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 271b8.}}
**II.2** **pass over, omit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 499 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.211, Aristeas 297.}}
**II.3** **let pass**, καιρούς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.36.}}
**II.4** οὔ με παρέβα φάσμα it **escaped** me not, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 704 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** **pass on**, π. εἰς ἀπέχθειαν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 38.12.3 (sed to be read προβῆναι).}}
**IV** **come forward**, especially of the Comedy texts parabasis (see. παραβασις),π. πρὸς τὸ θέατρον **step forward** to address the spectators, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 629, “Eq.” 508, “Pax” 735; also οὐκ ἂν παρέβην εἰς λέξιν τοιάνδ᾽ ἐπῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 92.2: similarly, _metaphorically_, δοκεῖν παραβεβηκέναι τῇ πρώτῃ σκηνῇ 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.523 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**V** in _middle_, with _accusative_ _person_, **commit an offence against**, Chor.p.68 {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}

3846:
** παραβάλλω**,
_future_ -βᾰλῶ: _aorist 2_ παρέβᾰλον: _perfect_ -βέβληκα:— **throw beside** or **by, throw to** one, as fodder to horses, παρὰ δέ σφισι βάλλετ᾽ ἐδωδήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.504, compare 5.369; πὰρ δ᾽ ἔβαλον ζειάς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.41; π. [τοῖς ἵπποις] ἀμβροσίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 247e; π. τοὺς ἀνθρώπους τοῖς ὄχλοις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 38.17.2}}; πυρὶ φρύγανα π. **add** fuel to the flame, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.18.5, compare 2.18.12:}} — _passive_, παραβληθῆναι [τοῖς θηρίοις] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 59.10}}; τάριχος.. ἀπόνως παραβεβλημένον **thrown** carelessly **before people**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 333:}} —_middle_, μάζας ἐπὶ κάλαμον παραβαλλόμενοι **ordering** them **to be served up**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372b.}}
**b** **throw in**, φακέλους ἐς τὸ μεταξύ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.77, compare 6.99.}}
**2** **hold out to** one **as a bait**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 11.2.}}
**3** **cast in one's teeth**, τινί τι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.189}}; **object, offer in rejoinder**, τῷ πρώτῳ -βληθήσεται τοιοῦτος λόγος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.95 W.}}
**II** **expose**, παρέβαλέν τ᾽ ἐμὲ παρὰ γένος ἀνόσιον **put** me **in** their **power**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 333 (Lyric poetry); τῇ τύχῃ.. αὑτὸν π. 4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 6 (variant for{προ-})}}]; ἂν δ᾽ ἀληθινὸν σαυτὸν παραβάλλῃς if **you present, show** yourself.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 26}}:—frequently in _middle_, **expose oneself** or **what is one's own to hazard** or **danger**, αἰὲν ἐμὴν ψυχὴν παραβαλλόμενος πολεμίζειν **risking** it in war, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.322}}; π. τὰ τέκνα **risk the lives of one's** children, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10. ; παῖδας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.44}}; πλείω παραβαλλόμενοι **having** greater **interests at stake**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.65; οὐκ ἴσα π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.11:}}_perfect_ _passive_ in _middle_ sense, Λακεδαιμονίοις πλεῖστον δὴ παραβεβλημένοι **having risked** far the most upon them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.113; also τὸν κίνδυνον τῶν σωμάτων παραβαλλομένους [prev. author] 3.14}}; **venture**, πρὸς τὴν θάλατταν ὅταν -βάλωνται {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.37.9; π. καὶ τολμᾶν [prev. author] 18.53.2: with _dative_, π. τοῖς ὅλοις [prev. author] 2.26.6; τῷ βίῳ “IG” 12(3).1286.22 (from Astypalaea)}}: with _infinitive_, **venture** to do, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 8:}} —_passive_, παραβεβλημένον τι εἰπεῖν make an **unguarded** statement, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 4.42.}}
**II.b** in wagering, **deposit** one's stake, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 44.}}
**II.2** _passive_, with _dative_, **to be given up to**, πόρναισι καὶ κύβοισι παραβεβλημένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 243.}}
**III** **set beside** or **parallel with**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 668a17 (_passive_),compare “Rh.” 1419b35}}; Εὔβοια τῇ ἠπείρῳ παραβεβλημένη **lying parallel with**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.1.22: hence}}
**III.2** **compare** one with another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.34, etc.; τι παρά τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 472c}}; π. [ἵππον] ἵππῳ **match** one **against** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 9.8:}} —in _middle_, παραβάλλομαί σοι (i.e. ὄρνιθι) θρήνους I **set my** songs **against**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1094 (Lyric poetry): _absolutely_, παραβαλλόμεναι}} **vying with one another**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Andr.” 289 (Lyric poetry); [ἀφορμὰς] αἷς οὔτε Ἁρμόδιος παραβεβλήσεται 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.34}}:—frequently in _passive_, π. τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.198; πρός τι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.5; παρά τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 475e}}; ἀπάτα δ᾽ ἀπάταις παραβαλλομένα one piece of treachery **set against** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 231 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III.3** **bring alongside**, in _middle_, τὴν ἄκατον παραβάλλου **bring your** boat **alongside, heave to**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 762; ἐφόλκιον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 73; also π. τὼ κωπίω 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 269: _absolutely_, παραβαλοῦ [prev. work] 180: _metaphorically_, παραβάλλου λοιδορῶν}} **avast** with your abuse! {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.711d.}}
**IV** **throw, turn, bend sideways**, ὄμμα π. θύννου δίκην **cast** it **askance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 308; τὸν ὀφθαλμὸν παράβαλλ᾽ ἐς Καρίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 173; τὠφθαλμὼ παραβάλλεις [prev. author] “Nu.” 362 (referred to by 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 221b); π. τὸ ἕτερον οὖς πλάγιον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.32}}; π. τὰ ὦτα **apply** one's ears to listen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 531a; παραβαλὼν τὴν κεφαλήν [prev. author] “Phd.” 103a}}; Ἡρακλεῖ στόμα π. **lend** one's mouth to Heracles, i.e. join in his praise, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.87 (variant{περιβ-})}}]; π. τοὺς γομφίους **lay to** one's grinders, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 34}}; π. τὸ θύριον τοῦ λόγου, _metaphorically_, **put to** the door.., **close** it, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.94 of.}}
**V** **deposit with** one, **entrust to** him, τινί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.154.}}
**VI** in _middle_, **deceive, betray**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.108, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.133, 5th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Comicus 30}} (_active_ in the same sense, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; compare παραβαλλέταιρος).
**VII** Geometry texts, π. παρά.. **apply** a figure to a finite line, παραλληλόγραμμον π. παρὰ εὐθεῖαν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 6.27, compare 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἰσορροπιῶν” 2.1.}}
**VII.2** since to **apply** an area **xy** to a line of length **x** is to divide **xy** by **x**, π. = **divide**, τι παρά τι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus 5.10, +others; compare παρά C. 1.4c.}}
**B** _intransitive_, **come near, approach**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 203b, “PPetr.” 3p.102 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; **enter**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1331a34}}; π. ἀλλήλοις **meet** one another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 556c}}; _falsa lectio_ for{περιβάλῃ},{{Refs|[prev. work] 499b; παρέβαλεν Ἀναξιμένει τῷ ῥήτορι}}**was a pupil of** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.846f.}}
**B.II** **go by sea, cross over**, παρέβαλε νηυσὶ ἰθὺ Σκιάθου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.179, cf. Philipp. cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.16, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 836a29}}; of ships, ναῦς Πελοποννησίων ἐς Ἰωνίαν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.32.}}
**B.III** **come alongside, bring to**, περὶ Ῥόδον παραβαλόντος ναυτικοῦ στόλου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 763a31}}; παραβαλόντες τῇ πεντήρει **having come alongside of her**, in a sea-fight, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.2.12, compare 1.22.9}}: generally, **come to land**, of quails, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 597b15}}:—in _middle_, **put in**, πρός τινας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 6.16.}}
**B.IV** _metaphorically_, **direct one's course towards**, εἰς ἡδονάς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1153b34.}}
**B.V** Astrology texts, **to be in the same right ascension as**, with _dative_, {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 1.113, 5(1).188.}}

3847:
** παρά-βᾰσις**,
_Epic dialect_ **παραίβ-**, εως, ἡ, **going aside, escape**, παραίβασις ἔσσετ᾽ ὀλέθρου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.832}}; **deviation**, variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1307b33, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.649b (_plural_)}}; **digression**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.2, 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 12.5 (_plural_).}}
**2** of the action of **walking**, π. καὶ παράλλαξις σκελῶν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 6.}}
**3** **transition, passage**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1012.31.}}
**II** **overstepping**, ὅρων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.122e; ἔθους 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 12.8.9; τοῦ πατρίου νόμου 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.8.2; τῶν δικαίων παραβάσεις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Comp. Ages.Pomp.” 1}}: _absolutely_, **transgression**, {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.4Ki.2.24, NT.Gal.3.19, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.209a, 746c, etc.}}; **error, illusion**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 11.7}}; compare παραβασία.
**III** **parabasis**, a part of the Old Comedy, **in which the Chorus came forward** (compare παραβαίνω IV) and addressed the audience in the Poet's name, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.711f, 7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus “Poëm.” 8, Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 733, etc.}}

3848:
** παρα-βάτης** [βᾰ],
_poetry_ and early _Attic dialect_ **παραιβάτης** {{Refs|(“IG” 12.5, etc.)}}, ου, ὁ, (παραβαίνω I) **one who stands beside**: properly **the warrior** or **combatant who stands beside the charioteer**, ἂν δ᾽ ἔβαν ἐν δίφροισι παραιβάται ἡνίοχοί τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.132, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.29; παραιβάτας ἔστησαν ἐς τάξιν δορός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 677; ἀναλαβεῖν τοὺς παραβάτας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.29, etc.; δύο δ᾽ εἰσὶν ἐπὶ τῷ ἅρματι π. πρὸς ἡνιόχῳ 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.52: = _Attic dialect_ ἀποβάται, _accusative_ to 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.73}}; _feminine_ **παραιβάτις**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.754.}}
**2** light troops (**velites**) who **ran beside** the cavalry, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 12.}}
**II** (παραβαίνω II.1)**transgressor**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 553}} (Lyric poetry, in _poetry_ form παρβάτης),{{NT+1st c.AD+|compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Symmachus LXX.Psa.16(17).4; π. θεῶν 1st-2nd c.AD: Polemo Sophista cited in 4th-5th c.AD: Macrobius Grammaticus “Sat.” 5.19.29; π. νόμου NT.Rom.2.25.}}

3849:
** παρα-βῐάζομαι**,
**do** a thing **by force against nature** or **law**, {{LXX|LXX.Deu.1.43}}; **use violence**, περὶ τῶν τοιούτων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 24.8.3.}}
**II** with _accusative_, π. τὸν χάρακα **force** the palisade, {{Refs|[prev. author] 21.27.7}}; π. τινά **constrain, compel** him, {{LXX+NT|LXX.4Ki.2.17, +others, NT.Luke.24.29, NT.Act.16.15}}; of arguments or explanations, τὸ ἀδύνατον π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.36U., “Nat.” 107 G.}}; μύθους π. καὶ διαστρέφειν **to do** them **violence**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.19f, compare “Lyc.” 6}}; **constrain**, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 19.2 (_passive_)}}:—_active_ in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.287.}}

3850:
** παραβολ-ή**, ἡ,
**juxtaposition, comparison**, τῶν βίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 33b; π. καὶ σύγκρισις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.2.2}}; ἐν παραβολῇ by **juxtaposition**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 104a28, compare 157a14; ἐκ παραβολῆς [prev. author] “Rh.” 1420a4.}}
**2** **comparison, illustration, analogy**, τὴν π. ἀπρεπῆ πεποιῆσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.227}}; π. δὲ τὰ Σωκρατικά (distinguished from λόγος, **apologue**) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1393b3; ἐκ τῶν θηρίων ποιεῖσθαι τὴν π. [prev. author] “Pol.” 1264b4.}}
**3** **NT, parable,** {{NT|NT.Mark.12.1, +others}}; **type, Ep. Hebrew **9.9, 11.19.
**4** **by-word, proverb**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Eze.18.2, NT.Luke.4.23}}; in bad sense, εἰς π. ἐν τοῖς ἔθνεσι {{LXX|LXX.Psa.43(44).14, cf. LXX.Wis.5.3.}}
**5** **objection** to an argument, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.5 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**II** **moving side by side**, ἐκ παραβολῆς [νεῶν] μάχεσθαι to fight a sea-fight **broadside to broadside**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.2.13, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.60.}}
**III** **sidelong direction, obliquity**, διὰ πολλῶν ἑλιγμῶν καὶ π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 22.}}
**IV** **venture**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 27.17, variant in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.131.}}
**V** Astron., **conjunction**, παραβολαὶ ἀλλήλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 40c, compare 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Timaeum commentarii” 3.146 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.1.5, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 9.4}}: also _falsa lectio_ for{περιβολή}, τοῦ ἡλίου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Maximus Tyrius Sophista 17.9.}}
**VI** in Mathematics texts, **division**, opposed to multiplication, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus 4.22}}; **quotient**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 10: hence}}**section produced by division** of a line, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 2.27.}}
**VII** Geometry texts, **application**, π. τῶν χωρίων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in primum Euclidis librum commentarius” p.419 F.}}; τὰ ἐκ τῆς π. γενηθέντα σημεῖα, of the foci of an ellipse or hyperbola, points found by **application** of an area to the axis, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 3.45, compare 48.}}
**VII.2** **parabola**, because the square on the ordinate is equal to a rectangle whose height is equal to the abscissa **applied to** the parameter, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.11.}}
**VIII** ={παράβολον} (see. παράβολος{{Refs 4th c.BC+|III. 1), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1348b13 (variants παράβολον, -βόλιον), “OGI” 41.5 (Samos, 3rd c.BC, _plural_). “PPetr.” 3p.232 (3rd c.BC, _plural_).}}

3852:
** παραγγ-ελία**, ἡ,
**command** or **order** issued to soldiers, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.4, NT.Act.16.24}}; τόπος ἐπιτηδειότατος εἰς π. for giving the **word of command**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.27.1}}: generally, **order** issued by an authority, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PAmh.” 2.68.63 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**II** **summoning one's partisans** to support one in a suit at law, **exertion of influence**, σπουδὴ καὶ π., συγγνώμη καὶ π., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.1, 283.}}
**II.2** **summons** to appear in court, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 484.18 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**II.3** **canvassing for public office**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 15, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.21, etc.}}
**III** **set of rules** or **precepts**, ὑπὸ παραγγελίαν πίπτειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1104a7; παραδόσεις καὶ π. 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.78 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus (_plural_); μεθοδικὴ π. 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 2.33}}; **instruction, precept, advice**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Jusj.”, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.36, 15.10; τὸ τέλος τῆς π. ἐστὶν ἀγάπη NT.1Tim.1.5}}; τεχνίτης π. λογικῆς, of **rules** of literary composition, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 26.1.}}

3853:
** παραγγ-έλλω**,
_future_ -ελῶ: _aorist 2_ _infinitive_ _passive_ παραγγελῆναι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHamb.” 25.9 (3rd c.BC)}}:—**pass on** or **transmit** a message, σέλας παραγγείλασα Μακίστου σκοπαῖς (nisi to be read παρηγγάρευσε) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 289, compare 294, 316; μνήμην παραγγέλλοντες ὧν ἐκύρσατε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1173.}}
**II** **give orders, give the word of command**, especially of a general, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 469, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.147, etc.; π. τινὶ κτείνειν [prev. author] 3.147, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116c, etc.: with _dative_ omitted, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.70, etc.}}; τισὶ ὅπως with _future_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 415b}}; π. ὅπως ἂν.. **give orders** to the end that.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 59e}}: with _accusative_ _of things_ only, **order**, π. παρασκευὴν σίτου **order** corn to be supplied, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.25; σιτία 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.43: with _accusative_ _cognate_, π. παράγγελμα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.17, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 14; παραγγελίᾳ π. NT.Act.5.28:—}}_passive_, τὰ παραγγελλόμενα **orders**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1298a18; ἐς τὰ π. ἰέναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.121, 3.55; κατὰ τὰ παρηγγελμένα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.8; παρηγγέλλετο ἐπ᾽ αὐτὸν στρατεία 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.65, compare 90.}}
**II.2** **recommend, exhort** (not so strong as κελεύω), π. τινὶ πράσσειν τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1178 (Lyric poetry), etc.; τινί τι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 825; τινί τι περί τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.129.}}
**II.3** **summon** to appear, π. παραγίνεσθαι ἐπὶ τὸ κριτήριον {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 3925.3 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.4** of a physician, **prescribe**, τι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 885b27.}}
**II.5** τὰ παρηγγελμένα **the points we have enjoined**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Top.” 153a5; τὰ ὑπὸ [λόγου] παραγγελθέντα [prev. author] “Rh. Al.” 1420b26.}}
**III** **encourage, cheer on**, with _accusative_, ἵππους {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 998}}; π. εἰς ὅπλα **call** to arms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.13.}}
**IV** **summon to one's help**, especially in politics, **summon one's partisans, form a cabal**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.4 (variant{περιήγγελκεν}),compare “Prooem.” 55, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.41.}}]
**IV.2** π. τὴν ἀρχήν **canvass for** office, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.61, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 5, etc.: _absolutely_, π. εἰς ὑπατείαν}} **to be candidate for**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Caes.” 13, compare “Cat.Mi.” 8; ἐς δημαρχίαν 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.21.}}
**IV.3** ἐκ μειρακίων π. εἰς ἄνδρα **claim** one's majority, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.10.}}

3854:
** παραγίγνομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ and later Gr. **παρα-γίνομαι** [ῑ], _aorist_ _passive_ παρεγενήθην {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.99.2, etc.}}:—**to be beside, by** or **near**: with _dative_ _person_ et _of things_, καί σφιν παρεγίγνετο δαιτί **attended** them **at** the banquet, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.173: with _dative_ _person_ only, Σοφοκλεῖ π. ἐρωτωμένῳ}} **was by** him when he was asked, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 329b, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.17:}} with _dative_ _of things_ only, π. τῇ μάχῃ **to be present at**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 153c; τῇ συνουσίᾳ [prev. author] “Smp.” 172c, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.109; also π. ἐν τοῖς ἀγῶσι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.52}}; ἐν τοιοῖσδε λόγοις, ἐν τῇ συνουσίᾳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 337a, “Smp.” 173b: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.5.}}
**2** π. τινί **come to one's side, stand by, second**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 429, 436, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.32; μάρτυρες.. τοῖσι θανοῦσιν π. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 319 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἐπί τινα against one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.95}}; μάχῃ.. π. τισί **support** them in battle, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.54: _absolutely_, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 432, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.67; ἄνδρες ἱππῆς -γένεσθε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 242; -γενηθεὶς ἐπάγγελτος “SIG” 708.21 (Istropolis, 2nd c.BC).}}
**3** of things, **to be at hand, accrue** to one, πόλεμος ὅθεν καί τις δύναμις παρεγένετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.15, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.2; φόβοι παραγιγνόμενοί τισι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.34; ἀρετὴ π. οἷς ἂν π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 99e, compare 86d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1099b16; ἀπὸ φυσιολογίας 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.122 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; of scientific learning, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 71a4}}; of virtue, ὅτῳ τρόπῳ παραγίγνεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 71a.}}
**4** π. ἀπό τινος **to be descended** from.., or perhaps **to have a right to attend a sacrifice through descent** from.., {{Refs|“Inscription Cos” 405.}}
**II** **come to**, τινι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 139, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.14, etc.; π. ἐς κώμην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.185}}; π. ἐς τὠυτό **come to** the same point, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.4; ἐς τὸ δέον [prev. author] 1.32; ἐπὶ τὰς ταφάς 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.235}}: _absolutely_, **arrive, come up**, παρεγένοντο αἱ νέες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.95.}}
**II.2** **come to maturity**, of corn, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.193}}; of the horns of oxen, **to be fully grown**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.29.}}
**II.3** **have recourse to**, ἐπὶ τροφὴν καὶ πόμα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.506; ἐπὶ τὸ τῆς ὄνου γάλα [prev. work] 746.}}

3855:
** παρ-άγω**,
_future_ παράξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.19 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}: _perfect_ παραγέωχα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 5.198 (2nd c.BC), παραγείοχα “Stud.Pal.” 22.3 (2nd c.AD)}}:—**lead by** or **past** a place, with _accusative_ loci, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.158, compare 9.47}}; πάραγε πτέρυγας fly **past**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 166 (Lyric poetry); π. θριάμβους 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Μιθριδάτειος” 117, compare “BC” 2.101}}; of a person, ἐν θριάμβῳ παράγεσθαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 55.}}
**2** in Tactics, **march** the men **up from the side, bring them from column into line**, π. τοὺς ἐπὶ κέρως πορευομένους.. εἰς μέτωπον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.22, compare “Cyr.” 2.3.21, “An.” 4.6.6; τὰς [τάξεις] εἰς τὰ πλάγια [prev. work] 3.4.14; ἔξωθεν τῶν κεράτων [prev. work] 3.4.21.}}
**3** **bring round** or **forward**, ἀγκῶνα παρὰ τὸ στῆθος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 2, compare 74}}; **twist round** or **out of place**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in librum de sensu commentarium - pars” 16.19.}}
**4** π. ὑπόχυμα **couch** a cataract, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “Θρασύβουλος - Scripta Minora” 23.}}
**5** **divert**, ὑδραγωγόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 971 (1st-2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **lead aside from the way, mislead**, ἔννυχοι πάραγον κοῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.25; σοφία παράγοισα μύθοις [prev. author] “N.” 7.23; π. τινὰεἰς ἀρκύστατα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 99 codices (Lyric poetry); π. ψεύδεσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 383a; φενακίζειν καὶ π. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.34, compare “PMagd.” 12.7 (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 289.20 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, φόβῳ παρηγόμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 974; λόγοις παράγεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.91}}; ἀπάτῃ π. ὑπό τινων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 34; νέοις παραχθείς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 232.}}
**II.2** **divert from one's course, influence**, Μοίρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.91}}: with _accusative_ _person_ et _genitive_ _of things_, **divert from**, [τινὰ] τοῦ τῆς ῥητορικῆς τέλους Phld.[same place]; **induce, lead to** or **into** a thing, ἔς τι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 478: mostly in bad sense, π. ἐς ἀμπλακίην, ἐς ἀναιδείην, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 404, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 78}}:—_passive_, **to be influenced, persuaded**, οἷοι θυσίαις τε καὶ εὐχωλαῖς παράγεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 365e, compare “Lg.” 885b, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64; λόγῳ παραχθέντες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.8.5}}: with _infinitive_, παρηγμένος μισθοῖς εἰργάσθαι τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 294.}}
**II.3** of things, **lead aside**: hence, **wrest**, π. τοὺς νόμους ἐπί τι **pervert** the laws to this end, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 550d, compare 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.36; οἱ θεοὶ τῶν πονηρῶν ἀνθρώπων τὴν διάνοιαν π. 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 92; π. τὴν ἀλήθειαν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Epistulae” 35:}} —_passive_, τὰ γράμματα παρῆκται, from age, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 6.19.5.}}
**II.4** **avert**, πειθοῖ καὶ λόγῳ τὴν ἀνάγκην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 2.}}
**II.5** **change slightly**, of letters in the derivation of words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 398c, 398d, 400c, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.354c}}: frequently in Grammars in _passive_, **to be derived**, ἀπό.. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1014.58, 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 641.4, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 34.25}}; ἐκ.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Synt.” 111.2}}; παρά with _accusative_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Adv.” 146.10: with _genitive_, τὸ μελιτηρὸν τοῦ τηρεῖν [παραχθέν] [prev. author] “Pron.” 30.17}}: generally, **to be formed**, διὰ τοῦ θεν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Adv.” 184.12; τὴν κτητικὴν διὰ τῆς οι π. [prev. author] “Pron.” 109.6}}; **to be inflected**, ἀντωνυμίαι ὡς ὀνόματα εἰς τὰ γένη καὶ τὰς πτώσεις π.{{Refs|[prev. work] 111.2, compare “Synt.” 110.8}}; ὁ ἀνδριὰς οὐ λέγεται ξύλον, ἀλλὰ παράγεται ξύλινος **is called by a modification**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1033a17.}}
**III** **bring and set beside** others, **bring forward, introduce**, ἐς μέσον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.129; εἰς τὸ μέσον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 713b; εἰς ὑμᾶς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.5}}; π. εἰς τὸν δῆμον **bring before** the people, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.32, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.45}}; εἰς τὸ δικαστήριον **before** the court, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 26.17; παραχθῆναι τὴν γραφήν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.6}}; also, **bring forward** as a witness, etc., τὸν ἥκοντα παρήγαγον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.170}}:—_middle_, μάρτυρα παραγόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 836c.}}
**III.b** **introduce** on the stage, **bring in**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.117d, 6.230b, +others, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.28, probably in Anonymus}} **de Comedy texts (CGF** p.7); οἵους οἱ κωμῳδοδιδάσκαλοι π. ἀγροίκους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1230b19}}: hence, **represent, portray**, τοξότας αὐτοὺς παρήγαγον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 32, compare 14 (_passive_).}}
**III.c** **produce, deliver**, ἐπὶ τὰ χώματα καλαμείαν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 5.198 (2nd c.BC), compare 92.8 (2nd c.BC, _passive_).}}
**III.2** **bring in**, with a notion of secrecy, ἄνδρας π. ἔσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.20}}:—_passive_, **come in stealthily, slip in**, π. γὰρ ἐνέρων δολιόπους ἀρωγὸς εἴσω στέγας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1391 (Lyric poetry)}}; of things, τὸ ὕδωρ ὀρύγμασι καὶ τάφροις εἰς τὸ πεδίον π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 4.}}
**IV** **carry on, protract**, τὴν πρᾶξιν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.65}}; π. τὸν χρόνον **pass** it, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agis” 13, etc.}}; see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus III.}}
**V** **direct, guide**, κῆτος παραγόμενον εὐπειθῶς {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.981a.}}
**VI** **produce, create**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.8.20, etc.}}; τὸ παράγον, opposed to τὸ παραγόμενον, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 7, compare 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 32, etc.}}:—_passive_, ἀπὸ τῶν ἀτελεστέρων τελειότερα παράγεται {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 3.22, compare “Geoponica” 9.1.1.}}
**VII** **draw along**, ἄνωθεν κάτω τὰς χεῖρας (in massage), {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 6.20.8.}}
**B** _intransitive_, **pass by, pass on one's way**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.44, 3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 10.15, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.18.4, etc.; τοῖς παράγουσιν χαίρειν “IPE” 2.378 (from Phanagoria)}}: also with _accusative_, **pass by**, μνήματα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 37.25; κώμην “PTeb.” 17.4 (2nd c.BC).}}
**B.2** **pass away**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.143(144).4, NT.1Cor.7.31}}:—in _passive_, {{NT|NT.1John.2.8, 17.}}
**B.II** **pass along** the coast, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.44.3}}; simply, **go**, εἴσω πάραγε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 188, compare 194, “Sam.” 80, “Pk.” 275.}}
**B.III** **delay** (see. above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus IV), παρῆγον ἐφ᾽ ἱκανὸν χρόνον 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.3; ἐξέκρουε καὶ π. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 23.}}

3856:
** παραδειγ-μᾰτίζω**,
**make an example of**, τινα {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Num.25.4, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.60.7, 29.19.5 (_passive_)}}; **make a show** or **spectacle of,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Matt.1.19; π. ἑαυτόν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.520b.}}
**II** **show by example**, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 153.18 (_passive_).}}

3857:
** πᾰρᾰδεισ-ος**, ὁ
(also **παράδισος** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 463.8 (Crete, 3rd c.BC))}}, **enclosed park** or **pleasure-ground**, Oriental word first used by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus}} always in reference to the **parks** of the Persian kings and nobles; π. μέγας ἀγρίων θηρίων πλήρης {{Refs|“An.” 1.2.7}}; π. δασὺς παντοίων δένδρων{{LXX+4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 2.4.14; τὰ ἐν π. θηρία “Cyr.” 1.3.14; θῆραι.. ἐν περιειργμένοις παραδείσοις “HG” 4.1.15, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.4.1, “AJA” 16.13 (Sardes, 4th c.BC), LXX.Neh.2.8, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 25.}}
**2** generally, **garden, orchard,** {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|PRev.Laws 33.11 (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 33.3 (3rd c.BC), “OGI” 90.15 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC), LXX.Cant.4.13, LXX.Eccl.2.5, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2694b (from Mylasa), “PFay.” 55.7 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**3** **the garden of Eden**, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.2.8.}}
**3.b** **Paradise, the abode of the blessed,** {{NT|NT.Luke.23.43, NT.2Cor.12.4.}}
**3.c** explanation of μακάρων νῆσοι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus ad 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 169.}}
**II** **stupid fellow,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Comica Adespota 1102. (Persian word, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.13, 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}, and Avest. **pairidaēza**- 'enclosure'.)

3858:
** παραδέχομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ **παραδέκομαι**, _future_ **-ξομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 155c}}:—**receive from** another, σῆμα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.178; [Γαῖα] σταγόνας παραδεξαμένη τίκτει θνητούς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 839.4 (_anapaest meter_); τὰ φερόμενα γράμματα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.17, etc.}}; of children, **receive by inheritance**, σοφώτατα νοήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.72; τὴν ἀρχήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.102; π. τὸν πόλεμον παρὰ τοῦ πατρός [prev. work] 18}}; but μάχην π. **take up and continue** the battle, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.40}}; **receive by way of rumour** or **tradition**, π. φήμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 713c; ἀκοήν τινος [prev. author] “Ti.” 23d}}; of magistrates or others, **receive** articles entered in an inventory, etc., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12.91.21, +others, “PHib.” 1.32.4 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; of pupils, **receive lessons from** a master, τοὺς μετὰ πόνου.. παραδεχομένους {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 1.}}
**b** **take over** an office or function, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1199.3, +others (1st c.BC).}}
**2** with _infinitive_, π. τινὶ πράττειν τι **take upon oneself** or **engage** to another to do a thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.38.}}
**3** **admit**, εἰς τὴν πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 394d, 399d, 605b}}; εἰς [τὴν οἰκίαν] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.2; εἰς τοὺς ἀγῶνας 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.178}}; **admit to citizenship**, τῶν περιοίκων τινάς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1303a7}}; **admit** as a pupil, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 304b}}; π. τὸ ἔθνος **admit to friendly relations**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 38.9.8.}}
**4** **admit, allow**, τὴν ἀπαγωγήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.86, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 155c, “Lg.” 935d; π. σκῆψιν 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 7}}; π. τὸν λόγον **accept** the definition, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 162e, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 4a28}}; **recognize as correct, agree to**, συντίμησιν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1119.54 (1st c.BC)}}; τὸ δαπανηθέν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFay.” 125.10 (2nd c.AD).}}
**5** **signify**, κτῆσιν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 171.6.}}
**II** in later writers the _aorist_ παρεδέχθην takes also a _passive_ sense, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 2.21, “Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”; ἀξιῶ παραδεχθῆναί τινα εἰς τοὺς ἐφήβους}}**to be admitted,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 477.24 (2nd c.AD)}}; also, **to be credited** as a set-off, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“BGU” 831.15 (3rd c.AD)}}: so _future_ **-δεχθήσομαι**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PAmh.” 2.86.13 (1st c.AD).}}

3859:
Related to: ** διατρῐβή**, ἡ,
**wearing away,** especially of Time, **way** or **manner of spending,** χρόνου τε διατριβὰς.. ἐφηῦρε.. πεσσοὺς κύβους τε **pastimes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 479.2: hence, _absolutely_}}
**1** **pastime, amusement,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 923, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 219.4, etc.; ἐν συνουσίᾳ τινὶ καὶ δ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.71; γέλωτα καὶ δ. παρέχειν τινί 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.175, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 11; τοῦ συμποσίου δ. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 185; παρέσχε τοῖς κωμικοῖς δ.}}**materiem jocandi,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 4, compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.52b}}; **place of amusement,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 481.10, 3rd c.BC: Bato Comicus 2.4.}}
**2** **serious occupation, study,** etc., τοὺς ἐν φιλοσοφίᾳ καὶ τῇ τοιᾷδε δ. τεθραμμένους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 172c; διατριβὰς ποιεῖσθαι περί τι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.11, compare 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.37; πρός τι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.38; ἐπί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1498; ἡ δ. τὰ πολλὰ ἐν λόγοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 204a.}}
**2.b** **discourse,** τὰς ἐμὰς δ. καὶ τοὺς λόγους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ap.” 37d, compare “Grg.” 484e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.19, etc.; αἱ πολιτικαὶ δ. 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 10.15.}}
**2.c** **short ethical treatise** or **lecture,** δ. βραχέος διανοήματος ἠθικοῦ ἔκτασις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ μεθόδου δεινότητος” 5, compare 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus: title of works by Zeno, Cleanthes, etc.}}
**2.d** **school** of philosophy, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.211d, +others, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 5; Μωυσοῦ καὶ Χριστοῦ 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.579; Ἐπικούρου δ. 2nd c.AD: Numenius Apamensis Platonicus cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 14.5}}; also, **a place of teaching, school,** ἡ ἐν τῷ κήπῳ δ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 217, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.39 M., 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 25, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.350b.}}
**3** **way of life, passing of time,** δ. ἐν ἀγορᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1055; δ. νέων ἐν δικαστηρίοις 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.32}}; ἡ ἐν Σικελίᾳ δ. **stay** there, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 337e}}; ποιεῖσθαι ἐν τῷ ὑγρῷ τὴν δ., ἐν τῇ γῇ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 487a20, “Resp.” 474b26; διατριβὰς μετ᾽ ἀλλήλων διατρίβειν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.147.}}
**4** **place of resort, haunt,** τὰς ἐν Λυκείῳ δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 2a; ᾖα ἐπὶ τὰς συνήθεις δ. [prev. author] “Chrm.” 153a.}}
**II** in bad sense, **waste of time, loss of time, delay,** with or without χρόνου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 751, etc.; δ. ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.164: _plural_, δ. καὶ μελλήσεις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.82}}; χρόνου δ. ἐμποιεῖν, παρέχειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.38, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.13, etc.; ἐμβαλεῖν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 20}}; διατριβὴν ποτῷ ποιεῖν **prolong** a carouse, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 226.4.}}
**III** Rhet., **occasion for dwelling on a subject,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1418a27 (_plural_).}}
**IV** **continuance, permanence,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mete.” 374a12.}}
**V** obscene meaning, ={συνουσία}, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 2.}}

3860:
** παραδίδωμι**
(late **παραδια-δίδω** (δειδ-) {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Tab.Defix.Aud.” 156.8 (Rome, 4th-5th c.AD))}}, **give, hand over to another, transmit**, [παιδίον] τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.117}}; τὰ ἐντεταλμένα, of couriers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.98; καθάπερ λαμπάδα τὸν βίον π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 776b, etc.}}; of sentinels, π. τὸν κώδωνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.135; τὴν ἑωθινὴν φυλακήν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 7; τῷ παιδὶ π. τὴν ἀρχήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.159; τὰ πάτρια τεύχεα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 399 (Lyric poetry)}}; of letters to the person addressed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.17}}; of a purchase to the buyer, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Oec.” 20.28}}; of articles entered in an inventory by magistrates, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 12.324.2, etc.; in Astrology texts, π. τὸ ἔτος 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 100.30, 4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “I.” 4}}; of an argument, π. τινὶ τὸν ἑξῆς λόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 106b}}; π. τὴν προξενίαν **hand** it **down** to one's posterity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.4; τὴν πόλιν εὐδαιμονεστάτην τοῖς ἐπιγιγνομένοις π. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.94, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.36, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372d}}; π. τὴν ἀρετήν **transmit, impart** as a teacher, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Men.” 93c}}: with _infinitive_, παῖδάς σφι παρέδωκε τὴν γλῶσσαν ἐκμαθεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.73; ἣν ἐμῇ μητρὶ παρέδωκεν τρέφειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 64; π. τινὶ τοὺς νέους διδάσκειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 812a, compare “Ti.” 42d, +others}}
**2** **give** a city or person **into another's hands**, τὴν Σάμον π. Συλοσῶντι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.149; ἄλλον ἐς ἄλλην πόλιν π. [prev. author] 5.37}}; especially as a hostage, or to an enemy, **deliver up, surrender**, ἑωυτὸν Κροίσῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.45, compare 3.13, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.86; τὰς ναῦς 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.11, etc.}}: with collateral notion of treachery, **betray**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.51, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.2.1; π. ὅπλα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.28, etc.}}; τύχῃ αὑτὸν π. **commit** oneself to fortune, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.16}}; ταῖς ἡδοναῖς ἑαυτὴν [τὴν ψυχήν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84a; ἑαυτοὺς [ἐπιθυμίαις] [prev. work] 82c}}: without _accusative_, **give way**, ἡδονῇ παραδούς {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 250e.}}
**3** **give up** to justice, etc., ἥντινα μήτε.. παραδοῦναι ἐξῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.42; π. τινὰς τῷ δικαστηρίῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.17; τοῖς ἕνδεκα παρεδόθη 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.17; also π. τινὰ εἰς τὸ δεσμωτήριον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 51.8; δεθέντα εἰς τὸν δῆμον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.3 (_passive_)}}; ἐπὶ κρίσει παρεδέδοτο εἰς τὸν δῆμον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.9}}: with _infinitive_, π. τινὰ θανάτῳ ζημιῶσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.2}}; **give up** a slave **to be examined by torture**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.15, Test. cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.61:}} —_passive_, ἐγκλήματι π. uncertain reading in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 62.27: _metaphorically_, σιωπῇ καὶ λήθῃ παραδοθείς 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 3.}}
**4** **hand down** legends, opinions, etc., **by tradition**, φήμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 16c; παραδεδομένα καὶ μυθώδη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.65; οἱ παραδεδομένοι μῦθοι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1451b24; ὁ π. τρόπος [prev. author] “Pol.” 1313a35}}; οἱ παραδεδομένοι θεοί the **traditional** gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.94; ἡ οἰκία.. ἐγκεκωμιασμένη παραδέδοται ἡμῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 157e}}; δόγματι παραδοθῆναι **to be embodied** in a decree, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 57.20.}}
**4.b** **teach** doctrine, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Luke.1.2, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.124, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 1.8, 3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 37.3, 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 154, 433, 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 6.50}}:—_passive_, ὅταν [τέχνη] παραδιδῶται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.14.2.}}
**II** **grant, bestow**, κῦδός τισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.52}}: in _present_ and _imperfect_, **offer, allow**, αἵρεσιν {{Refs|[prev. author] “N.” 10.83.}}
**II.2** with _infinitive_, **allow** one **to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.210, 6.103, +others}}: with _accusative_ _of things_, **permit**, ὁ θεὸς τοῦτό γε οὐ παρεδίδου {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.67}}; πληγὴν.. παραδοθεῖσαν εἰσιδών a blow **offered**, i. e. opportunity of striking, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1393: _absolutely_, τοῦ θεοῦ παραδιδόντος}} if he **permits**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.18; ἢν οἱ θεοὶ παραδιδῶσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.34; ὅπως ἂν οἱ καιροὶ παραδιδῶσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.118; τῆς ὥρας παραδιδούσης 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.41.9}}: less frequently in _aorist_, πότμου παραδόντος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.3; ὡς ἂν ὁ δαίμων παραδῷ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.19.}}
**III** **hazard**, τὰς ψυχὰς ὑπέρ τινος {{NT|NT.Act.15.26.}}

3861:
** παράδοξος**, ον,
**contrary to expectation, incredible**, π. λόγος **a paradox**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 472a; π. τε καὶ ψεῦδος [prev. author] “Plt.” 281a; παράδοξα λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.16; ἂν παράδοξον εἴπω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.10}}; ἐκ τοῦ παραδόξου καὶ παραλόγου **contrary to all expectation**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 25.32, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.23 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; πολλὰ ποικίλλει χρόνος π. καὶ θαυμαστά 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 593; π. μοι τὸ πρᾶγμα 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 1.6; τὸ ἔνδοξον ἐκ τοῦ π. θηρώμενος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 14}}; παράδοξα Stoical **paradoxes**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.1060b}} {παραδοξότης}: _comparative_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.72 K., 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.9.2}}: _superlative_, {{LXX|LXX.Wis.16.17}}. _adverb_ **-ξως**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.40, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.21.11, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.83}}: _superlative_ **-ότατα**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 67.11}}; **-οτάτως** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.876.}}
**II** παράδοξος, title of distinguished athletes, musicians, and artists of all kinds, **the Admirable,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 3.1442, 14.916, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.18.22, “IGRom.” 4.468 (Pergam., 3rd c.AD), “PHamb.” 21.3 (4th c.AD), “Rev.Ét.Gr.” 42.434 (Delph.), etc.}}

3862:
** παρά-δοσις**, εως, ἡ,
**handing down, bequeathing, transmission**, τοῦ σκήπτρου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9}}; **handing over, transfer**, ἡ π. τῶν χρημάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309a10, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 915d}}; σίτου, etc., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1257.3 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}; τῆς βασιλείας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Comp. Lyc.Num.” 1}}; ἐν παραδόσει παραλαμβάνειν ἀεί, of a reserve fund, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 11 (2).161 A 126 (Delos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**2** **transmission** of legends, doctrines, etc., **tradition**, διδασκαλία καὶ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 803a; πραγματεῖαι αἱ ἐκ π. ηὐξημέναι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 184b5; ἐν παραδόσει ἔχειν τι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.6.1, etc.}}; **treatment, exposition**, ὅπως πᾶσιν εὐπαρακολούθητος γένηται ἡ π. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 73.12}}; ἡ βοτανικὴ π. **the subject** of botany, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Praef.” 1; παραδόσεις καὶ παραγγελίαι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.78 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; σύντομος π. succinct **account**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Ammonius Philosophus “in Porphyrii Isagogen sive V voces - pars” 38.10.}}
**2.b** in military sense, **transmission of orders**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 21.2.}}
**3** **that which is handed down** or **bequeathed, tradition, doctrine, teaching**, ἡ π. τῶν πρεσβυτέρων {{NT+5th c.AD+|NT.Matt.15.2, NT.Mark.7.3, etc.; αἱ π. τῶν θεῶν καὶ τῶν θείων ἀνδρῶν 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 265}}: also in Grammars, Ἑλληνικὴ π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 213.13, compare 19 (_plural_)}}; in textual criticism, defined as ἡ τῶν γραμματικῶν μαρτυρία, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Etymologicum Magnum” 815.18; so παρὰ τὴν π. γράφειν 2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1012.34, compare “Etymologicum Magnum” 240.4, +others}}
**II** **surrender**, πόλεως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.53}}; ἐκ παραδόσεως, opposed to κατὰ κράτος, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.25.5}}; **giving up to punishment** or **torture**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.16; π. ἐπὶ θανάτῳ 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.36.}}
**II.2** Astrology texts, **handing over**, τῶν χρόνων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 141.4.}}

3863:
** παραζηλ-όω**,
**provoke to jealousy**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.1Ki.14.22, NT.Rom.10.19, etc.}}
**2** **fret, be vexed**, ἔν τινι {{LXX|LXX.Psa.36(37).1.}}
**II** **emulate, imitate**, [{{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {Ἀστυάνασσα}.}}]

3865:
** παραθεωρ-έω**,
**examine** a thing **beside** another, **compare**, πρὸς τοὺς ἄλλους ἐμαυτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.8.7; τινά τινι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herod.” 8, compare “Pr.Im.” 7, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.45, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.33a.}}
**2** **keep in mind, take into account**, ποσαχῶς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.29U.; τοὺς πολιτικοὺς καιρούς 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.373 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**II** **take slight notice of, overlook**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isaeo” 18, etc.}}:—_passive_, {{NT+1st c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 1161.38 (1st c.BC), NT.Act.6.1.}}

3867:
** παραιν-έω**,
_3rd.pers._ _singular_ _imperfect_ παρῄνει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.139}}; _Ionic dialect_ παραίνεε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.19}}: _future_ **-έσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1181, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1245, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.11, etc.}} **-έσομαι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 236e}}: _aorist_ παρῄνεσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1434, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1420, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.264}}; _Ionic dialect_ **-αίνεσα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80}}: _perfect_ παρῄνεκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 2.1, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Im.” 16}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ παρῃνέθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 8}}: _perfect_ _infinitive_ παρῃνῆσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.69}}:— **exhort, recommend, advise**, παραίνεσε μὴ φειδομένους κτείνειν πάντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80; ὧδε παραινέων, πέμψαντα δέεσθαι [prev. author] 3.4}}; π. τινί with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1132, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 234b; τοῖς ναύταις παραινῶ μὴ ἐκπεπλῆχθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.63; π. τινί τι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.23, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 309, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 464, etc.}}; τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59, 5.31, etc.}}; π. τινί **advise** a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 903; τοῖς πέλας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.9; ἄλλῳ πονοῦντι ῥᾴδιον παραινέσαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 75.1}}:—_passive_, ὥσπερ πρότερον παρῃνέθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus [same place], compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.69.}}
**2** **advise** or **recommend publicly, propose**, παρῄνει τοιάδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.139, compare “IG” 12.90.43, etc.; π. περὶ τῶν παρόντων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13}}; οὐ π. **advise** not.., with _infinitive_ (compare οὔ φημι, etc.), {{Refs|[prev. work] 18.}}

3868:
** παραιτ-έομαι**,
**beg of** or **from** another, **ask as a favour of** him, τινά τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 27b, etc.}}; τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24, 90}}: with _infinitive_ added, ἓν δ᾽ αὐτοὺς παραιτησώμεθα, ἐπίδηλον ἡμῖν.. ποιεῖν, ἢν τοῖς ἔπεσι χαίρωσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 37}}: with _infinitive_ for _accusative_, θεοὺς παραιτοῦ τῶν σ᾽ ἔρως ἔχει τυχεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 521}}; Προμηθέα -εῖται Ἐπιμηθεὺς αὐτὸς νεῖμαι **asks** P. **for permission to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 320d, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.146: with _accusative_ _cognate_, παραίτησιν π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 107a.}}
**2** παραιτησάμενος βασιλέα **having obtained** the king's **leave**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.24}}: generally, **intercede with, appeal to** a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.132, 5.33; κτεῖν᾽, οὐ παραιτοῦμαί σε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 1026, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1257}}; π. σφέας, ὡς ἄξουσι.. **entreating** them **and saying** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.158}}: with _double_ _accusative_, **beg** one's **pardon for**.., σὲ παραιτοῦμαι τάδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 685: _absolutely_, εἴ τις ὑμῶν ἀχθεσθήσεται, παραιτοῦμαι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.21, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 39.1.6.}}
**3** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **entreat** one **to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90, 6.86. γ, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.14, etc.}}; παραιτήσομαι δ᾽ ὑμᾶς μηδὲν ἀχθεσθῆναί μοι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.58; π. σε συγγνώμην ἔχειν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 867}}: with _genitive_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **beg of**.., παραιτήσῃ πατρὸς φυγὰς ἀφεῖναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1154}}: with _infinitive_ only, π. μηδὲν τούτων δρᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.63.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _of things_, **avert by entreaty, deprecate**, τὴν ὀργήν {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.198; τὰς ζημίας ὑπέρ τινος [prev. author] 2.19, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.5; αἰκίαν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.80.8; τὸν φθόνον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 56; τὸ ἀποθανεῖν NT.Act.25.11: _absolutely_, τοῖς.. παραιτουμένοις [πρᾷοί εἰσιν] 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1380a28, compare “PCair.Zen.” 482.14 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.2** **decline, deprecate**, χάριν {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.30; τὴν διαίρεσιν τῶν ὀνομάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 358a; τοὺς πότους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 3; τοὺς.. γραώδεις μύθους NT.1Tim.4.7}}; π. [τὰ ὦτα] **refuse** to hear, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 11}}; **refuse**, βρώσεις {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. author] “VA” 1.8, compare 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 4.7: with}}_infinitive_, παλιλλογεῖν παρῄτηται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.365; παραιτοῦ φαγεῖν κάπρου κεφαλήν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus in “Rev.Phil.” 32.252}}; also τοὺς πολλοὺς χαρακτῆρας παρῃτημέναι εἰσὶν αἱ ἀντωνυμίαι **do not admit**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 104.16}}; **reject** a theory, interpretation, or M{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus reading, Theo Sm.p.200 H., 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 2.7, Scholia 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.127, Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 854}}; **except**, {{Refs|Hdn.Gr. 2.929}}; **reject the use of, avoid**, τὴν λογικήν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.15, compare 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 107, etc.}}
**II.2.b** **demand exemption from**, τὴν εἰς τὸ μέλλον γεωργίαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.1231.3 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἀρχήν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1252v28 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ _person_, **ask** him **to excuse** one, **decline** his **invitation**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.27.3}}; παραιτησάμενος Ἔφορον, Latin _pace Ephori_, {{NT|[prev. author] 5.33.2: _absolutely_, NT.Luke.14.18:—}}_passive_, ἔχε με παρῃτημένον{{Refs|[prev. passage]}}
**II.4** π. γυναῖκα **divorce** her, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.206a}}; π. οἰκέτην **dismiss** him, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.82; π. τινὰ τῆς οἰκίας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Abd.” 19}}:—_passive_, τὴν παραίτησιν ᾗ παρὰ τοῦ Καρακάλλου παρῄτητο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 78.22.}}
**II.5** of medicines, **relieve**, ναυσίαν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.70; ὀδόντων ἀλγήματα [prev. work]48.}}
**III** with _accusative_ _person_, **intercede for, beg off**, especially from punishment, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.119, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.51.1; π. τινὰ τιμωρίας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 31}}; Θεσσαλοὺς τοῦ Μηδισμοῦ π. **excuse** them **from the charge** of Medism, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.868d; π. περί τινων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.29.}}

3869:
** παρακαθ-ίζω**,
_perfect_ παρακεκαθικέναι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.6.23}}:—**set beside** or **near**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 553d; τινὰ ἐπὶ τοῦ βάθρου 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 73.3; στρατιὰν π. περὶ τὴν πόλιν 4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 40: _intransitive_, = signification 11, LXX.Job.2.13, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 23.9, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus [same place], 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 17.}}
**2** _middle_ with _aorist 1_ παρεκαθισάμην, **let** another **sit down beside** one, π. παῖδας καὶ γυναῖκας ἑαυτοῖς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 141, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 19.4.5}}; also π. τινά **make** him **assessor** or **co-arbiter**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.14.}}
**II** mostly _passive_ and _middle_, _future_ **-καθιζήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 207b}}: _aorist 2_ παρεκαθεζόμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Euthd.” 273b, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 727}}; _participle_ παρακαθεζόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.7, “Mem.” 4.2.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 153c, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 3.2, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 26}}; later **-καθεσθείς** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Luke.10.39, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 6.11.9, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.637}}:—**seat oneself, sit down beside** or **near** another, [same places], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 144d.}}

3870:
** παρακᾰλέω**,
_Attic dialect_ _future_ -καλῶ, later **-καλέσω**{{LXX|LXX.Job.7.13, +others}}:—**call to** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.32.}}
**II** **call in, send for, summon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.77, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 215, etc.; σύμμαχον π. τινά 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.158, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.119, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89c, etc.; π. ἑταίρους 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.14; π. τινὰ ἐς τὸν πόλεμον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.205, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.24; π. τινὰ σύμβουλον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.5; τινὰς εἰς συμβουλήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 186a; συνήγορον 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.184}}; **invoke** the gods, τοὺς θεούς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.8}}; περὶ τούτου τὸν θεόν (as medical adviser) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 42(1).126.31 (Epid., 2nd c.AD)}}; τὸν Ἐνυάλιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.17; Διόνυσον εἰς τὴν τελετήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 666b; [τοὺς θεοὺς] π. βοηθούς 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.21.12:}} —_passive_, παρακαλούμενος καὶ ἄκλητος, '**vocatus atque non vocatus**', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.118; -κληθέντες ἐς ξυμμαχίαν [prev. author] 5.31}}; παρακαλουμένη ἀμύνειν **being called upon** to ward off, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 692e; -κληθεὶς γυμνασιαρχῆσαι “OGI” 339.53 (Sestos, 2nd c.BC).}}
**II.2** **summon** one's friends **to attend one** in a trial, π. τοὺς φίλους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.7, etc.}}; π. τινάς **call** them **as witnesses**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.28; π. πάντας ἀνθρώπους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.29}}:—_middle_, uncertain in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 28:}} —_passive_, παρακεκλημένοι **summoned** to attend at a trial, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.173.}}
**II.2.b** **summon** a defendant into court, in _passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 297.5 (2nd c.AD), Mitteis “Chr.” 71.5 (5th c.AD).}}
**II.3** **invite**, ἐπὶ δαῖτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1247}}; εἰς (variant{ἐπὶ}) θήραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.3}}; ἐπὶ τὸ βῆμα π. **invite** him to mount the tribune, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.72.}}
**II.4** **appeal to**, τινὰ περὶ γῆς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Αιβυκή” 136.}}
**III** **exhort, encourage**, τάξις τάξιν παρεκάλει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 380, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.60.5; π. τινὰ εἰς μάχην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1254; τινὰ ἐπὶ τὰ κάλλιστα ἔργα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.24; π. τὴν νόησιν εἰς ἐπίσκεψιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 523b; πρὸς τὸ μνημονεύειν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.12}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 156, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.19, Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.185}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.14; παρακέκληται ἡ διάνοια 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1175a7.}}
**III.2** **comfort, console**, τοὺς πενθοῦντας {{LXX|LXX.Sir.48.24}}:—_passive_, {{NT|NT.Matt.2.18, 5.4.}}
**III.3** **excite**, τινὰ ἐς φόβον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1583; ἐς δάκρυα [prev. author] “IA” 497}}; **incite**, π. καὶ παροξύνειν ἐπί.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 54}} G.; of things, **foment**, φλόγα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.23.}}
**IV** **demand, require**, ὁ θάλαμος σκεύη π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Oec.” 9.3:}} —_passive_, τὰ παρακαλούμενα **proposals, demands**, Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.166s q., 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.29.3.}}
**V** **beseech, entreat**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.82.8, “PTeb.” 24.46 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; π. τινὰ ἵνα..{{NT+2nd c.AD+|Aristeas 318, NT.Mark.8.22, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.7.11, etc.}}; ὅπως.. {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Matt.8.34: but ἐρωτῶ καὶ π. for δέομαι is condemned by 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ μεθόδου δεινότητος” 3.}}
**VI** _passive_, **relent**, πρός, ἐπί τινι, **towards** a person, {{LXX|LXX.Judg.21.6, 15, cf. LXX.2Ki.24.16.}}
**VI.2** **repent, regret**, παρακέκλημαι ὅτι..{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.15.11.}}

3872:
** παρακατα-θήκη**, ἡ,
**deposit** of money or property **entrusted to one's care**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92. ; αἱ τῶν χρημάτων π. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.22}}; π. Ἀθηναίας, i.e. deposited in her temple, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1407.42}}; π. ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]12.116.16, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.72, compare 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 55.1; π. χρυσίου ἢ ἀργυρίου δεξάμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 442e; π. καταθέσθαι παρά τινι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.16}}, compare 5; ἀποδιδόναι to restore it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1135b7}}; ἀποστερῆσαι to withhold it, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1383b21}}; ἐν π. δοθῆναι, ἔχειν, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.74.5, Mitteis “Chr.” 372 vi 19 (2nd c.AD)}}; αἱ π. τῆς τραπέζης banking **deposits**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.6: _metaphorically_, ταῦτ᾽ (i.e. τοὺς νόμους) ἔχεθ᾽.. παρὰ τῶν ἄλλων ὡσπερεὶ π. [prev. author] 21.177; οἱ τὴν τῶν νόμων π. ἔχοντες 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.187.}}
**2** of persons entrusted to guardians, **ward**, Ἀπόλλωνα παρὰ Ἴσιος π. δεξαμένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.156}}; of children, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.15}}; of persons under the protection of the state, **sacred trust**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.9. (Cf. παρκαθήκα.)}}

3873:
** παράκειμαι**,
_poetry_ **πάρκειμαι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (see. below)}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ παρεκέσκετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.521:}} —used as _passive_ to παρατίθημι, **lie beside** or **before**, ἔτι καὶ παρέκειτο τράπεζα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.476; ὀϊστόν, ὅ οἱ παρέκειτο τραπέζῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.416, compare 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 108.17, 5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 1.7, etc.; ἡ παρακειμένη τροφή 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 599a25}}: generally, **to be at hand, available**, οἷα τέκτοσιν ἡμῖν ὕλη παράκειται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 69a}}; **to be adjacent**, with _dative_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 74.56 (2nd c.BC): _metaphorically_, ὑμῖν παράκειται ἐναντίον ἠὲ μάχεσθαι ἢ φεύγειν}} **the choice is before** you, to fight or flee, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.65; ἔρδειν.. ἀμηχανίη παράκειται 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 685}}; ἅμα παρακεῖσθαι λύπας τε καὶ ἡδονάς **lie side by side**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 41d: frequently in}}_participle_, Ἀΐδᾳ παρακείμενος **lying at** death's **door**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 861 (Lyric poetry)}}; παρκείμενον τέρας the **present** marvel, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.73}}; τὸ παρκείμενον the **present**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “N.” 3.75; ἱκανὰ τὰ κακὰ καὶ τὰ παρακείμενα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1048; τὰ π. ὕδατα “PTeb.” 61 (b). 132 (2nd c.BC)}}; τὰ π., also, **dishes on table**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 30.6; κλίνην.. παρακειμένην τε τὴν τράπεζαν 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Diodorus Epigrammaticus Comedy texts 2.10}}; ἡ π. πύλη the **nearest** gate, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.16.5}}; ἐν μνήμῃ παρακείμενα **things present** in memory, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 19d}}; **under discussion**, λόγος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 16}}; **obvious**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1.3,6 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; **to be closely connected with**, παράκεινται τῇ μαθηματικῇ θεωρίᾳ ἥ τε θεολογικὴ ἐπιστήμη καὶ ἡ φυσική lamb.{{Refs|“Comm.in Mathematics texts” 28.}}
**b** in legal phrases, **to be attached** or **appended**, of documents, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 889.15 (2nd c.AD)}}; **to be noted, scheduled,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 27.7 (2nd c.BC)}}; **to be preserved** in a register or archive, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PSI” 5.454.18 (4th c.AD), etc.}}
**2** **press on, urge**, with _dative_, πυκνότερον ἡμῖν -κείμενοι {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.3Mac.7.3, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.34.7.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **lie prostrate**, of absolute subjection, π. πρὸ προσώπου σου {{LXX|LXX.Jdt.3.3.}}
**4** **to be permissible**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ὀδοντοφυΐης” 15.}}
**II** in Grammars, etc.:
**II.1** **to be laid down, mentioned** in text-books, τὰ σημεῖα οὐ παράκειται {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 29}}; simply, **to be cited**, ἐκ τῶν Θεοφράστου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 720.}}
**II.2** ὁ παρακείμενος (i.e. χρόνος) the **perfect** tense, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 205.15.}}
**II.3** ἀντίφρασίς ἐστι λέξις.. διὰ τοῦ π. τὸ ἐναντίον παριστῶσα, **ex adjecto**, as when the Furies are called {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Eumenides”, 1st c.BC: Trypho Grammaticus “περὶ τρόπων” 2.15, cf. Pseudo-Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Vitae Homeri” 25.}}
**II.4** of words, **to be joined by juxtaposition** (not **com**position,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare παράθεσις 1.2), 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 330.26, +others}}
**II.5** **to be interpolated**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).58.}}

3874:
** παρά-κλησις**, εως, ἡ,
**calling to one's aid, summons**, οἱ ἐκ παρακλήσεως συγκαθήμενοι a **packed** party in the assembly, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.143.}}
**2** **imploring, appealing**, τινος **of** or **on the part of** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.61}}; **deprecation**, συγγνώμης δεῖ καὶ π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.1.1.}}
**3** **invocation** of gods, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 4.3 (_plural_).}}
**4** **demand, request,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PGrenf. 1.32.7 (_plural_, 2nd c.BC), etc.}}; κατὰ-σιν on **demand,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PLond. 3.1164d10 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** **exhortation, address**, πρὸς τὸν ὄχλον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.92}}; οὐ π. εὑρόντες, ἀλλὰ παραίνεσιν γράψαντες not **a mere address** to their feelings, but counsel to act rightly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.5; π. τῶν πολιτῶν πρὸς ἀρετήν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.117; τὴν τῆς σωφροσύνης παράκλησιν.. αὐτοὺς παρακέκληκα [prev. author] 2.180}}; **ἀξιώσεισκαὶ-κλήσεις** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.67.10.}}
**III** **consolation**, {{LXX+NT+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Isa.30.7, LXX.Nah.3.7, NT.Heb.6.18, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 103.1.}}

3875:
** παρά-κλητος**, ον,
**called to one's aid**, in a court of justice: as _substantive_, **legal assistant, advocate**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.1, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator “fragment” 102, etc.}}
**2** **summoned**, δοῦλοι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 46.20, compare “BGU” 601.12 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **intercessor**, {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.520: hence in “NT”, Παράκλητος}}, of the Holy Spirit, {{NT|NT.John.14.16, cf. NT.1John.2.1.}}

3876:
** παρᾰκοή**, ἡ,
**that which has been heard imperfectly, hearsay**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 341b.}}
**2** **misunderstanding**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 4.764, 8.629.}}
**II** **defect of hearing**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.108.}}
**III** **unwillingness to hear, disobedience, contumacy,** {{NT|NT.Rom.5.19, NT.2Cor.10.6.}}

3877:
** παρᾰκολουθ-έω**,
**follow** or **attend closely, dog one's steps**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 725; τὸ παρακολουθοῦν εἴδωλον ἑκάστῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 266c, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.14,69, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 124; οὓς σὺ ζῶντας μέν, ὦ κίναδος, κολακεύων παρηκολούθεις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.162; πόροι κατὰ πάντα τὸν πλεύμονα παρακολουθοῦντες 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 496a29}}; **accrue**, especially of loss or damage, with _dative_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PSI” 3.168.24 (2nd c.BC), “PRein.” 18.15 (2nd c.BC).}}
**2** **make a succession** of growths, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.4.8.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **follow closely, attend minutely to**, π. τῷ νοσήματι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 406b}}; π. ἅπασι [τοῖς πονηρεύμασι] **trace accurately** all his knaveries, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.257; π. τοῖς πράγμασιν ἐξ ἀρχῆς [prev. author] 18.172}}; π. χρόνοις **follow all** the times and dates, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Nicomachus Comicus 1.20, cf. NT.Luke.1.3}}; τοῖς δικαίοις π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demades Orator 1}}; π. ταῖς τιμαῖς (prices) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 28.26 (3rd c.BC)}}; τῇ Ὁνοράτου κρίσει {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 653.29 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** of an audience, προσέχειν τὸν νοῦν καὶ π. εὐμαθῶς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.116}}: generally, **follow with the mind, understand**, π. τῷ πῶς.. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.12.7, etc.: as Stoic term, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.16.33, etc.}}; παρακολουθεῖν τούτῳ ὅτι.. **understand** that.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 2.26.3}}; simply, π. ὅτι.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.554}}; διότι.. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.132 (3rd c.BC)}}: with _participle_, π. ἐλεγχόμενος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.5.21: with _accusative_, τίς παρακολουθεῖ ταῦτ; 4th-3rd c.BC: Damoxenus Comicus 2.25}}; π. τὰ ἐψηφισμένα **become acquainted with**.., {{Refs|“GDI” 4940.8 (Cretan dialect), compare “BSA” 29.64 (Eretrian, found at Sparta), “IG” 11(4).1065a17 (from Delos), etc.: in later Philosophy, ἑαυτῷ π}}. **to be conscious**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.4.5}}; especially **to be** self-**conscious**, νοῦν νοοῦντα μόνον, μὴ παρακολουθοῦντα δὲ ἑαυτῷ ὅτι νοεῖ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|[prev. author] 2.9.1, compare 4.4.37, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 3.4, 14.}}
**II.3** of things, πυρετοὶ π. μοι καὶ ἀλγήματα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.11, compare 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 44.17.2}}; τῷ βίῳ π. **keep company with, keep close to**, of things that benefit, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.262; αὐτοῖς π. ἡ ἔχθρα [ἡ] παρὰ Λακεδαιμονίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.98}}; of rules, **hold good throughout**, δι᾽ ὅλης τῆς ἱππικῆς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 8.14.}}
**II.4** of a logical property, τὸ ἀεὶ παρακολουθοῦν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 131b9; also of the genus, [prev. work] 125b28, compare 123a19}}; of notions **inseparably connected** one with another, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cat.” 8a33, “Metaphorical” 1054a14}}; of cause and effect, {{Refs|[prev. author] “APo.” 99a17}}; τὸ παρακολουθοῦν τινι **constant attribute**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 8, 19, compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 4.3}}; **to be proper to**, ταῖς αἰσθηταῖς [ἁρμονίαις] {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.6.3.}}

3878:
** παρᾰκούω**,
**hear beside**, especially **hear accidentally, hear talk of**, Δημοκήδεος τὴν τέχνην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.129; ἀξίων λόγου πραγμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 339e; παρακήκοα νῦν ὅτι τίκτει “Anthologia Graeca” 5.74 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus).}}
**II** **eavesdrop, overhear from**, δεσποτῶν ἅττ᾽ ἂν λαλῶσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 750; τι παρά τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 300d}}; π. τινός **overhear** him, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 37; π. τὸν λόγον NT.Mark.5.36.}}
**III** **hear imperfectly** or **wrongly, misunderstand**, ἀκούειν τι τοῦ λόγου, παρακούειν δέ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1149a26, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 330e, “Tht.” 195a, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.102 K., 1st c.AD: Cebes Philosophus 3, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 31}}; ἑκουσίως π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 30.8.}}
**IV** **hear carelessly, take no heed of**, τῆς παραγγειλάσης φύσεως {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 200; τῶν γραφομένων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 24.9.1, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 6, etc.; τῶν ἐντολῶν LXX.Tob.3.4; τῶν λεγομένων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.12.9 (but τὰ λεγόμενα LXX.Est.3.3).}}
**IV.2** with _genitive_ _person_, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.170 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.8.3, 3.15.2, NT.Matt.18.17}}:—_passive_, **to be disregarded**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.35.5; περί τινος [prev. author] 30.20.2, probably conjecture in 23.3.3.}}
**IV.3** **disobey**, τοῦ θεοῦ {{LXX+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 1.10.4: _absolutely_, LXX.Isa.65.12, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 1.1.4, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sat.” 10}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 6.7.4.}}
**IV.4** **pretend not to hear**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 16, Luc “Jud.Voc.” 2.}}

3879:
** παρα-κύπτω**,
_poetry_ **παρκύπτω**, **stoop sideways**, of the attitude of a bad harp-player, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 16}}; **lean over** a railing, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 475.23 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **stoop for the purpose of looking**, and so,
**II.1** **look sideways** at, **cast a careless glance** on a thing, παρακύψαντ᾽ ἐπὶ τὸν τῆς πόλεως πόλεμον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.24.}}
**II.2** **peep out of** a door or window, ἐκ θυρίδος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 797, compare 799, “V.” 178; π. ὥσπερ γαλῆ [prev. author] “Ec.” 924}}; of girls **peeping after a lover**, {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pax” 982, 985, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.7; διὰ τῶν θυρίδων LXX.Cant.2.9}}; π. τὸν ἐραστὴν ἰδεῖν so as to see him, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.766d: _metaphorically_, σωτηρία παρέκυψε}} a hope of safety **peeped out**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 202}}; ὀδόντων παρακυψάντων, of the first teeth, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.118}}: followed by an _interrogative_ clause, **peep out and see**, π. τίς ἄνεμος πνεῖ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.1.16:}} — _passive_, θυρίδες παρακυπτόμεναι probably **out of which people look**, {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.6.9(4).}}
**II.3** of persons outside a place, **peep in, look in**, εἰς οἰκίαν{{LXX+NT|LXX.Sir.21.23; παρέκυψεν εἰς τὸ μνημεῖον NT.John.20.11}}; παρακύψας βλέπει{{NT+2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 5, NT.Luke.24.12; ὁ παρακύψας εἰς νόμον τέλειον NT.Jam.1.25; π. εἰς τὰ ὑμέτερα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 30, cf. NT.1Pet.1.12}}; of a thing, **appear in**, ἐς ἀρχόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ συρίγγων” 3.}}
**II.4** **meddle with**, πράγματι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PLips.” 29.10 (3rd c.AD).}}

3880:
** παραλαμβάνω**
(Cretan dialect **παλλαμβάνω** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Riv.Fil.” 58.472 (Gortyn, 3rd c.BC))}}_future_ -λήψομαι, _Ionic dialect_ **-λάμψομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.120}}:—**receive from** another, especially of persons succeeding to an office, etc., [τὴν βασιληΐην] Hdt.[same place],{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9; τὴν βασιλείαν παρὰ τοῦ πατρός “OGI” 90.1 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC)}}; τοῖς παραλαμβάνουσι (i.e. τὴν βασιλείαν) the **successors**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1285b8; π. τὴν ἀρχήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 698e; τῆς πόλεως τὰ πράγματα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 107; τὴν ἐπιμέλειάν τινος 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.143; τὴν τριηραρχίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.32; π. πόλιν ἀνάστατον 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.108, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9, etc.}}; νόμον ὄντα παραλαβόντες, opposed to θέντες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.105, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.102}}; of inherited rites or customs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51}}; of persons succeeding **by inheritance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 814, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.5, etc.; οἱ μὴ κτησάμενοι ἀλλὰ -λαβόντες τὴν οὐσίαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1120b12}}; παρὰ τοῦ πατρὸς πολλὴν οὐσίαν π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.157}}; opposed to ἐπικτᾶσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330a}}; π. ἀράς **inherit** curses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1611}}; of officers, **receive** things as stated in an inventory from their predecessors, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.301.5, +others; τὰ μὲν παρειληφότα τὰ δ᾽ αὐτὸν εὑρηκότα 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.208}}: Astrology texts, **take over**, [χρονοκρατορίαν] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 171.16}}: generally, **receive**, ἔρια παραλαβοῦσα ἱμάτιον ἀποδεῖξαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.6}}; of cargo, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 276.13 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**2** **take upon oneself, undertake**, πρᾶγμά τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 345}}; τὰ παραλαμβανόμενα **undertakings**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.38}}; **take** to oneself, **admit, employ**, π. ἐν ταῖς μάχαις τὸν θυμόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.988e:}} —_passive_, π. πρὸς τὴν σύστασιν τῆς ψυχῆς{{Refs|[prev. work]1027d.}}
**3** **take in pledge**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.136}}; **take by force** or **treachery, seize**, οὐδὲν ἐδυνέατο π. τῆς ἐσόδου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.211; ναῦς παραλαβόντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.19, 4.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.39}}; π. τὰ πράγματα **get control of** affairs, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 26}}:—in _middle_, **lay hold of**, ἄκρων τῶν χειρῶν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 6.4.1 (assuming variant).}}
**4** **receive by hearing** or **report, ascertain**, παρὰ τῶν Αἰγυπτίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.19; π. ἀληθείην [prev. author] 1.55; π. ἀκοῇ [prev. author] 2.148; π. τὰ περὶ Ἀλκμέωνα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.102; τι περί τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.22.5}}; **receive by way of lesson**, σοφίαν παρά τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 197d}}:—_passive_, **to be received, accepted**, τὰ παρειλημμένα **the received** or **traditionary doctrines**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 365a16; οἱ π. μῦθοι [prev. author] “Po.” 1453b22; [λόγοι] ἔνιοι π. ὡς Ἀριστογείτονος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.850e.}}
**5** **take, receive**, or **use** as a substitute or equivalent, τὸν ἀριθμὸν ἀντὶ τοῦ νοῦ π. {{Refs|“Placita Philosophorum” 4.2.3}}:—_passive_, ὑμέτερον ἀντὶ τοῦ ὑμεῖς παρείληπται {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Amm.” 2.14.}}
**5.b** Grammars and in Medicine texts, simply, **use, employ**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 25; εἰς λόγον 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 250.3}}; θλῖψιν, βοηθήματα, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 8.6.37, 8.10.1 (_passive_)}}:—frequently in _passive_, **to be found, used**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 14, 17, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 83.2, +others}}; π. ἐκ κοινοῦ, δεικτικῶς,{{Refs|[prev. work] 123.1, “Pron.” 10.17.}}
**5.c** **admit**, ἡ ὅλη ὑπόθεσις τὸ ἓν εἶναι παρελάμβανεν {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 417.}}
**6** **take up, catch up**, τὸ οὔνομα τοῦτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.122, compare 126; τὸν λόγον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 33.18.9}}; π. ἐπὶ βραχύ **give a résumé of**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.58.1.}}
**7** **compare**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Categorias commentarium - pars” 97.8 (_passive_).}}
**8** _passive_, **to be derived**, ἔκ τινος variant in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 32.16.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _person_, **take to oneself, associate with oneself**, as a wife or mistress, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.155}}; as an adopted son, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.113}}; as a partner, auxiliary, or ally,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 76, 7.150, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.111, etc.; παραλαμβάνων ἄλλος ἄλλον ἐπ᾽ ἄλλου.. χρείᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369b}}; συμβούλους π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1112b10}}; **get control of**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18b, “R.” 460b, 541a, Alcaeus1.121e}}; μάρτυρας π. **call in** witnesses, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.67}}: c._infinitive_, τὴν αἴσθησιν ὑπουργεῖν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 8.248a.}}
**II.2** **invite**, ἐπὶ ξείνια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.154; παραληφθεὶς ἐπὶ δεῖπνον 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.46; ἐφ᾽ ἑστίασιν παρειλημμένος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.40b; παραληφθεὶς εἰς τὸ συσσίτιον [prev. author] “Lyc.” 20: _absolutely_, [prev. author] 2.461d}}; παραληφθῆναι πρός τινα Parmenisc. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.156e.}}
**II.3** **receive, take over** in succession, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.203; Λυκοῦργος π. τοὺς Σπαρτιάτας οἴκοι σκηνοῦντας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 5.2.}}
**II.4** **take prisoner**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.69.2 (_passive_).}}
**II.5** of the dead, παραλημφθεὶς ὑπὸ θεῶν καταχθονίων {{Refs|“IG” 14.1702.}}

3881:
** παραλέγω**,
**pluck out superfluous hair**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}:—_passive_, παραλέλεξαι **you have had your eyebrows plucked.** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 904 (Lyric poetry); παραλελέχθαι τὰς τρίχας 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.35.}}
**II** _middle_, παραλέγεσθαι τὴν γῆν **sail** or **coast along**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hanno Geographus “Periplus - GGM” 11, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.55, “Periplus Maris Rubri [1st c.AD]” 60; τὴν Ἰταλίαν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.3; τὴν Κρήτην NT.Act.27.8, 13: _absolutely_, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.1.22.}}
**III** **speak beside** the purpose, **wander in one's talk, rave**, πολλὰ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.18, 26.δ᾽:}} —_middle_, παραλεξάμενος **speaking beside the point**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.101 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**III.2** **speak incidentally**, μῦθον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.653e}}:—_passive_, **to be cited**, ἐπὶ παραδείγματος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 4.7.}}
**III.3** **add** to what one has said, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 665 ii 15 (1st c.AD).}}

3882:
** παράλιος**, α, ον
or ος, ον (see. below), ={πάραλος}, **by the sea**, παραλία ψάμμα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 573 (Lyric poetry)}}; γῆ, πόλις παραλία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1592, “Rh.” 700 (Lyric poetry); ὄρνιθες παράλιοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1065; τὰπ. τῆς Αακωνικῆς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.213a; νησιῶται καὶ π. [prev. work]965c}} of maritime plants, especially **sea-spurge, Euphorbia Paralias**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.11.7, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.164.6}}; of πιτύουσα, {{Refs|[prev. work]165.}}
**II** ἡ παραλία, _Ionic dialect_ -ιη (i.e. γῆ or χώρα),
**A** **seacoast, seaboard**, τῆς Θρηΐκης τὴν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.185}}; of Epidaurus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1411a11.}}
**2** especially of **the maritime district** or **seaboard of Attica**, between Hymettus, Brauron, and Sunium, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.81}}; παραλία [γῆ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.56}}; ἡ χώρα ἡ π. {{Refs|“IG” 2.1195}}; ἡ π. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.39.3, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.1.21; also ἡ παράλιος 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.15, +others}}
**III** οἱ Παράλιοι, ={οἱ Πάραλοι}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.805e.}}
**IV** Παράλιον, τό, **sanctuary of the hero** Πάραλος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Rev.Et.Gr.” 44.294 (Attica, 4th c.BC), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.25, 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 294. [πᾱρᾰλίη, for the sake of meter, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1560, 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 253.]}}

3883:
** παραλλ-ᾰγή**, ἡ,
**passing from hand to hand, transmission**, πυρὸς παραλλαγαί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 490}}; **change of position, movement**, τὸ τάχος τῆς π., of the sun's apparent motion, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.3.10.}}
**b** Astrology texts, of a heavenly body, **passing beyond** the degree occupied by another, Antioch.Astr.in {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(3).113.}}
**2** **alternation**, μυῶν παραλλαγαὶ καὶ νεύρων their **alternate movements**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 30}}; παραλλαγὰς τοῖς ποσὶν ἐποίουν, of dancers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 36.}}
**3** **distortion** of the vertebrae, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 48}}; **contortion, twisting**, of wood, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.1.12 (_plural_).}}
**4** **interchange**, διανοίας πρὸς αἴσθησιν π. **interchange** of intellect and sense, putting one for the other, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 196c}}; π. προσώπων, πτώσεως, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 110.3, “Synt.” 214.9.}}
**II** **difference between** things, ποιεῖν τινα π. εὐοσμίας καὶ ἀοσμίας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.6.5; μεγάλας τὰς π. ποιεῖσθαι περί τι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.7.5}}; μεγάλην ἔχειν π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.37, compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.1.5; ἡ π. βραχεῖα 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 2.5; ἡ π. ἡ [τοῦ ἀνθρώπου] πρὸς τὰ ἄλογα 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.8.3; κάλλους πρὸς αἶσχος [prev. work] 2.23.32.}}
**III** **variety, variation**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.3.2; μεγεθῶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.15U. (_plural_), +others, compare 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.182, NT.Jam.1.17, 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.7; γραμμῶν καὶ γωνιῶν “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 63}}; **change** of meaning, παραλλαγῶν κατὰ σύμβολον γινομένων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.258, compare 3.33.}}
**IV** **frenzy, madness**, ἐν π. γενέσθαι {{LXX|LXX.4Ki.9.20}}; π. ψυχῆς **perturbation** of soul, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 25.111.}}

3884:
** παραλογ-ίζομαι**,
in keeping accounts, **cheat**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.29, 41.30, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 32}}: with _double_ _accusative_, **defraud of**, π. τρία ἡμιωβέλια τοὺς ναοποιούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1374b26, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.243}}; **reckon fraudulently**, τὸν μισθόν {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Gen.31.41; τὰ πορθμεῖα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 4.1}}: generally, **defraud**, τινα {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Gen.29.25, “PMagd.” 29.5 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, δαπάνας παραλογισθείσας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 665.15 (Egypt, 1st c.AD).}}
**II** **reason falsely, use fallacies**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 186a10, 239b5.}}
**II.b** **draw a false inference**, π. ἡ ψυχή {{Refs|[prev. author] “Po.” 1460a25, “Rh.” 1408a20; π. ὁ ἀκροατής [prev. work] 1401b8.}}
**II.2** **mislead by fallacious reasoning**, σφᾶς αὐτούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 6.12; σαυτόν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.221, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.134S.; ἀπάτῃ τινὶ π. τινάς 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.117}}; μεγάλα τὴν πόγιν π. {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.128}}:— _passive_, **to be misled by fallacious reasoning**, π. ἡ διάνοια ὑπό τινων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1307b35, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 2; αἰτίαις 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.49}} O.:—_passive_ and _middle_ opposed, παραλογισθῆναι καὶ παραλογίσασθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 108a27.}}
**III** **disguise**, τῆς ἐσθῆτος ὄψις παραλογιζομένη τὴν ἐπιδημίαν ἡμῶν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.597a.}}

3886:
** παραλύω**
[see. λύω]:
**I** with _accusative_ _of things_, **loose and take off, detach**, τὰ πηδάλια τῶν νεῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.136 (so in _middle_, παραλυόμενοι τὰ πηδάλια}} **taking off** the rudders, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.1.11:}} — _passive_, παραλελυμέναι τοὺς ταρσούς **with** their oars **taken off**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.4.2); τὴν πτέρυγα -λύσασα τοῦ χιτωνίου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 325; τὸν θώρακα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 76:}} —_middle_, π. τὴν ῥαφὴν [τοῦ χιτῶνος] {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cleom.” 37; τοὺς στεφάνους [prev. author] 2.646a}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.105.}}
**I.b** **hamstring**, ἅρματα {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.8.4.}}
**I.2** **undo, put an end to**, πόνους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 304 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὴν τοῦ παιδίου ἀμφισβήτησιν **relinquish** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.10}}:—_middle_, **get rid of**, τὸν κίνδυνον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.28.}}
**I.3** **undo secretly**, τὰ σακκία τῶν χρημάτων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.10b, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.106.}}
**I.4** **pay** a penalty, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.4.15}}; = Latin _persolvo_, νόμισμα {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PStrassb.” 50.8, 14 (6th c.AD).}}
**II** with _accusative_ _person_ et _genitive_ _of things_, **part from**, πολλοὺς ἤδη παρέλυσεν θάνατος δάμαρτος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 932 (Lyric poetry, uncertain reading); μία γάρ σφεων παρελύθη ὑπὸ Ἰώνων one city (from Smyrna)}} **was detached from** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.149}}; π. τινὰ τῆς στρατιῆς **release from** military service, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.38}} (and in _passive_, **to be exempt from** it, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|5.75),compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.33.10; τοῦ ὅρκου “OGI” 266.46 (Pergam., 3rd c.BC)}}; π. τινὰ δυσφρονᾶν **set free from** cares, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.52}}; π. τινὰ τῆς στρατηγίης **dismiss from** the command, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.94, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.16, 8.54; τῆς δυνάμεως τινά 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1315a12}} (so in _passive_, π. τῆς φυλακῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cleomenes” 37; τῆς ἀρχῆς 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Vitae Sophistarum” p.481 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus)}}; also τὴν ἀρχήν τινι π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] p.479 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}; τοὺς Ἀθηναίους π. τῆς ἐς αὐτὸν ὀργῆς **set** them **free, release** them **from**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65; φαρμάκῳ π. ἑαυτὸν τοῦ ζῆν 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.6.14; παραλελύσθαι τοῦ φόβου 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.4.7}}: with _accusative_ only, **set free**, δυστάνου ψυχάν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 117 (Lyric poetry)}}:—_middle_, **obtain leave of absence from**, τοὺς παιδονόμους {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 577.56 (Milet., 3rd-2nd c.BC).}}
**III** **loose besides, in addition**, π. καὶ ἑτέραν [κύνα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.14.}}
**IV** **disable, enfeeble**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 367b; π. τροφῆς ἀποχῇ τὸ σῶμα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 38}}:—mostly in _passive_, **to be paralysed**, δεξιὴ χεὶρ παρελύθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.26. ι; τὰ παραλελυμένα τοῦ σώματος μόρια 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1102b18}}: generally, **to be exhausted, flag**, ἡ δύναμις.. τῆς πόλεως παρελύθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.46; τῇ σωματικῇ δυνάμει παραλυόμενος ὑπὸ τῶν τραυμάτων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.5.7; παραλελυμένοι καὶ τοῖς σώμασι καὶ ταῖς ψυχαῖς [prev. author] 20.10.9; τὴν δύναμιν παρελέλυντο [prev. author] 1.58.9; τὰς χεῖρας Teles p.38 H.}}

3887:
** παραμένω**,
_poetry_ **παρμένω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 169 (trimeter)}}: — **stay beside** or **near, stand by**, οὐδέ τις αὐτῷ Ἀργείων παρέμεινε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.402, compare 15.400; παράμεινον τὸν βίον ἡμῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1108 (hexameters), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 39e, +others}}; of slaves, **remain, stay**, opposed to δραπετεύω, ἀποδιδράσκω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Men.” 97d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 3.4}}; in Law, of slaves whose manumission was deferred, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 1208 (Thespiae, 2nd c.BC), etc.: hence Παρμένων, “Trusty”}}, as a slave's name, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 302, etc.}}
**II** _absolutely_, **stand one's ground, stand fast**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.151, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82, 6.14, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 440, etc.}}; more fully, μάχαις τλάμονι ψυχᾷ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.48; παρμένοντας αἰχμᾷ [prev. work] 8.40}}; εὐανθεῖ ἐν ὀργᾷ π{{Refs 5th c.BC+|. [prev. work] 1.89; πρὸς τὰ ὑπόλοιπα τῶν ἔργων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.10}}; ἀδύνατός εἰμι.. παραμένειν **to remain with** the army, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.15}}; offortune, **remain steady.** παραμένει γὰρ οὐδὲ ἕν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 51.}}
**II.2** **stay** at a place, **stay behind** or **at home**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.64, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.2.}}
**II.3** **survive, remain alive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30.}}
**II.4** of things, **endure, last**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.57, etc.}}; ἀεὶ παραμένουσα [ἡ φύσις] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 942; π. ἡ πολιτεία 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.28; αἱ εὐπραγίαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.13; ἡ ὑγίεια 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.17, etc.; δίχα τῆς σφοδρότητος π. τὸ μέγεθος 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 9.13}}; of money, **stay by one, last for ever**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 281, 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 9.1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 128.2}}; of wine, **last, keep good**, εἰς τριγονίαν π. ἐν ἀπιττώτοις ἄγγεσι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 11.10.1, compare “Ostr.Bodl.” i 145 (3rd-2nd c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.655f.}}

3888:
** παραμῡθ-έομαι**,
**encourage, exhort** one **to do** a thing, with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, τοῖς ἄλλοισιν ἐγὼ παραμυθησαίμην οἴκαδ᾽ ἀποπλείειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.417, compare 684, 15.45: later with _dative_ _person_ only, “PFay.” 19.6 (2nd c.AD): also with _accusative_ _person_ et}}_infinitive_, πῶς οὖν αὐτοὺς παραμυθησόμεθα προθύμους εἶνα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 666a}}; παραμυθοῦ μ᾽ (i.e. ποιεῖν) ὅ τι καὶ πείσεις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1063 (_anapaest meter_)}}; θαρσεῖν (i.e. σε) οὐδὲν παραμυθοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 935 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**2** **speak soothingly to**, with _accusative_, παρεμυθεῖτο **attempted to reassure** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.75, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 476e, etc.}}; **encourage** a dog, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.25: _metaphorically_, παραμυθεῖται ὁ σκάφος τὴν διψῶσαν ἄμπελον “Geoponica” 3.5.4}}; **console, comfort**, τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.δ᾽, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.44, 4th c.BC: Alcidamas Rhetor “περὶ σοφιστῶν” 10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 346b, +others; ἡ φιλοσοφία τὴν ψυχὴν ἠρέμα παραμυθεῖται [prev. author] “Phd.” 83a; π. τινὰ λόγοισι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 115; τινὰ ὀψαρίοις [prev. author] “fragment” 45; τινὰ ἐπὶ τῇ κολάσει 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 33; ἑαυτὸν τῆς ἥττης 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 48.46}}; ταῖς ἐλπίσιν τἀλγεινὰ π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 641}}: with _neuter_ _adjective_, πόλλ᾽ ἂν εἶχέ τις αὑτὸν παραμυθήσασθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.214}}; π. τὰς πόλεις ὡς.. **console.. by saying** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.1.}}
**3** **relieve, assuage, abate**, π. ὁ οἶνος τὴν τοῦ γήρως δυσθυμίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 120}}; Ἐπίκουρος τἀποθνῄσκειν π. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1013.13; ᾄδουσιν ἡσυχῇ τὸ ἔργον -ούμενοι 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 1.9; π. τὰς ἐν στρατοπέδοις συμφοράς 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 1.13}}; π. τὸν φθόνον, τὸ πένθος, τὴν ξυμφοράν, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 13, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philops.” 27, “DMort.” 28.3}}; [θρίδακες] κόρυζαν π. {{Refs|“Geoponica” 12.13.11}}; πῦρ ἀνάψαντες τὸ πικρὸν τοῦ κρυμοῦ π. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.1.}}
**4** **soflen down, palliate**, τὸ τῆς μοναρχίας ὄνομα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cleomenes” 11; διὰ τῶν τῆς γοργότητος ἰδίων τὸ λίαν ὕπτιον 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 2.4}}; **explain away**, τὸ μυθῶδες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.248b}}; **excuse**, τὴν ἀπὸ μικρῶν ἐπίκλησιν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.1.64.}}
**5** **support, justify** a thesis, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.66, +others, compare παραμυθητέον 3}}; **explain**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 9.32.}}

3889:
** παραμῡθ-ία**, ἡ,
**encouragement, exhortation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 450d, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.65 W.(_plural_)}}; **reassurance, gentle persuasion**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 70b, “Lg.” 720a.}}
**2** **consolation**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Ax.” 365a, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.1.18}}; **diversion, distraction**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 224a.}}
**3** **relief from, abatement of**, φθόνου {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 22; τῶν πόνων καὶ τῶν κινδύνων [prev. author] “Dio” 52, etc.}}; π. ταλαιπωρούντων, of sleep, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 13.}}
**4** **explanation, solution** of a difficulty, π. πρὸς τὴν ἀπορίαν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.395f, compare 929f, 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 361.19.}}
**5** **excuse**, ἔχειν τινὰ π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 4.7, compare 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.11, +others}}

3890:
** παραμῡθ-ιον**, τό,
**address, exhortation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 773e, 880a (both _plural_)}}; **encouragement**, τοῦ μὴ φοβεῖσθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Euthd.” 272b.}}
**2** **assuagement, abatement of**, καμάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 130 (Lyric poetry)}}; πυρσῶν **of** the fires of love, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.7; ἐλπὶς κινδύνῳ π. οὖσα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.103; παραμύθια ποιήσασθαι τῆς ὁδοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 632e, compare 704d}}; τοῖς γὰρ πλουσίοις πολλὰ π. φασιν εἶναι many **consolations**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 329e, compare “Phdr.” 240d, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 19; λύπης παραμύθιον “epigram Gr.” 298.7 (from Teos), compare “IG” 3.768a.}}
**3** παραμύθια πλησμονῆς **stimulants** of a sated appetite, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 115b.}}

3891:
** παρανομ-έω**,
_imperfect_ παρενόμουν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.17, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 17.22, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.77}}: _future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 45}}: _aorist_ παρενόμησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.238, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.67, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 20, later παρην- 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 37}}: _perfect_ παρανενόμηκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.126, later παρηνόμηκα 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.61}}: _pluperfect_ παρενενομήκεσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.31}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ παρενομήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.16, etc.}}: _perfect_ παρανενόμημαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.31, 54.2}}; _participle_ παρηνομημένος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 167.38 (Mylasa, 4th c.BC)}}:—**transgress the law, actunlawfully**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.65, +others}}; κοινῇτι π{{Refs 5th c.BC+|. [prev. work] 82; παρανομοῦντά τε καὶ ἀδικοῦντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 338e, etc.: with _accusative_ _cognate_, παρανομίαν π. 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 1.15b.}}
**2** **commit a crime** or **outrage**, τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.15, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.21, Aeschin.[same place], 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1307b31; ἐς τὸν νεκρὸν ταῦτα παρενόμησε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.238, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.17; εἰς θεούς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.126; εἰς τὸ μαντεῖον 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.61; also π. τὸ θεῖον καὶ πάτριον ἀξίωμα τῆς εὐσεβείας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.166b; περὶ σφᾶς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.108}}; π. τὰ δημόσια **act illegally** in public matters, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.37:}} —_passive_, κάθοδος παρανομηθεῖσα a return **illegally procured**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.16.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be ill-used**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.45, 44.31, “PSI” 4.330.8 (3rd c.BC)}}; εἰς τὸ σῶμα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 13}}; ἡ φύσις παρανομεῖται, ἡ συνήθεια παρανενόμηται, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.755c, 1070c.}}

3895:
** παραπίπτω**,
**fall beside**, ἐγγὺς τῶν τειχῶν -πεπτωκός {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lysander” 29}}; **come side to side**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 718a1, +others}}
**2** in Mathematics texts, as _passive_ of παραβάλλω, **to be applied**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 2.}}
**II** **fall in one's way**, κατὰ τύχην παραπεσοῦσα νηῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.87, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 27.15; ἀκοντίσαι ὅπου ἂν παραπίπτῃ [θηρίον] 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.10; ἀγοράσαι.. χιτωνάριον, μάλιστα μὲν ἐὰν παραπίπτῃ χειριδωτόν “PCair.Zen.” 469.5 (3rd c.BC)}}; π. κατὰ βοήθειαν **come in time** to aid, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.5.2, etc.}}; ὁπότε καιρὸς παραπέσοι as opportunity **offered**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 7.4, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.23; οὐ δεῖ τοιοῦτον παραπεπτωκότα καιρὸν ἀφεῖναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.8; εἴ ποθεν ἄελπτος παραπέσοι σωτηρία 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1173; ὁ -πίπτων παρὰ τῶν πολλῶν ἔπαινος 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 29}}; ὁ παραπεσών **the first that comes**, ἡ παραπίπτουσα ἀεὶ [ἡδονή] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 561b}}; ὁ παραπεπτωκὼς λόγος **that happened to arise**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 832b, compare “Phlb.” 14c}}; πᾶν τὸ παραπῖπτον or παραπεσόν all **that befalls**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.51.5, 11.4.5}}; κατὰ τὸ -πῖπτον **incidentally**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 37.}}
**II.2** with _dative_, **befall**, θαυμαστὸν κτῆμα παραπεσεῖν τοῖς Ἕλλησι **fell to** their **lot**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 686d}}; π. τῇ πόλει νομοθέτης **comes to** its **aid**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 709c: in bad sense, ἀσθένειά τινι παραπεπτωκυῖα 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.49O.; παραπέπτωκε τῇ πόλει ὥστε ἀνακτᾶσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 5.8.}}
**III** **fall, rush in**, εἰς τὸ Σαμικόν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.80.9, etc.}}
**IV** **go astray, err**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.4}}; τοῖς ὅλοις πράγμασιν ἀγνοεῖν καὶ π. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.36.6; π. τῇ διανοίᾳ Vett. Val. 73.25.}}
**IV.b** **to be mislaid** or **lost**, of a document, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Ostr.Bodl.” i62 (2nd c.BC), “POxy.” 95.34 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.; σανδάλιον παραπεσόν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philops.” 27.}}
**IV.2** **fall aside** or **away from**, with _genitive_, τῆς ὁδοῦ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.54.5; τῆς ἀληθείας [prev. author] 12.12.2; τοῦ καθήκοντος [prev. author] 8.11.8; τῆς ἱστορίας 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.1.7}}: _absolutely_, **fall away,** {{NT|NT.Heb.6.6.}}
**V** **fall down before, cringe, flatter**, παραπεπτωκώς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.84.}}
**VI** Astrology texts, **to be unfavourably situated**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 5.5, 27.18.}}

3896:
** παραπλέω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **παρα-πλώω** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 733, 1271: _Epic dialect_}}_aorist 2_ παρέπλων (see. below):— **sail by** or **past**, _absolutely_, οἴη δὴ κείνῃ γε παρέπλω.. Ἀργώ was the only ship that **sailed through** that way, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.69, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.1.11}}; ἐν χρῷ παραπλέοντες **sailing close in**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.84, compare 90; π. παρὰ τὰς πρῴρας τῶν νεῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.100}}; π. τὴν Ἔφεσον **sail past** Ephesus, {{NT|NT.Act.20.16.}}
**2** **coast by** or **along**, ὃς τῆς Ἀττικῆς ταῦτα μὴ -πέπλωκε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.99, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.123; ἐς Σικυῶνα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.111; ἐνθένδε μὲν εἰς Σινώπην π., ἐκ Σινώπης δὲ εἰς Ἡράκλειαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.10, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.31; ἐκεῖθεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.61}}; π. ἀπὸ κάλω, see at {κάλως}.
**3** _metaphorically_, π. τὰς συμφοράς **sail past, escape** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 3.4.}}

3897:
** παραπλήσιος**, α, ον
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.202, 4.128, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.78, etc.}}; also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.84, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.37.8}}:— **coming alongside of**: hence, **coming near, nearly resembling**; of numbers, **nearly equal, about as many**; of size, **about as large**; of age, **about equal**; etc.:
**1** _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.128, etc.}}; τοιαῦτα καὶ π. such and **such-like**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.22}}; τὰς πράξεις ὁμοίας καὶ π. ἀποβαίνειν Isoc. [prev. cited]; ταὐτόν ἐστι σοφιστὴς καὶ ῥήτωρ, ἢ ἐγγύς τι καὶ παραπλήσιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 520a; ναυσὶ παραπλησίαις τὸν ἀριθμόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.70; ἱππεῖς π. τὸ πλῆθος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.3.15; ἀγωνίζεσθαι πρὸς π. ἱππέας [prev. author] “Eq.Mag.” 8.17.}}
**2** frequently with _dative_, ἐν τῇ ναυμαχίῃ παραπλήσιοι ἀλλήλοισι ἐγίνοντο were **about equal**, of a drawn battle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.16; νῆσοι Λέσβῳ μεγάθεα παραπλήσιαι [prev. author] 1.202; ἐσθὴς τῇ Κορινθίῃ παραπλησιωτάτη [prev. author] 5.87; π. τούτῳ καὶ ὅμοιον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.196}}; ὅμοι᾽ ἢ π. τούτοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]307: with _dative_ of the person for _dative_ of that which belongs to the person, ἔπαθε παραπλήσια τούτῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.78, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.14.2, etc.: rarely with _genitive_, [prev. author] 1.23.6}}; ἦχος συριγμοῦ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 21.1 (in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 217b the _genitive_ ὧν is due to the attraction).}}
**3** followed by a _relative_, τρόπῳ παραπλησίῳ, τῷ καὶ Μασσαγέται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.172; by καί, Λυδοὶ νόμοισι π. χρέωνται καὶ Ἕλληνες [prev. author] 1.94, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.112, 7.71}}; also π. πάσχουσιν ὥσπερ ἂν εἰ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.27: _neuter_ παραπλήσια as}} _adverb_, π. ὡς εἰ.., **perinde ac si**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.99: _sing._, παραπλήσιον καὶ οὐ πολλῷ πλέον}} **about the same distance** and not much more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.19}}; τὸ π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.43: more frequently regular}} _adverb_ -ίως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 37a, +others}}; ἆρά γ᾽ ὁμοίως ἢ π.; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.27}}; ἀγωνισάμενος π. having fought **with nearly equal advantage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.77; π. τοῖς εἰρημένοις πράττοντας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.51, etc.}}; π. καὶ.., Latin _perinde ac_.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.119}}; π. ἔχει καθάπερ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 321a}}: _comparative_ παραπλησιαίτερον {{Refs|[prev. author] “Plt.” 275c.}}

